globalThis.HELA_LIBRARY = [{"source":"CHANGELOG.md","title":"[1.11.26] - 2026-11-27","summary":"Version housekeeping, no cell version changes. Nucleus and Receptor lockfiles state their package versions; Cytosol, Mitosis, Ribosome and Genome changelogs drop [Unreleased]…","category":"releases","archived":false,"link":"library/CHANGELOG.html","text":"## [1.11.26] - 2026-11-27\n\n### Changed\n- Version housekeeping, no cell version changes. Nucleus and Receptor lockfiles state their package versions; Cytosol, Mitosis, Ribosome and Genome changelogs drop `[Unreleased]` blocks that had already shipped; Membrane, Nucleus and Receptor changelogs are ordered by version; the Membrane, Genome and Ribosome tool documents match the catalog; the runtime README names 1.4.5. Phenotype pins the zod advisory fix in its Remotion test fixture.\n\n## [1.11.25] - 2026-11-26\n\n### Security\n- `hela-runtime` 1.4.5: one permission gate covers retrieval, planning and dispatch in `hela.agent.run`; write-banned and lookup-only tasks get read-only tools, banned page actions are refused, and paths or filenames no longer pull destructive tools into the shortlist (hela-runtime#9). Ban detection understands English only; other limits are listed in the runtime changelog.\n\n### Fixed\n- Namespaced catalogs plan and execute, and the model's tool picks no longer depend on the naming mode (hela-runtime#8). A time question opens with the clock tool (hela-runtime#4). Catalog search matches cell names.\n\n## [1.11.24] - 2026-11-25\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-runtime` 1.4.4: `hela.agent.run` no longer ends \"single action complete\" after one non-failing call to a tool that does not address the task; unanswered runs end `degraded` (hela-runtime#4, RT-07). A write ban scoped to the task's own input keeps a permitted copy (RT-03), a timed-out read is retried once (RT-04), a plan cut short by `maxToolCalls` is `degraded` with `pendingSteps` (RT-05) and early exits return `status: \"failed\"` (RT-06). A ban naming a concrete file or path stays a full write ban.\n\n## [1.11.23] - 2026-11-25\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-runtime` 1.4.3 gives the Mitosis cell `HELA_RUNTIME_URL`: a loopback-only endpoint on an ephemeral port behind a random capability path, so cross-cell workflow steps no longer fail with `No runtime transport bound`. An operator-set value still wins (chaining-mcp#28, ISS-61).\n- `hela-mitosis` 3.1.3: the three LLM planners share the model-backed time budget instead of the 10 s cap, `get_skill` distinguishes unknown skill, file not in skill and path not allowed, `maxChars` counts the body, `search_prompts` reports an empty category match, and prompt `expectedTools` use real tool names (ISS-62, ISS-63).\n- `hela-ribosome` 3.2.4: a server from `opencode_server_spawn` keeps its generated password inside the cell and passes it to attached agents, a startup failure is reported, `opencode_import` is `ok:false` when nothing imported and takes `cwd`, and follow-up runs get unique names (menager-mcp#23, ISS-64…66).\n- `hela-genome` 3.3.0: tombstones carry `deleted_at` and report their age, `restore:true` re-creates a deleted name, a stale edited copy cannot resurrect a deleted entity, `delete_entities` reaches central-only entities, `read_graph` pages stop at about 100 KB with `truncated` and `nextCursor`, and the year-32971 repair is an explicit `repair_timestamps` action (project-mcp#41, ISS-67, 68, 70, 71).\n- `hela-enzyme` 4.3.4: `news_monitor` and the `google_search` news fallback mark publisher-home links, `fact_checker` judges each source against the claim (supports, refutes or unclear), and CSV export keeps plain numbers unquoted and includes columns that only later rows have (researcher-mcp#44, ISS-72…74; the alphabetical column order did not reproduce in the cell).\n\n## [1.11.22] - 2026-11-25\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.8 repairs the cookie handling reopened in browser-mcp#22. `browser_save_session` keeps real cookie values in its owner-only, page-scoped file, so `browser_load_session` no longer overwrites a live cookie with the text `[redacted]`; masked cookies from older saves are skipped and counted.\n- `browser_upload` refuses credential and system paths (`.ssh`, `.aws`, `.gnupg`, `.config`, every `.env*`, `/etc`, …) by real path even when `HELA_ALLOWED_ROOTS` is `/` or `$HOME`; `HELA_BROWSER_UPLOAD_ANY=true` still overrides.\n- `browser_export_state` and `browser_save_session` write 0600 atomically (temp file + rename), replacing an existing world-readable file instead of chmod'ing it by path.\n- Cytosol pin moved to the merge commit `93681e7`; `latest-stable` and the `v1.11.22` snapshot pair carry it.\n\n## [1.11.21] - 2026-11-25\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-runtime` 1.4.2 stops reporting a clean success when the tool it called failed. Failed payloads and thrown errors are recorded as failed, an identical failing call is skipped instead of retried verbatim, and the result carries `ok`, `status`, `failedCalls`, `degraded` and `decision.action: \"failed\"` (hela-runtime#4).\n- A task that forbids modification is enforced on the final tool selection: \"Read notes.txt. Do not edit any files.\" executes `read_file` instead of writing, and a run with no permitted tool stops with `no permitted tools` (hela-runtime#5, RT-01).\n- `classifyToolResult` honours `isError` on results that carry `structuredContent` but no `content` array and reads failure/degradation from `structuredContent` first, so `hela.pipe` is fail-closed again for that shape. The classifier is shared by the pipe and the native agent (hela-runtime#5, RT-02; hela-mcp-ecosystem#8).\n- Runtime pin and `latest-stable` snapshot now point at the 1.4.2 merge commit; the runtime suite is 74/74.\n\n## [1.11.20] - 2026-11-24\n\n### Fixed\n- `doctor.mjs --hosts` no longer counts the runtime's aggregate tool entry on top of the cells. A `HELA_NAMESPACE_TOOLS=0` host showed \"287 declared tools shadowed by bare-name collisions\" and recommended enabling namespacing, when the real gap was zero: 593 declared was 295 cell tools plus the runtime's 298 already-aggregated names. The check now compares live tools against the cells only and reports 9 ok, 0 warn on lappy.\n\n## [1.11.19] - 2026-11-24\n\n### Fixed\n- The apply result now uses the same profile-aware comparison as the check. 1.11.18 filtered snapshot additions in the check and the pre-apply decision but left the post-apply summary comparing the whole snapshot, so a `dev-workspace` update still printed `hela-receptor` and `hela-plastid` as added. All four `compareRelease` call sites now pass the profile's cell set.\n\n## [1.11.18] - 2026-11-24\n\n### Fixed\n- The update controller no longer reports a permanent update for profile installations. `compareRelease` compared every snapshot cell against the installed profile subset, so a `dev-workspace` install treated `hela-receptor` and `hela-plastid` as \"added\" on every check and kept `update-available` true even at the latest snapshot. It now filters snapshot additions to the profile's own cells; removals are still reported. (`scripts/update-controller.mjs`, one regression test)\n\n## [1.11.17] - 2026-11-24\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-phenotype` 3.3.0 returns every Ellis UI page (index, letter, note, slideshow) and accepts a `page` selector, so the links inside the returned templates resolve. The list tool is a compact JSON index instead of 24 full design documents, both tools emit real newlines instead of literal `\\n`, and the false \"Zero Dependencies\" claim now states the actual Tailwind CDN, Google Fonts and icon dependencies. (`designer-mcp#41`)\n\n## [1.11.16] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-enzyme` 4.3.3: summarizer drops page chrome, dedup returns survivors with URL canonicalisation, fact_checker reports stances and a verdict, research_assistant dedups across queries, wikipedia loses the empty parentheses, and multi_site_search reports per-site counts.\n\n## [1.11.15] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.7 marks mock-served requests so synthetic traffic is distinguishable from real traffic, with filters and an exposed mock count.\n\n## [1.11.14] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-ribosome` 3.2.3: the trigger-echo strip no longer lets a bare newline consume the pending echo, removing a race that flaked the hook regression under CI.\n\n## [1.11.13] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-ribosome` 3.2.2: `session_read` lines mode replays carriage-return redraws, removing interleaved prompt echoes.\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.6: `extract_schema` reads JSON-LD/microdata/tables, `emulate` sets language and Accept-Language, `get_state` exposes checked/name/textBlocks, captcha verify is honest, and intercept/switch_tab/cache are diagnosed.\n- `hela-mitosis` 3.1.2: prompt `expectedTools` map to real HeLa tools with a catalog test, the category enum is derived from data, the local instruction fallback is marked degraded, and `execute:false` records a proposed call.\n\n## [1.11.12] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-phenotype` 3.2.3 removes the local `docs/TOOLS.md` generator that 3.2.2 added; the file stays owned by `npm run docs:generate -- --servers`. The consistency test keeps its live `tools/list` count checks.\n\n## [1.11.11] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Fixed\n- Tool references in phenotype, cytosol, genome, enzyme, mitosis and ribosome are regenerated from the schema catalog. Several had drifted (missing tools, stale descriptions) because the file is not regenerated by the cell release, only by `npm run docs:generate -- --servers`.\n\n## [1.11.10] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-phenotype` 3.2.2 publishes only derived numbers: 37 slop gates, 68 brands in 9 categories, 14 motion categories, 19 themes, 18 systems, and the tool count from `tools/list`. Two rules that contradicted the code are removed, `get_reference` no longer leaks its absolute path, and a consistency test fails when a claim drifts.\n\n## [1.11.9] - 2026-11-23\n\n### Changed\n- `hela-genome` 3.2.0 requires `HELA_GENOME_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=true` for `insert_data`, `update_data` and `import_data`, matching `delete_data`; `read_graph` is paged with `nextCursor`. **Breaking for bulk writes.**\n\n### Fixed\n- Genome: AKIA keys detected with named finding types, one-commit repositories report modified and untracked files, the pre-commit hook cannot delete after a failed merge, `bug:` guidance matches `openBugs`, and empty conditions are rejected explicitly.\n\n## [1.11.8] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.5 returns vision boxes with explicit units: `bbox` normalised 0-1, `bboxPx` in source pixels, `image` dimensions and `coordinateSpace`. The preprocessed OCR image moved to `imageBase64` so `image` means dimensions.\n\n## [1.11.7] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.4 restores the restricted wording on `browser_search` that 3.3.3 dropped while documenting its tools scope; the canonical-boundary check asserts it.\n\n## [1.11.6] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.3: `browser_switch_to_new_tab` no longer throws, quiz batches report real counts, macro export honours format and save, generated tests run, and catalogue search finds tool names.\n- `hela-genome` 3.1.3: harness sync cannot store a fabricated year (zero and seconds timestamps are normalised or skipped with a reason, existing bogus rows repaired), `get_timeline` uses real timestamps with numeric `since` and sorts, and the dry run honours watermarks.\n\n## [1.11.5] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- The Cytosol tool count in the site and `llms.txt` is derived from the generated catalog instead of a hand-written number, and the docs test compares against the catalog rather than the literal `75`. Adding `browser_list_dialogs` in cytosol 3.3.2 made the stale literal fail the gh-pages verification job.\n\n## [1.11.4] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-genome` 3.1.2: mission transitions are guarded (queued → claimed → in_progress → completed/failed, cancel from any state, explicit requeue), lifecycle updates must name the claimant, and claims carry a 30-minute lease.\n- `hela-enzyme` 4.3.2: citations keep every author, invert names per style, use et al. for MLA, and warn instead of dropping an unparseable name.\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.2: dialogs are dismissed by default instead of auto-accepted, `browser_dialog` sets the next action and prompt text, `browser_list_dialogs` records them, and popup dismissal never clicks an affirmative control.\n\n## [1.11.3] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- Runtime 1.4.1 repinned at `14cc13e`: the version bump changed the npm deps derivation input, so the fixed-output `npmDepsHash` no longer matched and the Nix verify job failed. Hash refreshed (`sha256-VlP6gbWoi6TTkbk4FSSKey/bTw/rNT40H+99k7J4A7c=`); no functional change from 1.11.2.\n\n## [1.11.2] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Added\n- **Response contract for degraded results.** `hela.runtime` 1.4.1 reports `status: ok | degraded | failed` on `hela.pipe`, per-step status, and a `degraded` array naming the step and reasons. A step that marks itself `degraded`, `partial`, `truncated`, `status: \"degraded\"` or `warnings` makes the pipe degraded while `ok` stays true. Contract documented in `hela-runtime/docs/response-contract.md`.\n- `hela.mitosis` 3.1.1 adopts it: `get_skill` marks a truncated or file-omitted answer `status: \"degraded\"` with a reason.\n\n### Fixed\n- The nine instances tracked by #8 were fixed individually; this release makes the convention enforceable rather than per-tool.\n\n## [1.11.1] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-genome` 3.1.1: entity deletions are reconciled across project and central sync, sync reports what it deleted or skipped, and a root switch no longer imports another project into a fresh root.\n- `hela-enzyme` 4.3.1: sentiment applies negation across a window, so \"never recommend\" and \"hardly a success\" score negative; the negations found and the lexicon caveat are reported.\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.1: seventeen handlers expose their payload through `structuredContent` with a bounded text summary, and failures set `isError`.\n- `hela-phenotype` 3.2.1: React components use their declared props and derive classes from the requested style and palette; the snippet is typechecked and rendered in tests.\n\n## [1.11.0] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `hela-phenotype` 3.2.0: theme colours and every derived token are inside sRGB (0 of 342 emitted values out of gamut); `stitch_sync_design_system` writes one accent; browser templates apply their palette; the 8-state component renders 8 distinct states; logo search matches catalogue facets; the export covers `index.html`.\n- `hela-enzyme` 4.3.0: trends report a window and never page counts as interest; CSV escapes quotes and guards formulas; research sessions persist to disk; `news_monitor` enforces `dateRestrict` and resolves redirects.\n- `hela-mitosis` 3.1.0: `workflow_orchestrator` dispatches to named cells and marks failed steps failed; planners name a tool or say why not; analysis tools stop inventing metrics; skill frontmatter parses properly and the response is bounded.\n- `hela-ribosome` 3.2.1: opencode v2 CLI alignment; follow-ups resume the addressed session; the managed-server password is redacted from session output; hooks wait for output rather than their own echo.\n- `hela-cytosol` 3.3.0: cookies are page-scoped and redacted with explicit widening; uploads are contained to workspace roots; `browser_extract_table` honours `header` without eating a data row.\n- `hela-genome` 3.1.0: deletes are durable across central sync; the secret scan no longer opens live SQLite files, so WAL locks and post-scan writes survive.\n\n## [1.10.10] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `designer-mcp` 3.1.0: inline contrast is computed (WCAG 2.x) rather than string-matched, so white-on-white at 1.01:1 now fails and the sample page grades B/75 instead of A/96; palette variants pass achromatic inputs through unchanged; the High Contrast label names the surface it was measured against; derived `--color-focus` chroma is clamped into sRGB; and the five Stitch CLI tools are advertised only when the CLI is present (23 vs 28 tools, `HELA_PHENOTYPE_STITCH` overrides).\n\n## [1.10.9] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- Repin the runtime to the commit that refreshes `npmDepsHash`. The 1.4.0 version bump changed the npm-deps derivation input, so the Nix verification job failed at build time on a fixed-output hash mismatch — the installer and CI both build from the pinned revision, which still carried the stale hash. No runtime behaviour change.\n\n## [1.10.8] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `researcher-mcp` 4.2.2: the IDF half of the scorer was inert in production because `research_brief` passed no candidate corpus. Terms are now weighted by how much they discriminate among the returned pages, so a common term contributes little and a rare one carries the match.\n\n## [1.10.7] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `researcher-mcp` 4.2.1: the enzyme fixes from 4.1.0/4.2.0 were compiled into a module the shipped entry never loads. The real `research_brief` (root `research-server.ts`) now backs off once on an empty ranked result, discloses it, returns the feedback handle, and appends spool signals from `extract_content`. Found by post-deploy verification: the pinned 4.2.0 cell still behaved like 4.0.9.\n\n## [1.10.6] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Changed\n- **`hela.pipe` fails closed.** A chain whose middle step failed returned `ok: true` and piped the error text into the next step, which produced a confident result computed from `ENOENT`. `ok` now reflects every step, `failedStep` names the first failure, `onError` defaults to `stop`, and each result carries its own `ok`. This is a breaking default by intent: callers that checked `ok` were getting a wrong answer. Runtime 1.4.0.\n\n## [1.10.5] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `filesystem-mcp` 3.0.6: four responses promised what the implementation did not deliver. Ranged `read_file` now snaps to character boundaries and reports `startByte`/`endByte` (chunked reads of non-ASCII files no longer lose a character per boundary); `extract_archive` reports `extracted`/`entries`/`skipped` with a reason instead of a silent partial success; `watch_file` buffers events and returns them on the next call (plus waits for chokidar's initial scan); `compare_files` reports `size` in bytes with a separate `characters` field and a correct line count.\n\n## [1.10.4] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Added\n- The adaptive search priors can now learn. Enzyme observes when a caller follows up on a URL a brief returned and appends a used=true record (with display rank) to `~/.local/state/hela/search-feedback.jsonl`; genome drains that spool into `source_feedback` whenever `search_feedback` or `search_priors` is called. One writer keeps the table honest, the file is claimed by rename so nothing imports twice, and failed inserts are preserved for a retry. `hela-enzyme` 4.2.0, `hela-genome` 3.0.7.\n\n## [1.10.3] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `chaining-mcp` 3.0.9: peer discovery reads the runtime's generated tool catalog, so Mitosis sees the ten cells it orchestrates when they are multiplexed behind one endpoint. `validate_tool_chain` no longer reports every real tool as unavailable (`read_file` on `hela-membrane` included), and availability now distinguishes an absent tool (error) from an undiscovered server or missing catalog (warning) — the direction that had been discarding correct plans. Verified against the installed catalog: 10 cells, 299 tools.\n\n## [1.10.2] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- `terminal-mcp` 3.0.7: local commands run from a temporary script instead of `sh -c`, so `pkill -f` no longer matches and kills the wrapper shell, and `pgrep -f` no longer reports it as a live process. RTK summarisation rejects summaries below 5% of the source, retains and names the original output, and anchors its log heuristic to line starts. Two defects that both made a failed step look successful.\n- `researcher-mcp` 4.1.0: `research_brief` retries once with a reduced query when every source returns nothing and discloses it (`backoff: \"…\"`); scoring folds morphological variants and weights terms by how much they discriminate among the candidates; the brief returns a feedback handle plus the exact `genome search_feedback` call, and a follow-up tool on a brief URL appends it with `used=true`.\n\n## [1.10.1] - 2026-11-22\n\n### Fixed\n- Running `doctor` against a bare checkout printed ten rows of `missing*` / `Not checked` without naming a path or a next step, which is indistinguishable from a broken install — and reads as a platform bug when it happens on Windows. Rows now say `not installed`, and when every component is missing the report lists the paths it resolved plus the two commands that fix it (install, then `doctor --prefix`).\n- A profile can be installed under any mode, but doctor only looked at `\u003cprofile>-individual` unless `--prefix` was passed. Installing with `--runtime` therefore made a healthy host report every component missing. When the default prefix has no manifest, doctor now scans for sibling `\u003cprofile>-*` installations and prints each one with the `--mode` or `--prefix` that would diagnose it; JSON output carries the same list under `installationCandidates`.\n\n## [1.10.0] - 2026-11-21\n\n### Added\n- `doctor.mjs --hosts` inspects the layer the doctor previously ignored: the wiring between client applications and the runtime. It checks the interpreter floor derived from cell `engines`, whether `nodejs` is still marked manual for apt, the gate file the launcher reads, credential-file modes and key presence, all six client configs, backup exposure, and a live `initialize`/`tools/list` handshake. `--repair` reinstalls the pinned node package, re-asserts the manual mark and fixes credential/backup modes; exit codes remain 0/1/2.\n- `scripts/host-entry.sh` is the canonical client entry point, installed as `~/.local/bin/hela-runtime-mcp`. It resolves the interpreter at spawn time, refuses to start below the required major, sources one 0600 credentials file, and logs failures because MCP hosts swallow child stderr. It is PATH-independent: every external command is absolute, so it reports a stripped environment instead of failing inside it.\n- `docs/reliability.md` documents the host-to-cell failure model with eight diagrams. Sources are Mermaid under `assets/diagrams/`, renders are SVG beside them, because the document renderer carries `markdown-it` without Mermaid and escapes raw HTML.\n\n### Changed\n- Credentials found in client-config backups were copied into mema-vault before those backups were deleted, so the host doctor reports a clean backup surface instead of a permanent warning. `docs/reliability.md` documents vault-based recovery.\n\n## [1.9.27] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- The Docker doctor reported `DEGRADED` with an empty `[]` on a clean image because it compared the runtime catalog against bare cell tool names. The container never receives `HELA_NAMESPACE_TOOLS`, so the runtime applies its new default and advertises `hela_membrane_read_file`; 23 of 24 expected names therefore looked missing. Coverage now accepts the runtime's namespaced name or the bare one, and a real gap names the cell and tool instead of printing an empty array. Unit tests cover both surfaces and a genuinely missing tool.\n- Enzyme `4.0.9` runs its priors suite on Node 20 instead of failing at import: `node:sqlite` only exists from 22.5, so the database cases are gated on the runtime version while the resolution and collapse-guard tests still run. The package claims Node 20, where learned priors deliberately degrade to the static table.\n\n## [1.9.26] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- Learned search priors never applied on a host whose genome still uses the legacy central store. Enzyme resolved `~/memory/memory.db` only, while genome keeps writing `~/memory.db` when its migration did not run — so every brief reported `priors: static` and the recorded decisions sat in a file nobody read. Enzyme now resolves the way genome does: the migrated path when it exists, otherwise the legacy file. Verified end to end with an isolated HOME: ten decisions land in the file the brief reads and it reports `priors: learned (10 samples)`.\n\n## [1.9.25] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Added\n- The search loop closes through Genome. Genome `3.0.6` records which source produced the result a caller actually used in a `source_feedback` table in the central memory database, exposed as `search_feedback` (record) and `search_priors` (read). Enzyme `4.0.7` reads that table read-only with `node:sqlite` — no new dependency — and ranks `research_brief` / `multi_site_search` with the derived multipliers instead of constants, stating which table it used (`priors: static` or `priors: learned (N)`).\n- The priors carry a collapse guard, because a feedback loop can starve itself: one unlucky run lowers a source's multiplier, the lower multiplier keeps its results under the relevance floor, no further samples arrive, and the mistake becomes permanent. A source with no samples in an active class, or whose newest sample is over 30 days old, is floored at 1.0 and lifted by 0.15 so it stays reachable. Three tests in each repo fail if the guard is removed.\n- `scripts/check-prose-facts.mjs` now fails `docs:check` when hand-written prose restates a fact the build knows: an unknown Hela repository name, a live version literal, a roster claim that disagrees with the inventory or profile set, a licence no cell uses, or a per-profile server count that drifted. Ten tests prove each rule fires. It found two real drifts on its first run (a page claiming runtime 1.3.6 after the runtime moved, and a setup page pinning Phenotype 3.0.3 against an inventory at 3.0.5).\n\n### Changed\n- Runtime `1.3.7` namespaces cell tools by default: `hela_nucleus_execute_command` instead of `execute_command`. A bare name shadows whatever the host already exposes and the collision is settled by whichever server connected last. `HELA_NAMESPACE_TOOLS=0` restores the previous surface; this is breaking for clients that pin bare tool names, and the manifest cache key already covers the mode so a switching client rebuilds its fingerprints once.\n- Genome creates the parent directory of a configured database path. `GUARDIAN_CENTRAL_DB` pointing into a directory that did not exist yet used to die with `SQLITE_CANTOPEN` on the first query.\n\n### Closed\n- Four Dependabot pull requests (chaining `#13`, `#18`; filesystem `#17`, `#16`) were closed as already satisfied: both default branches resolve zod 4.6.7, glob 14.0.6 and minimatch 11.0.9.\n- `filesystem-mcp#15` (signed receipts) was answered: no signing keys in the server, but populated `artifacts[].sha256` digests for produced files are a change worth taking.\n\n## [1.9.24] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- Cytosol `3.2.6` accepts what `browser_observe` actually reports. `browser_fill_form` keys resolve in order — a ref number from the last observe/get_state, a bare name or id, then the key as a CSS selector — so the observe → fill path no longer misses and every existing selector caller keeps working. Attribute values are quoted, so a field name containing a quote cannot break out of the selector. An unmatched key now aborts the call and names what it tried instead of reaching `page.fill` and blocking for the full 30-second locator timeout; a miss resolves in milliseconds.\n\n## [1.9.23] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- Enzyme `4.0.6` scores every search result on query-term coverage with a phrase bonus, title weighted above snippet, and drops results below a named floor. Wikipedia's floor is higher (0.4) than Google's (0.16) because its OR-matched recall is cheap and its precision is not: the scatter query that returned Smalltalk and XML now returns nothing, while a precise query still returns five pages. `research_brief` and `multi_site_search` apply cheap query-class priors, every result line carries its score, and `academic_search` enforces `dateRange` on arXiv with a submittedDate window plus a published-date post-filter — a 2021 paper can no longer answer a `y2` query.\n- Nucleus `3.0.6` passes any command containing `;`, `&` or `|` to the shell instead of treating the whole string as a working directory, so `cd /tmp && echo marker-ok` runs; only a standalone `cd` updates the session directory, and it takes its first path argument so a trailing option is not swallowed.\n- Runtime `1.3.6` types the cell RPC boundary: `callTool` resolves a validated MCP tool result and raises `CellProtocolError` on a JSON-RPC error or an unexpected shape instead of forwarding `undefined`; cell directory resolution no longer consults `process.cwd()` and names the root and every path tried when a cell is missing; and a malformed JSON-RPC frame rejects its request immediately rather than costing a full timeout.\n\n### Changed\n- One agent tool: Mitosis's cell-local `agent_run` was advertised next to the runtime-native `hela.agent.run` and carried the runtime description, so callers could not tell which one ran. Only `hela.agent.run` is exposed now.\n- Genome `3.0.5` adds `npm run vault:conflicts`, which archives frontmatter-only sync conflicts without rewriting the note and lists body-divergent ones for a per-note decision. First pass: 641 conflicts archived, 615 left across 302 notes.\n\n## [1.9.22] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- The licence wording in the legal pages, `llms.txt` and the JSON-LD licence list was hand-written, so a seventh copyleft cell would have left those statements stale — the same failure mode as the old runtime prose. They are now generated from `config/inventory.json`: counts, cell names, the always-installed set, the structured-data licence URLs and the machine-readable line all follow the inventory, and meta descriptions no longer encode a count that can drift.\n- A test recomputes those strings from the inventory and fails on any divergence; `docs:check` also rejects a stale page, verified by flipping one cell's licence.\n\n## [1.9.21] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- Runtime `1.3.4` no longer hides a cell whose catalog manifest went stale: each cell's manifest source is logged with the regeneration command, only stale cells are probed once so their tools stay advertised, and the sources are exposed on `/readyz`. On servy this had silently reduced the surface from 240 to 209 tools.\n- Cytosol `3.2.5` reconnects once when the browser context was closed under it; a blank page is reported instead of retried, because the previous call may already have acted.\n- Plastid `3.0.5` fails honestly when a headless Blender render writes no file, instead of returning a path to nothing.\n- Mitosis `3.0.8` and Membrane `3.0.5` complete the zod 4 / glob 14 migration; `npm audit --omit=dev` now reports zero vulnerabilities in every cell and the runtime.\n\n### Added\n- Genome `3.0.4` stamps `PRAGMA user_version` after creating its schema, treats a rerun as a no-op and refuses a database written by a newer build.\n- `npm run nix:hash` in the runtime refreshes the flake's npm dependency hash and rebuilds to confirm, so a version bump no longer costs a CI round trip.\n\n## [1.9.20] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Added\n- `docs/deployment-2026-11-19.md`: the servy rollout record for 1.9.19 / runtime 1.3.3 — eight-cell `dev-workspace` roster, Nix package, staged wrapper, drop-in switch, backups and rollback, plus the stale-digest failure that hid two cells until the catalog was regenerated on the host.\n\n## [1.9.19] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- The legal pages still claimed the whole ecosystem was MIT and granted sublicensing and sale over code that is GPL-3.0-or-later. Terms (EN and ID), their meta and JSON-LD descriptions, `llms.txt` and the `SoftwareApplication` structured data now state the split: MIT repository, six GPL-3.0-or-later cells named explicitly, per-cell terms govern redistribution. A test fails if the blanket claim returns.\n\n### Added\n- Documented the runtime update tools (`hela.update.check`, `hela.update.apply`) in the README, the on-demand guide and `llms.txt`. They already shipped with runtime 1.3.x; the docs now name them so agents can find them.\n\n## [1.9.18] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Changed\n- `npm audit fix` across nine cells and the runtime cleared a critical advisory in Mitosis and Cytosol plus twenty others: mitosis 11 to 3, cytosol 9 to 0, nucleus 8 to 0, membrane 10 to 3, genome/ribosome/enzyme/phenotype/runtime 2 to 0, receptor 1 to 0. The remaining findings need breaking majors (zod 4, glob 14) and stay with their open dependabot PRs.\n- Runtime `1.3.3` refreshed its Nix fixed-output dependency hash, verified with a local `nix build` before release.\n- No tool surfaces changed (292 base tools); the version guards kept every `ServerInfo` in step.\n\n## [1.9.17] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Added\n- Per-cell licence disclosure (PR #7): `config/inventory.json` now carries each cell's licence, the library footer and page headers derive the real blend (MIT suite + six GPL-3.0-or-later cells) instead of a blanket MIT claim, and the architecture reference states which copyleft cells every profile installs. Derived from the inventory and covered by tests.\n\n### Fixed\n- `update --json` (PR #6) emits one compact document, so a caller reading the last stdout line cannot be defeated by installer output.\n\n## [1.9.16] - 2026-11-19\n\n### Fixed\n- Runtime prose sat outside the single-sourcing pipeline: the architecture cards (EN and ID) and the on-demand guide said `1.2.1` while the inventory said `1.3.2`, and CI stayed green because the generator skipped the `hela-runtime` prose entirely. `generate-docs` now rewrites those sentences from the inventory, `docs:check` fails on drift (verified by deliberately reverting a mention), and a test covers both languages while keeping the dated Servy rollout record at the version it actually deployed.\n\n## [1.9.15] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Fixed\n- The tmux provisioning tests inherited `CI` from `process.env`, so on GitHub Actions provisioning short-circuited to `skipped`/`ci` before any assertion: eight failures that only existed on CI. The fixtures now strip CI variables, and the CI-disable case keeps its own explicit test.\n\n## [1.9.14] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Added\n- Mitosis `3.0.6` bundles the registry `pcb` skill (KiCad workflow, verification scripts, JLCPCB preparation, MIT licence preserved). The staged copy contained a duplicate of itself under `skills/pcb/pcb`; 3.3 MB removed so discovery sees one definition.\n\n### Fixed\n- Cytosol `3.2.3`: `BROWSER_HEADLESS` defaults to auto — headless without `DISPLAY`, headful with one, explicit values still winning. Display-less service processes could not launch Chromium before. `resolveHeadless` is exported and tested.\n- Browser CI pins `ubuntu-24.04`; `ubuntu-latest` moved to 26.04 where `playwright install --with-deps` exits without a Chromium build.\n- Runtime `1.3.2` refreshes the Nix `npmDepsHash` after the version bump.\n\n## [1.9.13] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Fixed\n- Every cell and the runtime now single-source the advertised MCP version in one module, guarded by a test against `package.json`. Six cells had shipped a stale `ServerInfo` this way (nucleus, enzyme, phenotype, receptor, cytosol, plastid), and a deliberate drift in mitosis was verified to fail its suite.\n\n## [1.9.12] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Added\n- Hardware and live-service validation: receptor `3.0.4` makes the device integration suite runnable (it spawned a non-existent entrypoint) and passes 18 checks on a Pixel 8 — UI hierarchy, element discovery, app lifecycle, rotation, screen recording, session, screenshot, tap/swipe, clipboard.\n- `npm run test:browserless` validates a remote Browserless instance with local fallback disabled; `npm run test:blender` drives the Blender bridge (scene, in-Blender code execution, object details); `npm run test:fallback` proves the search degradation chain with an invalid provider key.\n- Scrcpy parses the `SET_CLIPBOARD` acknowledgement frame instead of resetting the buffer on it.\n\n### Known limitations\n- The remote Browserless context can be closed between calls; `browser_health` reports it but there is no automatic reconnect.\n- Headless Blender reports a render path without writing a file; rendering still needs a GUI session.\n\n## [1.9.11] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Fixed\n- Receptor `3.0.3`: the hardware integration harness spawned a non-existent entrypoint, so `npm run test:integration` could never pass; it now runs 18 checks against a real Pixel 8 (UI hierarchy, element discovery, app lifecycle, rotation, screen recording, session, screenshot, tap/swipe, clipboard).\n\n### Changed\n- Repins Receptor and publishes the `v1.9.11` snapshot.\n\n## [1.9.10] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Added\n- `npm run test:acp` drives the Zed Codex adapter over ACP (`initialize` → `session/new` → `session/prompt`) and passes only on a real text reply, so the authenticated ACP session is verified without the GUI.\n\n### Fixed\n- Documented why the registry Codex adapter failed with `model_not_found` for every model: the bundled adapter (`codex-acp` 0.11.1) carries a Codex core older than the account's model list. `@agentclientprotocol/codex-acp` 2.7.0 works with the same ChatGPT login; the guide shows the custom `agent_servers.codex-acp` entry and the manual troubleshooting signature.\n\n## [1.9.9] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Added\n- Mitosis `3.0.4` splits `terminal.execute` into one-shot execution (Nucleus) and `terminal.session` (Ribosome), so planning can tell \"run pytest\" from \"drive a tmux session\".\n- Runtime `1.3.1` adds structured per-cell logs, per-cell start/restart/failure/tool counters on `/healthz`, and separate `/livez` and `/readyz` endpoints.\n\n### Changed\n- Ribosome `3.1.0` gains `npm run test:remote`, a live integration test for the ssh transport (spawn, tmux, reattach, kill on close, transport exit kind) verified against `servy`.\n- Repins the coordinated `v1.9.9` snapshot; `latest-stable` moves to it.\n\n## [1.9.8] - 2026-11-18\n\n### Changed\n- Repin every changed cell in `config/inventory.json` and publish the coordinated `v1.9.8` snapshot: Nucleus `3.0.3`, Ribosome `3.1.0`, Membrane `3.0.2`, Enzyme `4.0.2`, Receptor `3.0.2`, Plastid `3.0.2`, Phenotype `3.0.3`, and Mitosis at the mema-vault backup commit.\n- Regenerate `config/tool-catalog.json` and the per-cell canonical tool references from the new pins; the catalog still advertises 292 base tools across ten cells.\n- Sharpen the advertised versions that had drifted from `package.json`: Nucleus reported `3.0.2`, Enzyme's legacy entrypoint reported `4.0.1`, and Phenotype reported `3.0.2` from both the MCP server and export metadata.\n\n# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to this project.\n\n## [1.9.7] - 2026-11-18\n\n- Provision tmux for Nucleus/Ribosome profiles with `HELA_SYSTEM_DEPS_INSTALL=1` as an explicit noninteractive opt-in, still blocked by CI, failed sudo checks, and `--without-system-deps`.\n- Report optional tmux readiness as a doctor warning and cover a Nucleus profile install plus doctor run in CI without changing system packages.\n- Sweep runtime facades, pin the 292 cell-schema metric definition, generate the installer flag reference, and add a reproducible persona/discovery payload measurement script.\n\n## [1.9.6] - 2026-11-15\n\n### Added\n- Bundle Google Chrome's Modern Web Guidance skill in Phenotype 3.0.3, pinned to upstream release `v1.0.0`, CLI package `1.0.0`, and corpus `2026_11_09-5bcc021b`.\n- Add a dedicated prebuilt-skills catalog showing server ownership, availability in all eight profiles, and the install/sync lifecycle.\n- Publish the coordinated `v1.9.6` snapshot and advance `latest-stable` to the new Phenotype revision.\n\n## [1.9.5] - 2026-11-15\n\n- Clarify that Frea and Mema are Azzar's personal AI assistant personas and that the distributable HeLa persona carries their documented operating principles without copying private state.\n\n## [1.9.4] - 2026-11-15\n\n- Install a complete profile-aware HeLa persona on a clean workspace, blending Frea's autonomous execution with Mema's security and operational discipline.\n- Preserve existing `agent_persona/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `GEMINI.md` files instead of overwriting a user's persona.\n\n## [1.9.3] - 2026-11-15\n\n- Bundle the registry `pcb` skill through HeLa Mitosis so selected profiles receive KiCad schematic, layout, routing, independent verification, and fabrication-preparation guidance through the normal skill-sync path.\n- Document the recommended PCB workflow across Mitosis, Genome, Membrane, Nucleus, Cytosol, and Enzyme; no PCB project files, credentials, browser state, or personal paths are distributed.\n- Publish Phenotype `3.0.2` with the research-backed `brand-architecture` skill and refresh the reproducible `latest-stable` pin to its new commit.\n\n## [1.9.2] - 2026-11-14\n\n### Added\n- Make Needle and Mirage installer capabilities profile-aware: every canonical profile provisions Needle through Mitosis, while profiles containing Cytosol provision the managed Mirage Python bridge automatically.\n- Add checksum-verified Windows x64/ARM64 Needle assets so native Windows profiles receive the same local agent capability without manual path configuration.\n- Pass the managed Mirage and Windows-compatible Needle paths through launchers and generated configurations, and validate Mirage readiness in `doctor`.\n- Publish Terminal/Nucleus `3.0.2` with checksum-verified user-local RTK bootstrap on Linux, macOS, Windows, and Windows ARM64.\n- Move `latest-stable` to the coordinated `v1.9.2` snapshot while retaining `v1.9.1` unchanged.\n\n### Fixed\n- Align the mutable `latest-stable` metadata and generated Cytosol catalog with the current post-release documentation commit without rewriting the immutable `v1.8.0` snapshot.\n\n## [1.9.1] - 2026-11-14\n\n### Fixed\n- Refresh every canonical cell and runtime revision to the latest validated default-branch commit.\n- Publish the coordinated `v1.9.1` snapshot while retaining the immutable `v1.9.0` release.\n\n## [1.9.0] - 2026-11-14\n\n### Added\n- Add a cross-platform Node update controller with read-only snapshot checks, explicit confirmation for apply, rollback records, and doctor verification.\n- Add opt-in check-only schedules for systemd user timers, launchd agents, and Windows Task Scheduler; automatic checks never apply updates.\n- Expose `hela.update.check` and confirmation-gated `hela.update.apply` through the profile-aware runtime agent catalog.\n- Publish the immutable `v1.9.0` snapshot with runtime `1.3.0`.\n\n## [1.8.0] - 2026-11-13\n\n### Added\n- Upgraded HeLa Cytosol to `3.2.0` with dedicated image analysis through local paths, safe HTTP(S) URLs, and base64 image input.\n- Added the full Mirage VLM pipeline to the catalog: scene understanding, OCR, object detection, segmentation, spatial relations, and schema-based decisions.\n- Published the reproducible `v1.8.0` snapshot and moved `latest-stable` to the dedicated-image release.\n\n## [1.7.0] - 2026-11-13\n\n### Added\n- Upgraded HeLa Cytosol to `3.1.0` with first-class Mirage visual intelligence.\n- Added lazy local model acquisition, managed Cactus runtime setup, and the `browser_visual_analyze` capability to the canonical catalog.\n- Published the reproducible `v1.7.0` snapshot and moved `latest-stable` to the new Cytosol release.\n\n## [1.6.4] - 2026-11-13\n\n### Fixed\n- Updated the `latest-stable` snapshot alias to the coordinated 1.6.3 cell and runtime release pins.\n\n## [1.6.3] - 2026-11-13\n\n- Add Node-first and PowerShell/CMD installer, updater, build, test, and clean entrypoints; retain Bash files as compatibility shims.\n- Allow unsupported pinned Needle platforms to complete installation with an explicit runtime fallback instead of aborting the whole ecosystem install.\n\n## [1.6.2] - 2026-11-08\n\n- Adopt the Dependabot-updated Plastid and Receptor lockfiles, clearing the open MCP SDK and `@hono/node-server` advisories.\n- Replace Plastid's `\"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk\": \"latest\"` with a caret range so future lock refreshes are reviewable instead of silent.\n- Plastid keeps 12 passing regressions and `npm audit` reports zero vulnerabilities; Receptor keeps 123 passing tests.\n- Cell versions and every other pin are unchanged from 1.6.1.\n\n## [1.6.1] - 2026-11-08\n\n- Add `setup.sh mode` to convert an existing managed installation between `individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, and `nix` in place.\n- Reuse cells already built at the pinned revision, preserve `data/` and the profile/snapshot, and verify the converted installation with doctor before reporting success.\n- Restore the previous manifest and leave a pending marker when verification fails; refuse pending, unmanaged, or unchanged targets.\n- Cover host-family switching, transport guards, pending markers, and Docker round-trips in tests and CI. Enzyme, runtime, and other cell pins are unchanged from 1.6.0.\n\n## [1.6.0] - 2026-11-08\n\n- Pin Enzyme 4.0.0, whose `google_search` now requires `query`. The old `q` argument is rejected rather than translated; batch tools keep `queries`.\n- Share one strict search schema between Enzyme's shipped and modular entrypoints: trimmed non-empty queries, integer result counts from 1 to 10, unknown fields refused before any provider request.\n- Add `setup.sh mode` to convert a managed installation between individual, runtime, Docker, Docker-runtime, and Nix in place, reusing verified builds and preserving data directories.\n- Regenerate the canonical tool catalog and documentation from the new Enzyme schema. Tool counts and all other cell/runtime pins are unchanged.\n- Preserve every historical snapshot from v1.0.0 through v1.5.3.\n\n## [1.5.3] - 2026-11-07\n\n- Render Genome relation links in canonical order so replicas with different SQLite insertion orders produce identical notes.\n- Add an insertion-order regression; all 19 suites / 264 tests, the TypeScript build and zero-warning source lint pass.\n- Preserve the published 1.5.2 snapshot and all other cell/runtime pins.\n\n## [1.5.2] - 2026-11-07\n\n- Pin Genome's replicated vault timestamp/order convergence fix and explicit archived repair API; ordinary CLI sync still leaves divergent notes unresolved.\n- Replace Genome's unsafe `any` types with typed database results and validated request/import boundaries. Source lint now covers top-level files and fails on warnings; 19 suites / 263 tests and the TypeScript build pass.\n- Retain the managed individual/runtime/Docker/Nix installer and doctor shipped after 1.5.1; its mechanism acceptance remains documented separately.\n- Keep all other cell/runtime pins and historical versioned snapshots unchanged. No vault content, credentials, or operational backups are included in this source snapshot.\n\n## [1.5.1] - 2026-11-06\n\n- Refresh current EN/ID web pages, setup/integration/troubleshooting guides, and all ten cells' version/tool references from canonical metadata.\n- Validate generated documentation, local links/anchors, and browser configuration previews before Pages publication.\n- Refresh sanitized client examples and document deferred security, provider/hardware validation, SQLite safety, and optional Obsidian mirroring accurately.\n- Pin documentation-only cell commits in a new immutable snapshot. Cells remain 3.0.0; runtime remains 1.2.0. Historical snapshots, vendor versions, and live services are unchanged.\n\n## [1.5.0] - 2026-11-06\n\n- Pin runtime 1.2.0 with true first-call startup and profile/build/config-aware schema caching.\n- Generate standalone schema catalogs and documentation counts from canonical inventory metadata and isolated feature-variant probes.\n- Share inventory validation/path resolution across config, doctor, version, profile, and integration tooling; verify runtime catalog parity without child startup.\n- Measure cold/warm routing across all profiles; retain standalone compatibility and machine selections.\n\n## [1.4.0] - 2026-11-06\n\n- Pin current published cell/runtime revisions in a new snapshot; retain v1.0.0–v1.3.0 unchanged and correct runtime snapshot metadata to 1.1.0.\n- Include Genome memory safety, atomic claims, runtime timeout recovery, and clean-install Zod 4 compatibility fixes.\n- Replace process-alive smoke checks with isolated MCP initialize/tool discovery and strict version/pin agreement.\n- Correct Codex TOML to `mcp_servers`, escape generated values, and verify the 80-combination configuration matrix.\n- Record native x86_64/aarch64 Linux Nix builds separately from client authentication, hardware testing, and unresolved vault conflicts.\n\n## [3.0.0] - 2026-10-31\n\n### Changed\n- **Version reset to 3.0.0** — per-server version metadata baseline reset (Phase 7). All `package.json`, MCP `ServerInfo.version`, and HTTP metadata now report `3.0.0`. This is a metadata/interface baseline reset; for `hela-plastid` it is not an upgrade from the previous `4.2.0` advert.\n"},{"source":"CONTRIBUTING.md","title":"Contributing to the HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"Thank you for your interest in contributing to the HeLa MCP Ecosystem! This document provides guidelines and information for contributors and maintainers.","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/CONTRIBUTING.html","text":"# Contributing to the HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\nThank you for your interest in contributing to the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**! This document provides guidelines and information for contributors and maintainers.\n\n![Blotcat carefully placing a new, glowing building block on top of an established, complex structure](assets/blotcat-contributing.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Boundaries & 10-MCP Ceiling\n\n* **The 10-MCP Capability Ceiling**: The ecosystem maintains exactly 10 canonical MCP servers (7 core headless, 3 specialized GUI/device). Contributions focus on reliability, performance, tool quality, and testing rather than adding new server repositories.\n* **The 4-Tier Naming Architecture**:\n  * Tier 1: Public Identity (`HeLa \u003cComponent>`, e.g. *HeLa Mitosis*, *HeLa Genome*)\n  * Tier 2: Machine Identifier (`hela-*`, e.g. `hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`)\n  * Tier 3: Technical Source Repository (`chaining-mcp`, `project-mcp`, etc.)\n  * Tier 4: Immutable Commit Hash (the selected immutable snapshot in `config/snapshots/`)\n* **Repository Autonomy**: Upstream repositories remain independent and modular.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Development & Verification Workflow\n\n### 2.1 Local Environment Setup\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\nnode setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client skip --non-interactive\n```\n\n### 2.2 Running Diagnostics & Tests\nBefore submitting changes, ensure all test suites pass locally:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Run diagnostic health checks\nnpm run doctor\n\n# 2. Run 80-combination client configuration checks\nnpm run test:matrix\n\n# 3. Run standalone MCP integration (tool counts depend on features)\nnpm test\n\n# 4. Keep generated versions, tool references and site metadata current\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts\nnpm run docs:generate\nnpm run docs:check\nnpm run test:docs\n\n# 5. Run configured pre-commit checks\npre-commit run --all-files\n```\n\n### 2.3 Version bumps touch seven surfaces\n\nA release is only consistent when every surface names the same version. `npm run docs:check`\nends with `scripts/check-release-consistency.mjs`, which fails with a per-surface list, so\nthis is enforced rather than remembered:\n\n| Surface | What it must say |\n|---|---|\n| `package.json` | the version itself — the source of truth |\n| `config/snapshots/v\u003cversion>.json` | exists, with matching `snapshot_version` |\n| `config/snapshots/latest-stable.json` | `snapshot_version` = new version |\n| `README.md` | `Current source snapshot: **v\u003cversion>**` |\n| `CHANGELOG.md` | leading `## [\u003cversion>] - YYYY-MM-DD` entry |\n| `RELEASE_NOTES.md` | leading `## v\u003cversion>` entry |\n| `docs/current-versions.md` | `Ecosystem **\u003cversion>**` |\n\nThe snapshot pair is the one people forget, and forgetting it is not cosmetic: docs then\nadvertise `--snapshot v\u003cversion>`, and the installer has nothing to resolve. Regenerate the\nrest rather than editing by hand:\n\n```bash\nnpm version \u003cversion> --no-git-tag-version\nnpm run catalog:generate     # only when the tool surface changed\nnpm run docs:generate\nnpm run docs:check           # includes the consistency guard\n```\n\n### 2.3 Build and preview the complete documentation site\n\nUse Node.js 22 LTS. Markdown rendering is a pinned build-only dependency; visitors receive static HTML, not a client-side Markdown framework.\n\n```bash\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts\nnpm run docs:generate\nnpm run docs:build\npython3 -m http.server 8080 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory _site\n```\n\nOpen `http://127.0.0.1:8080/library.html`. The builder publishes tracked root documentation, all Markdown under `docs/` (except website source files), the license, and public configuration examples. Add new documents to Git before building so they enter the publication inventory. Untracked notes, credentials, databases, Git internals and unrelated directories are not published.\n\nThe document bodies remain in their source language; English and Indonesian navigation lead to the same source content. Dated verification reports are labeled historical. Relative document links, heading anchors and illustrations resolve within the website; original source downloads and `llms-full.txt` are generated from the same files.\n\n`npm run test:docs` checks publication coverage, links and anchors, safe Markdown rendering, source downloads, search, and deterministic builds. The existing Pages workflow installs the locked dependencies, checks the docs, and publishes `_site` to `gh-pages` when public documentation changes. Do not edit `_site` by hand.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Pull Request Guidelines\n\n1. **Target Branch**: Target the maintained default branch (`master`) unless a maintainer requests a release/development branch.\n2. **Commit Conventions**: Follow standard Conventional Commits:\n   * `feat(scope): ...`\n   * `fix(scope): ...`\n   * `docs(scope): ...`\n   * `test(scope): ...`\n   * `chore(scope): ...`\n3. **CI Matrix Conformance**: Configuration checks cover `ubuntu-latest` and `macos-latest` on Node.js 18, 20, and 22; documentation builds and tests run on Node 22. This is not MCP runtime compatibility evidence for Node 18; use Node 22 LTS for the tested full-stack development environment. Hardware, provider and authenticated GUI integrations need separate validation.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Attribution & Recognition\n\nThe HeLa project respectfully recognizes **Henrietta Lacks** and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells as an inspiration for immortal, modular cellular architecture in software.\n"},{"source":"LICENSE","title":"MIT License","summary":"Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software…","category":"project","archived":false,"link":"library/LICENSE.html","text":"MIT License\n\nCopyright (c) 2026 Azzar\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\nof this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\nin the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\nto use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\ncopies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\nfurnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all\ncopies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\nIMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\nFITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\nAUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\nLIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\nOUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\nSOFTWARE.\n\n---\n\nThis license applies to the AZZAR MCP Server Suite repository and its contents. Individual MCP servers maintain their own licenses. Please check each server's repository for specific licensing information.\n"},{"source":"README.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"A modular, immortal cellular AI tooling ecosystem built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), engineered by Azzar to equip AI agents with full-spectrum autonomous capabilities…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/README.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\nA modular, immortal cellular AI tooling ecosystem built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), engineered by Azzar to equip AI agents with full-spectrum autonomous capabilities across reasoning, persistent memory, workspace management, system execution, research, design, device automation, and 3D modeling.\n\n> **New to MCP?** Read the **[Beginner's Guide](docs/beginner-guide.md)** for requirements, profile selection, installation, and verification.\n\n**Read all documentation on the website:** [Document library](https://hela.my.id/library.html) · [Navigasi Bahasa Indonesia](https://hela.my.id/id/library.html). Guides, release history, contribution rules, and verification reports are published in full, with search and source downloads. Documents retain their source language.\n\nCurrent source snapshot: **v1.11.26**, with runtime **1.4.5**. True first-call startup, fingerprinted discovery caches, canonical inventory tooling, profile-aware local capabilities, and managed snapshot updates are implemented; see [on-demand runtime](docs/on-demand-runtime.md) and [installation updates](docs/installation.md#updates-and-automatic-checks). Authenticated Zed/Codex ACP sessions, hardware integrations, and live vault conflict resolution remain separate; see [release notes](RELEASE_NOTES.md).\n\n![Blotcat plugging MCP server cables into the central AI Hub](assets/blotcat-hero.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## Respectful Attribution & Metaphor\n\nThe **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** is named in respectful recognition of **Henrietta Lacks** (1920–1951) and the immortal scientific legacy of HeLa cells, which transformed global biomedical research. In this software architecture, HeLa serves as a **cellular biology metaphor**:\n\n* **Cellular Modularity**: Just as biological cells contain specialized organelles coordinated by the nucleus and genome, the HeLa MCP Ecosystem deploys specialized tool servers coordinated by a dual backbone.\n* **Continuous Replicability & Immortality**: Environments can be snapshot-pinned, reproduced deterministically on any machine, and restored cleanly without state degradation.\n* **Component Independence**: Underlying repositories, technical package names, and Git commit histories remain autonomous and unaltered while functioning as a harmonious organism.\n\n---\n\n## Dual-Backbone Architecture\n\nEvery profile in the HeLa ecosystem is anchored by two core backbone servers:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Agent[\"Agent / LLM Orchestrator\"]\n\n    Mitosis[\"HeLa Mitosis\u003cbr/>(Orchestrator)\u003cbr/>- Dynamic Routing\u003cbr/>- Step Reasoning\u003cbr/>- Tool Planning\"]\n    Genome[\"HeLa Genome\u003cbr/>(State & Memory)\u003cbr/>- Knowledge Graph\u003cbr/>- Entities / Relations\u003cbr/>- Observations\"]\n\n    Membrane[\"HeLa Membrane\u003cbr/>Workspace FS\"]\n    Nucleus[\"HeLa Nucleus\u003cbr/>Command Exec\"]\n    Ribosome[\"HeLa Ribosome\u003cbr/>PTY Harness\"]\n    Enzyme[\"HeLa Enzyme\u003cbr/>Research / Wiki\"]\n    Cytosol[\"HeLa Cytosol\u003cbr/>Browser + Vision\"]\n\n    Agent -- \"stdio JSON-RPC\" --> Mitosis\n    Agent -- \"stdio JSON-RPC\" --> Genome\n    Mitosis \u003c-->|\"Shared Context\"| Genome\n    Mitosis --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol\n    Genome --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol\n```\n\n* **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis`)**: Cellular division and orchestration backbone. Handles dynamic tool routing, prompt decomposition, Needle-backed sequential reasoning (`sequentialthinking` / `agent_run`), and multi-tool planning.\n* **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome`)**: Cellular memory and state backbone. Maintains the persistent SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), entity-relation tracking, session restoration, and project milestones.\n\n---\n\n## 10-MCP Component Taxonomy\n\n| Canonical Identity | Machine ID (`id`) | Technical Source Repo | Scope | Cellular Metaphor & Role |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **HeLa Mitosis** | `hela-mitosis` | [`chaining-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp) | Core | **Orchestrator**: Bundled Needle 3 agent runtime, dynamic routing, 40 bundled prompt templates |\n| **HeLa Genome** | `hela-genome` | [`project-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp) | Core | **State Backbone**: Entity-relation knowledge graph, milestone tracking, memory persistence |\n| **HeLa Membrane** | `hela-membrane` | [`filesystem-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/filesystem-mcp) | Core | **Workspace**: Sandboxed filesystem operations, recursive search, archive extraction |\n| **HeLa Nucleus** | `hela-nucleus` | [`terminal-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/terminal-mcp) | Core | **Execution**: Command execution, subshell isolation, RTK token-optimized execution |\n| **HeLa Ribosome** | `hela-ribosome` | [`menager-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/menager-mcp) | Core | **Process Harness**: Interactive PTY multiplexing, Regex hooks, lifecycle management |\n| **HeLa Enzyme** | `hela-enzyme` | [`researcher-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/researcher-mcp) | Core | **Knowledge**: Zero-key dual-tier web search + arXiv + Google News RSS + Wikipedia synthesis and fact-checking |\n| **HeLa Cytosol** | `hela-cytosol` | [`browser-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/browser-mcp) | Core | **Interaction & vision**: Playwright automation, accessibility/DOM perception, OCR, visual analysis, detection, segmentation, and standalone image analysis |\n| **HeLa Phenotype** | `hela-phenotype` | [`designer-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp) | Specialized | **Design**: UI/UX design tokens, OKLCH color palettes, Tailwind and component synthesis |\n| **HeLa Receptor** | `hela-receptor` | [`scrcpy-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/scrcpy-mcp) | Specialized | **Mobile**: Android device automation, ADB bridge, XML view hierarchy inspection |\n| **HeLa Plastid** | `hela-plastid` | [`ll3m-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/ll3m-mcp) | Specialized | **3D Modeling**: Autonomous Blender procedural modeling, materials, and render pipeline |\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### 1. Prerequisites\n* **Node.js**: use Node 22 LTS for the tested full stack. Individual packages declare their own engine ranges; Receptor requires 22 and Enzyme requires at least 20.18.1.\n* **Git**: `>= 2.30.0`\n* **SQLite**: Genome uses Node bindings; the SQLite CLI is optional for inspection.\n\n### 🤖 Install via AI Agent (One-Click Copy)\n\nIf you are using an AI coding assistant (Cursor, Claude Code, OpenCode, Codex, Antigravity, Windsurf), you can copy and paste this autonomous prompt directly into your agent's chat:\n\n```text\nPlease autonomously install and configure the HeLa Cellular MCP Ecosystem through one profile-aware hela-runtime MCP endpoint:\n\n1. Environment & Harness Detection:\n   Verify Node.js (>= 22.x LTS), Git (>= 2.30.0), and build tools. Identify current client (Cursor, Claude, OpenCode, Antigravity, Zed, Codex).\n\n2. Confirm the profile with the user before installing:\n   Show https://hela.my.id/profiles.html for the quick comparison, and https://hela.my.id/library/docs/profiles.html for detailed persona, workflow, server membership, and prerequisite explanations. Ask which profile best fits the user's work: minimal, dev-workspace, headless-server, research, web-devops, android-testing, 3d-modeling, or all.\n   Wait for the user's choice and call it \u003cconfirmed_profile>. If they do not choose, use minimal. Do not run setup until the profile is confirmed.\n\n3. Clone & Install the Managed Runtime:\n   git clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\n   cd hela-mcp-ecosystem\n   # Windows PowerShell: .\\setup.ps1 ... and .\\setup.ps1 doctor ...\n   # macOS/Linux: node setup.mjs ... (setup.sh remains a compatibility shim)\n   node setup.mjs --profile \u003cconfirmed_profile> --client \u003cdetected_client> --mode runtime --non-interactive\n   node setup.mjs doctor --profile \u003cconfirmed_profile> --mode runtime\n   Safely merge generated client configuration into my active MCP configuration file.\n   Do not deploy or configure each hela-* cell as a separate client server.\n\n4. Synchronize Companion Skills:\n   Run `npm run skills:sync -- --profile \u003cconfirmed_profile>` from the ecosystem checkout. This reads the manifest from `config/inventory.json` and mirrors the selected organelles' bundled skills into the harness skill directory.\n\n5. Load the Runtime Profile Context:\n   - If the selected workspace has no `agent_persona/` and no existing `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `GEMINI.md`, the installer creates the profile-aware HeLa persona there and links the harness entrypoints to it.\n   - If a persona already exists, preserve it; do not overwrite the user's instructions. The runtime profile context remains authoritative for active organelles and tools.\n   - The generated HeLa persona is a distributable synthesis of Azzar's private Frea and Mema personal AI assistant personas: Frea contributes autonomous technical execution, while Mema contributes security and continuity discipline. Their private files, memories, credentials, and identifiers are not copied.\n   - After connecting, read the `hela://profile/context` resource or request the `hela-profile-bootstrap` MCP prompt.\n   - Treat the selected runtime profile as the authority for active organelles and available tools.\n\n6. Final Handshake:\n   Initialize the workspace memory graph through the exposed Genome tool when that organelle is active, and confirm the selected profile and runtime tools.\n```\n\n### 2. Run Diagnostics (`node setup.mjs doctor`)\nClone the source, then select an installation mode:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\nnode setup.mjs --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\n### 3. Interactive Installation & Profile Selection\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs\n```\n\n### 4. Non-Interactive One-Liners\n\n**Full Developer Desktop (Cursor IDE):**\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Headless Linux Server (Claude Desktop / CLI):**\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --profile headless-server --client claude --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Pinned Immutable Release Snapshot:**\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --snapshot v1.11.26 --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Single-endpoint Runtime (one MCP connection for the selected profile):**\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Change an existing installation's mode in place:**\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs mode --prefix /path/to/prefix --mode docker-runtime --client opencode --non-interactive\n```\n\n**Docker cells, Docker runtime, or Nix runtime:**\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode docker --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs --mode docker-runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs --mode nix --profile dev-workspace --client codex --non-interactive\n```\n\nInstalls use private managed prefixes under `${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/hela/installations/`. Existing repositories and live client files are not overwritten. Review and merge the generated client fragment. Use `--prefix` for an explicit location and pass it to doctor. See [installation modes and doctor](docs/installation.md) for HTTP transport, Compose, Nix GC roots, snapshots, optional model assets, prerequisites, and failure recovery.\n\n---\n\n## Supported AI Clients & Output Formats\n\nThe installer writes private fragments inside its prefix, never directly into live client files:\n\n- Cursor, Claude, Gemini, Antigravity: `mcpServers` JSON.\n- OpenCode and Kilo: `mcp` JSON with an installed local launcher, or a runtime HTTP URL.\n- Zed: `context_servers` JSON; preserve unrelated settings when merging.\n- Codex: `[mcp_servers.*]` TOML, with a launcher or HTTP URL.\n- Docker: functional `compose.json` for the built image, not speculative per-cell Dockerfiles or stdio port mappings.\n\n`--client skip` installs without generating a client fragment. Client authentication and GUI acceptance remain separate.\n\n---\n\n## Available Agent Profiles\n\n| Profile ID | Target System | Backbone Included | Servers Included | Focus Area |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `minimal` | Any | Mitosis + Genome | Enzyme | Research and memory without GUI dependencies |\n| `dev-workspace` | Any (GUI recommended) | Mitosis + Genome | Membrane, Nucleus, Ribosome, Enzyme, Phenotype, Cytosol | Full-stack software engineering, testing, web UI |\n| `headless-server` | Headless Linux | Mitosis + Genome | Membrane, Nucleus, Ribosome, Enzyme, Phenotype | Cloud server administration, CI/CD, headless dev |\n| `research` | Headless or GUI | Mitosis + Genome | Enzyme, Membrane, Cytosol | Deep literature research, document synthesis, web crawling |\n| `web-devops` | Headless or GUI | Mitosis + Genome | Membrane, Nucleus, Phenotype, Cytosol | Frontend development, Tailwind styling, DOM verification |\n| `android-testing` | Device connected | Mitosis + Genome | Nucleus, Receptor, Enzyme | Mobile QA testing, ADB automation, APK validation |\n| `3d-modeling` | GUI (Blender installed) | Mitosis + Genome | Plastid, Membrane, Nucleus | 3D procedural modeling, materials, rendering |\n| `all` | Full workstation | Mitosis + Genome | All 10 HeLa MCP servers | Complete full-spectrum AI agent capabilities |\n\n> **Single-endpoint serve mode:** add `--runtime` to a profile command. If no profile is selected, the runtime and installer default to `minimal`; choose `dev-workspace` or another profile explicitly.\n\nProfiles are installation choices, not machine-specific code paths. All eight pass isolated runtime verification with namespacing off/on (**16 checks**), including exact tool/schema parity and safe read-only routing. Lappy's ten-server `all` roster and servy's eight-cell `dev-workspace` setup remain different by design. See [all-profile verification](docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.md); run `npm run test:profiles`, or append `-- --profile \u003cid>` on a partial installation.\n\nRuntime discovery now starts **zero cells**; only invoked cells are started. [Generated profile counts](docs/generated-profile-counts.md) come from the inventory/catalog rather than static client-specific totals. `node setup.mjs doctor --profile \u003cid> --runtime` checks cold catalog parity as well as versions, paths, and revisions.\n\nFor complete persona guides and pipeline specifications, see **[Profiles Catalog](docs/profiles.md)**.\n\n---\n\n## Operational Workflows (Workflows A–F)\n\nThe ecosystem is built for structured multi-server agent workflows:\n* **Workflow A: Autonomous Feature Engineering**: Genome (restore) → Mitosis (decompose) → Membrane (code) → Nucleus (test) → Genome (persist).\n* **Workflow B: Deep Research & Architecture Synthesis**: Enzyme (search) → Cytosol (scrape) → Mitosis (synthesize) → Genome (record) → Membrane (write).\n* **Workflow C: Web Feature with Design & Browser Verification**: Phenotype (tokens) → Membrane (build) → Nucleus (serve) → Cytosol (DOM QA) → Genome (record).\n* **Workflow D: Mobile App Test Automation**: Genome (issue) → Nucleus (adb) → Receptor (drive UI) → Genome (record pass).\n* **Workflow E: Autonomous 3D Asset Creation & Render Pipeline**: Genome (spec) → Mitosis (plan) → Plastid (Blender) → Membrane (save) → Genome (record).\n* **Workflow F: Long-Horizon Complex Refactor with PTY Harness**: Ribosome (PTY) → Nucleus (exec) → Genome (persist).\n\nFor step-by-step tool invocation sequences, schemas, and diagrams, see **[Workflows & Integration Guide](docs/workflows.md)**.\n\n---\n\n## Project Health & Diagnostics\n\n```bash\n# Run system and server diagnostic health check\nnpm run doctor\n# or\nnode setup.mjs doctor\n\n# Run 80-combination client configuration checks (not GUI integration)\nnpm run test:matrix\nnpm run build:all -- --profile \u003cconfirmed_profile>\nnpm run test:all -- --profile \u003cconfirmed_profile>\n\n# Run master integration test suite across all 10 servers (tool counts are feature-dependent)\nnpm test\n\n# Sync bundled agent skills into the harness skills directory\nnpm run skills:sync\n\n# Report skill drift without changing the target\nnpm run skills:check\n```\n\n---\n\n## Documentation Index\n\n* **[Beginner's Step-by-Step Guide](docs/beginner-guide.md)**: 3-minute, zero-code onboarding.\n* **[Keys & Secrets Guide](docs/keys-and-secrets.md)**: API key setup, sign-up URLs, and zero-key offline fallbacks.\n* **[Profiles Reference](docs/profiles.md)**: Full persona reference and capability mappings.\n* **[Prebuilt Agent Skills](docs/prebuilt-skills.md)**: Per-server skill catalog, profile availability, pinning, and synchronization behavior.\n* **[Workflows & Integration Guide](docs/workflows.md)**: Cross-MCP workflow pipelines (Workflows A–F).\n* **[Security & Reliability Audit](docs/security-audit.md)**: Threat model, sandboxing, and isolation standards.\n* **[Reliability Contract](docs/reliability.md)**: Host-to-cell failure model, triage tree, diagrammed boot/supervision paths, and the hourly doctor loop.\n* **[Code Mode Research](docs/code-mode-research.md)**: Measured cost of the current catalog, the execute-and-discover pattern, sandbox options, and an implementation plan.\n* **[Architecture Guide](docs/architecture.md)**: In-depth cellular architecture and communication topologies.\n* **[Troubleshooting Guide](docs/troubleshooting.md)**: Common issues, diagnostic codes, and recovery procedures.\n\n---\n\n## Updating\n\nCheck for a newer snapshot without changing anything, then apply it behind an explicit confirmation:\n\n```sh\nnode setup.mjs update check --prefix \u003cinstallation>\nnode setup.mjs update apply --confirm --prefix \u003cinstallation>\n```\n\nAgents get the same controller through the runtime endpoint: `hela.update.check` (read-only, reports the snapshot delta) and `hela.update.apply` (confirmation-gated, stages through the pinned installer, verifies with doctor, and records a rollback directory). A successful apply returns `restartRequired: true`; restart the runtime or client to load the new build.\n\n## License & Attribution\n\nThis repository — the installer, runtime glue, configuration, and documentation — is distributed under the MIT License.\n\nThe cells it installs are separate upstream projects under their own licences, and six of the ten are copyleft:\n\n| Licence | Cells |\n| --- | --- |\n| GPL-3.0-or-later | Mitosis (`chaining-mcp`), Genome (`project-mcp`), Membrane (`filesystem-mcp`), Ribosome (`menager-mcp`), Enzyme (`researcher-mcp`), Plastid (`ll3m-mcp`) |\n| MIT | Nucleus (`terminal-mcp`), Cytosol (`browser-mcp`), Phenotype (`designer-mcp`), Receptor (`scrcpy-mcp`), Runtime (`hela-runtime`) |\n\nMitosis and Genome are members of every profile, so **every install — `minimal` included — places GPL-3.0-or-later code on disk.** Weigh that against your own licence policy before deploying. Per-cell licences are listed in [current versions](docs/current-versions.md) and on each card in the architecture reference; `config/inventory.json` is the source both are generated from.\n\nUnderlying repositories remain independently copyrighted by their respective authors.\n\nThe HeLa project respectfully recognizes **Henrietta Lacks** and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells.\n"},{"source":"RELEASE_NOTES.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Release Notes","summary":"A housekeeping release. Pins move so that every cell's released commit carries correct lockfile versions, changelogs and tool documentation. No cell changes its version or its…","category":"releases","archived":false,"link":"library/RELEASE_NOTES.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem Release Notes\n\n## v1.11.26 — version records match the code\n\nA housekeeping release. Pins move so that every cell's released commit carries correct\nlockfile versions, changelogs and tool documentation. No cell changes its version or\nits tools.\n\n## v1.11.25 — the agent asks permission before it acts\n\nRuntime 1.4.5 closes a gap in `hela.agent.run`: a task that said not to modify anything\ncould still run a model-written shell command, and \"do not click Delete\" could still\nclick it. One gate now covers retrieval, planning and dispatch. The same release makes\nthe default namespaced tool names work and picks the clock for a time question.\n\nBehaviour changes: a ban on any change a shell could make now closes the shell, and\n\"check that npm test passes\" no longer runs the tests. The gate understands English\nonly. Only the runtime pin changes from 1.11.24.\n\n## v1.11.24 — the agent checks that its answer answers\n\nRuntime 1.4.4 finishes the agent-side contract started in 1.4.2. A run that calls one\ntool and gets a non-failing reply is no longer declared complete when that tool has\nnothing to do with the task: the loop keeps looking, and says `degraded` if nothing\nanswers. Smaller fixes keep a permitted copy when the ban only protects the original,\nretry a timed-out read once, and make truncated plans and early exits report their\nreal status. Only the runtime pin changes from 1.11.23.\n\n## v1.11.23 — workflows can reach the other cells\n\nThe Mitosis workflow tool existed to chain tools across cells, but out of the box\nevery step outside Mitosis failed because nothing told the cell where the runtime\nwas. Runtime 1.4.3 now hands it a private loopback address that only the cell\nknows. The same release carries the second pass on the issue author's re-test:\nMitosis planners that finish inside their budget and say precisely what went wrong\nwith a skill file, an opencode server that agents can actually attach to, memory\ntombstones that expire on purpose and cannot be revived by a stale copy, a\n`read_graph` page small enough to read, and fact checks and news links that say\nwhat they are. Five cells and the runtime are pinned at their merge commits; the\nrest of the snapshot is unchanged from 1.11.22.\n\n## v1.11.22 — a saved browser session loads back as itself\n\nCytosol 3.3.8 fixes the regression the cookie-scoping change introduced: a\ndefault `browser_save_session` stored `[redacted]` and `browser_load_session`\nwrote that text over the live cookie, quietly logging the user out. The session\nfile is owner-only and scoped to the active page, so it now keeps real values;\nredaction applies to what tools return. The same release makes `browser_upload`\nrefuse credential and system paths even under a broad allowed root, and writes\nexports and sessions at mode 0600 atomically. The cytosol pin is the merge\ncommit of browser-mcp#27; the rest of the snapshot is unchanged from 1.11.21.\n\n## v1.11.21 — the agent stops calling a failure a success\n\nRuntime 1.4.2 closes the agent-side half of the response contract. A tool that\nreturns `success: false`, `ok: false` or `isError: true` is now a failed call:\nthe run records it, does not retry the identical call forever, and reports\n`status: \"failed\"` instead of \"single action complete\". The same commit blocks\na write when the task says not to modify anything, and makes the shared\nclassifier read `structuredContent` — including `isError` without a `content`\narray — so `hela.pipe` cannot fail open on that shape again. Five regressions,\n74/74 tests. The runtime is pinned at the merge commit; the rest of the\nsnapshot is unchanged from 1.11.20.\n\n## v1.11.20 — the doctor counted twice\n\nThe host doctor added the runtime's aggregate tool count to the ten cell\ncounts, then compared that inflated number with the live tool list. On a\nbare-name install it warned about hundreds of shadowed tools that did not\nexist. It now compares against the cells alone; the only remaining warnings\nare real ones. Carries 1.11.17–1.11.19.\n\n## v1.11.19 — the summary matches the check\n\n1.11.18 fixed the permanent \"update available\" flag but not the summary\nprinted after an apply, which still listed out-of-profile cells. One-line\nfollow-up; the install itself was already correct.\n\n## v1.11.18 — the update that stops offering itself\n\nA profile installation was comparing its own cells against the full snapshot,\nso a `dev-workspace` host saw `hela-receptor` and `hela-plastid` as newly added\nand stayed \"update available\" forever. The comparison now respects the\ninstalled profile. This release carries 1.11.17 and phenotype 3.3.0.\n\n## v1.11.17 — the whole Ellis design, not a quarter of it\n\nPhenotype 3.3.0 fixes the Ellis UI tools. `get_ellis_ui_template` used to\nreturn `design.md` and `index.html` while that index linked three pages no\ntool could fetch; it now returns all four pages, or one when `page` is set.\n`list_ellis_ui_designs` drops the 24 repeated design documents for a compact\nindex, and both tools emit real newlines. The \"Zero Dependencies\" line in every\ntemplate became an accurate description of the Tailwind Play CDN, Google Fonts\nand hotlinked icons, with the production caveat.\n\n## v1.11.16 — enzyme tool repair\n\nEnzyme 4.3.3 closes the six-tool roll-up. The headline change is that\n`fact_checker` now returns per-source stances and an overall verdict, and\n`content_deduplication` returns the surviving objects with what merged into\nthem instead of a bare list of titles.\n\n## v1.11.15 — synthetic traffic is labelled\n\nCytosol 3.3.7 tags a request served by a mock rule with the pattern, in the\nreadable line and structurally, and exposes the active mock count so a session\nthat forgot to clear them is visible. Closes the last reporting-honesty gap.\n\n## v1.11.14 — one race\n\nRibosome 3.2.3 stops a bare newline (the Enter key's own echo) from consuming\nthe pending trigger echo, which flaked the #21 regression under CI. No\nbehaviour change to the public API.\n\n## v1.11.13 — three roll-ups closed\n\nThe remaining Cytosol, Mitosis and Ribosome roll-ups land together. Cytosol\nreads structured data instead of guessing, Mitosis prompts point at tools that\nexist, and Ribosome stops echoing its own prompt into the session text.\n\n## v1.11.12 — one generator, not two\n\nPhenotype 3.2.2 added its own `docs/TOOLS.md` generator alongside the\necosystem's, so the two formats overwrote each other and CI failed once the\necosystem regenerated the file. The local generator is removed; the ecosystem\ngenerator owns the file, and phenotype's consistency test keeps checking README\ncounts against a live `tools/list`.\n\n## v1.11.11 — tool references caught up\n\nSix cells shipped with a stale `docs/TOOLS.md`: new tools were missing and\ndescriptions were older than the catalog. The files are regenerated from the\nschema catalog and the cells repinned at the docs commits. No behaviour change.\n\n## v1.11.10 — phenotype states only what it can count\n\nPhenotype 3.2.2 replaces hand-written counts with values read from the live\ncatalogues, deletes two rules the code contradicted, and adds a test that\nspawns the server and fails when a published number drifts. Claims that cannot\nbe derived were removed rather than guessed.\n\n## v1.11.9 — genome writes ask first\n\nGenome 3.2.0 makes the destructive gate consistent: `insert_data`, `update_data`\nand `import_data` now need `HELA_GENOME_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=true`, as `delete_data`\nalready did. That is a breaking default for bulk writes and is the first line of\nthe changelog. Also: AWS keys are detected by name, `read_graph` is paged, and a\none-commit repository reports its changes.\n\n## v1.11.8 — boxes say what they measure\n\nCytosol 3.3.5 returns every vision box twice: normalised `bbox` and `bboxPx`\nagainst the reported image size. A field named `bbox` no longer implies pixels,\nwhich is what made a 48 px element report coordinates up to 971.\n\n## v1.11.7 — one wording fix\n\nCytosol 3.3.3 rewrote `browser_search`'s description and lost the word\n\"restricted\", which the ecosystem boundary check asserts. The phrase is back and\nthe cell repinned; behaviour is identical to 3.3.3.\n\n## v1.11.6 — crashes and clocks\n\nTwo cells: the browser's new-tab switch crashed on every call and its catalogue\nsearch returned nothing, while genome wrote a fabricated year into harness\ntimestamps and read `[TAG]` as a date. Both fixed with tests that reproduce the\nreported inputs.\n\n## v1.11.5 — derive the tool count, do not retype it\n\nCytosol gained a tool in 3.3.2, and a hand-written \"75 tools\" in the site copy\nand `llms.txt` failed the docs job. Both the prose and the test now take the\ncount from the generated catalog.\n\n## v1.11.4 — consent, claims and citations\n\nThree places where a tool acted on the caller's behalf without asking: a\nJavaScript `confirm()` was auto-accepted, a mission could be completed by an\nagent that did not hold it, and a citation dropped two of three authors. Dialogs\nnow default to dismiss with an explicit override and a history, missions have\nclaimant-checked transitions with a lease and an explicit requeue, and citations\nfollow each style's author rules.\n\nCarries 1.11.3.\n\n## v1.11.3 — Nix hash for the 1.4.1 runtime\n\nThe 1.4.1 release changed the package version, which is part of the npm deps\nderivation input, so the fixed-output hash stopped matching and the Nix verify\njob failed on 1.11.2. The hash is refreshed and the runtime repinned at\n`14cc13e`; behaviour is identical to 1.11.2.\n\n## v1.11.2 — a result can now say \"degraded\"\n\n`hela.pipe` reports `status: ok | degraded | failed` next to `ok`, each step\ncarries its own status, and a degraded step contributes a reason. A tool that\ntruncates, filters or substitutes sets that marker; the pipe surfaces it instead\nof a clean success. Mitosis 3.1.1 is the first cell to adopt it (`get_skill`).\n\nThe nine false-success instances in #8 were already fixed one by one. This\nrelease turns the pattern into a shape that can be tested, documented in the\nruntime as `docs/response-contract.md`.\n\nCarries 1.11.1; runtime 1.4.1.\n\n## v1.11.1 — the follow-ups 1.11.0 exposed\n\nFour cells needed a second pass: a deletion that survived project sync but not\ncentral sync, sentiment that still read \"never recommend\" as praise, tool\npayloads sitting in a field MCP clients drop, and React components that ignored\ntheir props. Each fix ships with the test that failed without it.\n\nCarries 1.11.0; runtime unchanged.\n\n## v1.11.0 — six cells that stop guessing\n\nEvery fix in this release removes a claim the tool could not support. Trends no\nlonger call result-page counts \"interest\". Planners name the tool they will call\nor say why they cannot. Analysis tools return `null` instead of a constant.\nCookies are the active page's, redacted. A deleted entity stays deleted. A scan\nleaves the database's WAL locks alone, so a write after a scan is not lost.\n\nThe rest are failures that were invisible to callers: an orchestrator reporting\nfailed steps as completed, an agent loop recording `success:false` as `ok:true`,\na `complete` decision with no successful call, dialogs auto-accepted, payloads\ndropped because they sat in a non-standard field. Each now says what happened.\n\nCarries 1.10.10; runtime unchanged at 1.4.0.\n\n## v1.10.10 — phenotype measures what it claims\n\nThree of the five fixes in Phenotype 3.1.0 are about claims the tools could not back up.\n\n`design_audit` flagged inline contrast only when the colour strings were identical, so\nwhite-on-white (1.01:1) and yellow-on-white (1.07:1) passed and the page graded A/96. It now\ncomputes WCAG relative luminance; the same page grades B/75 with measured issues. Palette\nvariants no longer add saturation to greys (an undefined hue was read as red, turning\n`#767676` into `#816a6a`), the High Contrast variant names the surface its AAA claim is\nmeasured against, and the derived focus token is clamped into sRGB instead of being emitted\noutside it.\n\nThe fifth is a surface fix: the five Stitch tools that need the CLI are advertised only when\nthe CLI resolves — 23 tools instead of 28 on a host without it, with\n`HELA_PHENOTYPE_STITCH` to override either way.\n\nCarries 1.10.9; runtime unchanged.\n\n## v1.10.9 — the Nix pin catches up with the runtime fix\n\nBumping the runtime to 1.4.0 changed the npm-deps derivation input, so `flake.nix`'s\nfixed-output hash no longer matched. The runtime repository fixed it; the ecosystem still pinned\nthe older revision, so the installer and the clean-install CI job kept building the stale tree\nand failing at hash verification.\n\nThis release pins the corrected revision. Nothing changes at runtime — the runtime code is\nidentical; only the Nix dependency hash moves.\n\n## v1.10.8 — scoring weights that actually compute\n\n`research_brief` scored candidates without a corpus, so the IDF half of the scorer never\nengaged: every term kept a uniform weight and only morphological folding applied. Enzyme\n4.2.2 builds the corpus from the pages it returned, so a term present in every title\ncontributes almost nothing and a rare one carries the match.\n\nCarries 1.10.7; runtime unchanged.\n\n## v1.10.7 — the enzyme fix that actually ships\n\nThe backoff and feedback work released as 4.1.0/4.2.0 compiled into\n`src/combinedMcp.ts`, while the executable entry (`dist/index.js`) loads the root\n`research-server.ts`, which carries its own `research_brief`. Unit tests passed, the pin\nsaid 4.2.0, and the deployed cell still behaved like 4.0.9 — no backoff, no feedback\nhandle, no spool writes.\n\nEnzyme 4.2.1 ports all three into the entry production runs, and corrects the trigger:\nbackoff now fires when the **ranked** result is empty, since pages that all fall below the\nrelevance floor still read as \"No results from any source\".\n\nFound by verifying the deployed cell end to end after the previous release, not by the test\nsuite, which exercises the other entry. Carries 1.10.6; runtime unchanged.\n\n## v1.10.6 — pipe stops lying about success\n\n`hela_pipe` exists so intermediate results never pass through the model, which means the\nmodel cannot see a step failing — the runtime is the only thing positioned to notice, and it\ndid not. A three-step chain with a failed middle step returned `ok: true` and then fed the\nerror string to the next tool, which produced a plausible keyword analysis of `ENOENT`.\n\nRuntime 1.4.0 makes the pipe fail closed: `ok` reflects every step, `failedStep` names the\nfirst failure, `onError` defaults to `stop` (with `continue` for deliberate best-effort), each\nresult carries its own `ok`, and `{{prev}}` is unset after a failure instead of carrying the\nerror onward. The next step's payload is now the parsed result rather than the MCP envelope.\n\nThe default change is breaking on purpose — a caller checking `ok` was getting an answer that\nwas wrong. Cells carry forward from 1.10.5.\n\n## v1.10.5 — membrane answers match what happened\n\nFour tools in the filesystem cell returned a shape that promised more than the code did.\nRanged reads split UTF-8 characters and lost them at every chunk edge; extraction dropped\npath-traversal entries silently and called the remainder a success; `watch_file` started a\nwatcher whose events were discarded on return; `compare_files` measured characters where the\nrest of the cell measures bytes.\n\nMembrane 3.0.6 fixes the four: boundary-snapped ranges with `startByte`/`endByte`, extraction\nthat lists every skipped entry and why, buffered watch events collected by polling (and a\nwait for chokidar's initial scan), and bytes plus a separate `characters` field.\n\nCarries 1.10.4. Runtime unchanged at 1.3.7.\n\n## v1.10.4 — the priors loop is closed\n\nThe adaptive ranking mechanism had been built and never fed. Enzyme observed which source\nproduced a result a caller actually used, but could not write the table; genome owned it and\nnothing called the writer.\n\nEnzyme 4.2.0 now appends one record per observed follow-up to a spool file, with the display\nrank so position bias stays visible. Genome 3.0.7 drains that spool into `source_feedback` on\nthe next `search_priors` or `search_feedback` call: claimed by rename so two processes cannot\nimport the same line twice, malformed lines skipped, failed inserts put back for a later pass.\n\nThe header still reports `priors: static` until five samples accumulate per class — that is\nthe system being honest, and now it can stop being honest.\n\nCarries mitosis 3.0.9, nucleus 3.0.7 and enzyme's query/scoring work. Runtime unchanged.\n\n## v1.10.3 — Mitosis can see the organism again\n\nUnder `--mode runtime` the cells sit behind one endpoint, so Mitosis's config-based peer\ndiscovery found none of them and `validate_tool_chain` rejected every real tool. It now also\nreads the runtime's generated `config/tool-catalog.json`, which already knows each cell's\ntools and is revision-validated at build time. Verified against the installed catalog: ten\ncells, 299 tools.\n\nAvailability is three-state rather than two: a discovered server missing a tool is an error,\na server that was not discovered is a warning (\"could not be verified\"), and a run with no\ncatalog produces one warning instead of one error per step. The previous behaviour failed in\nthe direction that discards correct plans.\n\nCarries the nucleus 3.0.7 and enzyme 4.1.0 fixes from 1.10.2. Runtime unchanged at 1.3.7.\n\n## v1.10.2 — the cells stop reporting quiet failures\n\nTwo cell releases, both about a step that failed while looking like it succeeded.\n\nNucleus 3.0.7 runs local commands from a script file rather than through `sh -c`, so a\n`pkill -f` in a compound command no longer matches and kills its own wrapper (then reports\nexit 1 with empty output), and `pgrep -f` no longer counts the wrapper as a live process.\nIts RTK summariser now refuses a summary below 5% of the source, keeps the original output\nand names it, and only treats `Error`/`warning` at line start as log mode.\n\nEnzyme 4.1.0 reshapes a query once when every source returns nothing and says so in the\nheader, since Google CSE over-constrains on long natural-language questions. Scoring folds\n`tool`/`tools`-style variants and weights terms by rarity among the candidates, and each\nbrief now carries the `query_class`/`source` handle needed to record feedback — with the\nexact genome call, including when a follow-up tool opens a URL the brief returned.\n\nRuntime unchanged at 1.3.7.\n\n## v1.10.1 — doctor diagnostics for uninstalled and non-default prefixes\n\nTwo lookups produced a report that blamed the host for what was really a path miss. Doctor\nnow says `not installed` instead of `Not checked`, and when every component is missing it\nprints the paths it resolved along with the install-then-diagnose commands. Separately, an\ninstallation made with `--runtime` lives under `\u003cprofile>-runtime`, but doctor only checked\n`\u003cprofile>-individual`; it now scans for sibling `\u003cprofile>-*` installations and names the\n`--mode` or `--prefix` needed to diagnose each one, in human and JSON output alike.\n\nNo cell changes: revisions and versions pin unchanged from 1.10.0.\n\n## v1.10.0 — host-layer doctor and a single MCP entry point\n\nThe doctor now covers the layer it previously ignored: the wiring between client\napplications and the runtime. `node scripts/doctor.mjs --hosts` (also `npm run doctor:hosts`)\nchecks the interpreter floor derived from cell `engines`, whether `nodejs` is still marked\nmanual for apt, the gate file the launcher reads, credential-file modes and key presence,\nall six client configs, backup exposure, and a live `initialize`/`tools/list` handshake.\nFailures are actionable lines; `--repair` re-installs the pinned node package, re-asserts\nthe manual mark, and fixes credential-file and backup modes. Exit codes stay 0/1/2.\n\n`scripts/host-entry.sh` is now the canonical client entry point, installed as\n`~/.local/bin/hela-runtime-mcp`. It resolves the interpreter at spawn time, refuses to\nstart below the required major, sources one 0600 credentials file, and logs failures\nbecause MCP hosts swallow child stderr. It is PATH-independent: every external command is\nabsolute, so it can report a stripped environment instead of dying inside it.\n\n[The reliability contract](docs/reliability.md) documents the resulting chain with eight\ndiagrams. Sources are Mermaid (`assets/diagrams/*.mmd`) and the rendered SVG next to them;\nthe library pages embed the SVG because the document renderer carries `markdown-it`\nwithout a Mermaid plugin and escapes raw HTML.\n\nCredential handling also changed: every secret found in client-config backups was copied\ninto mema-vault before those backups were deleted, so `--hosts` reports a clean backup\nsurface instead of a permanent warning.\n\n## v1.9.6 — pinned modern web guidance\n\nHeLa Phenotype 3.0.3 now bundles Google's Modern Web Guidance agent skill. The skill, CLI package (`1.0.0`), upstream source commit, and guidance corpus (`2026_11_09-5bcc021b`) are pinned. Profiles that include Phenotype receive the skill through the normal skill-sync workflow; the command-line lookup needs Node.js 22+ and outbound network access.\n\nThe new [prebuilt-skills catalog](docs/prebuilt-skills.md) maps every bundled skill to its server and all eight profiles, and documents source pins, synchronization, and update handling. The coordinated v1.9.6 snapshot is immutable; `latest-stable` now resolves to it. v1.9.5 and older snapshots remain unchanged.\n\n## v1.8.0 — dedicated Cytosol image analysis\n\nHeLa Cytosol now accepts a dedicated local image path, HTTP(S) image URL, or base64 image directly through `browser_image_analyze`. The tool returns Mirage VLM scene understanding, OCR, object detection, segmentation, spatial relations, and schema-based decisions without launching a browser page. Image inputs are bounded and protected by workspace, content-type, redirect, and SSRF checks.\n\n## v1.7.0 — Cytosol Mirage visual intelligence\n\nHeLa Cytosol now includes first-class local visual intelligence through Cactus Mirage. The `browser_visual_analyze` tool can describe browser screenshots, detect objects, read text, return segmentation data and relations, and extract values against a schema. The Cactus runtime is managed per Cytosol installation while Mirage model weights remain lazy and are cached on first use.\n\n## v1.6.4 — stable snapshot alias correction\n\nThe `latest-stable` snapshot now resolves to the coordinated release pins and\nversions published in the 1.6.3 cell/runtime release set.\n\n## v1.6.3 — cross-platform maintenance entrypoints\n\nThe installer and maintenance workflow is now Node-first. Use `node setup.mjs`,\n`setup.ps1`, or `setup.cmd` for installation and doctor checks; `update.ps1` and\n`update.cmd` provide the corresponding Windows update path. The Bash entrypoints\nremain compatibility shims.\n\nProfile-scoped build, test, clean, and update operations now run through portable\nNode scripts. Runtime Needle discovery also recognizes `needle3.exe` and\n`needle.exe` automatically. Native Windows cold-install acceptance is still\ntracked separately from this source-level portability work.\n\n## v1.6.2 — Plastid and Receptor dependency refresh\n\nPlastid moves to `aa43877` and Receptor to `1b21cb7`, adopting the lockfile updates that had accumulated on their default branches. Both carried open advisories against `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`; the refreshed locks resolve them, and `npm audit` is clean for Plastid's runtime dependencies while Receptor reports none.\n\nPlastid's manifest also declared `\"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk\": \"latest\"`. That is why its lockfile drifted without a reviewable diff; it is now `^1.32.2`. Builds and the existing suites pass — 12 Plastid regressions and 123 Receptor tests — and no cell version changed.\n\nExposure was limited: the SDK advisories concern HTTP transports, body-size limits, and OAuth flows, while Plastid and Receptor run over stdio. Updating still removes the drift and the noise. Hardware, the Blender bridge, GUI clients, and providers remain separate checks.\n\n## v1.6.1 — switch deployment modes in place\n\n`./setup.sh mode --prefix PATH --mode MODE` converts an existing managed installation to another execution mechanism without a second prefix or a data migration. All five modes are valid targets. Profile, snapshot, workspace, transport, port, and the per-cell `data/` directories carry over; host-mode switches reuse packages already built at the pinned revision with a clean worktree, and Docker modes build or drop their image and `compose.json` accordingly.\n\nThe command runs the same verification as a fresh installation and generates a new client fragment instead of editing live client settings. Failed verification restores the previous manifest and writes a pending marker so doctor reports the degraded state. Pending or unmanaged prefixes are refused, HTTP targets require a runtime mode, and re-selecting the current mode is an error.\n\nEnzyme stays at 4.0.0 and the runtime at 1.2.0; every cell pin is unchanged from 1.6.0. Tests cover host-family round-trips, transport guards, pending markers, and Docker switches.\n\n## v1.6.0 — consistent Enzyme search input\n\nPin Enzyme source `eb98982`. `google_search` now takes `query`, matching every other single text search in the cell. The former `q` argument is removed, so a client that still sends it gets an MCP validation error instead of a silent translation; requests that send both fields are rejected as well. Batch tools keep `queries`, and provider HTTP parameters are unaffected internally.\n\nBoth Enzyme entrypoints share one strict schema: the query is trimmed and must be non-empty, `num` is an integer from 1 to 10, and unknown fields are refused before any provider call. Enzyme's contract tests cover discovery, dispatch, rejection of obsolete input, and provider failure for the shipped and modular servers; 32 contract tests and 111 total Enzyme tests pass with a clean TypeScript build and no lint errors. Enzyme moves to 4.0.0 because this is a breaking argument change.\n\nThe catalog, generated documentation, and profile counts are rebuilt from the new schema. Counts stay at 24 Enzyme tools, so profile totals do not change. Other cells, the runtime, and every snapshot from v1.0.0 to v1.5.3 are untouched.\n\n## v1.5.3 — canonical relation ordering\n\nPin Genome source `9ba1d0e`. Relation links now render in one canonical order regardless of database insertion order, preventing relation-order-only conflicts after a lossless repair. All 19 suites / 264 tests pass; the TypeScript build and zero-warning source lint pass. The published 1.5.2 snapshot remains unchanged, as do all other cell/runtime pins and package versions.\n\n## v1.5.2 — replicated vault convergence\n\nPin Genome source `c32ae94`: replicated notes keep the earliest creation timestamp and latest update timestamp, while incoming observation supersets can retain their order without losing database-only facts. The explicit programmatic repair API archives both originals, respects snapshot/lock checks, and permits rejecting a plan before publication. Normal CLI sync does not choose conflict winners automatically.\n\nGenome's 19 suites / 263 tests and TypeScript build pass, including regressions reproducing the previous timestamp/order loops and validating SQL bindings, JSON rows and tool arguments. Source lint reports zero errors and warnings, covers top-level files and fails on warnings. Package versions remain 3.0.0 for cells and 1.2.0 for runtime; this source snapshot records the exact fixed Genome commit. Other pins and historical snapshots are unchanged. Real vault repair and MCP reconnection require separate, backed-up operational verification; no private vault data is published here.\n\n## v1.5.1 — current documentation and bilingual website\n\nDocumentation-only refresh: all ten cells remain **3.0.0**, and runtime remains **1.2.0**. The new source snapshot pins the documentation commits without rewriting v1.0.0–v1.5.0. Plastid and Receptor documentation was merged into their default branches without discarding unrelated dependency updates; the snapshot deliberately pins the documentation-only parents, not those dependency changes.\n\nThe EN/ID website now loads generated versions, canonical tool names, and profile metadata. Configuration previews use real repository directories and Codex `mcp_servers` sections. Source guides cover all eight profiles, cold discovery, optional integrations, safe backup/restore, and the limits of current verification. Generated private configurations may contain supplied credentials; committed examples are regenerated with a sanitized environment.\n\nDocumentation checks cover 74 regression cases plus local links/anchors and structured metadata; the CLI matrix covers 80 combinations. Browser verification covers 64 visible selections per language and all 16 pages at desktop/mobile widths. These are documentation/UI checks, not new authenticated ACP, provider, Android, or Blender acceptance. Publication does not restart the runtime or change deferred access controls, credentials, databases, or vault conflicts.\n\n## v1.5.0 — on-demand runtime and canonical discovery\n\nRuntime **1.2.0** starts cells only when invoked, shares concurrent startup attempts, and discovers schemas without spawning cells. Revision/build/config-aware caches reject stale entries; legacy/corrupt caches fall back to a validated generated catalog. Unknown calls no longer start unrelated cells. Standalone servers, all eight profiles, and the `dev-workspace` default are preserved.\n\nThe canonical inventory drives config generation, doctor/version/profile checks, schema catalogs, and generated documentation counts. Doctor can verify exact runtime discovery with no cell startup via `--runtime`. A new immutable v1.5.0 snapshot pins runtime `9c92dbf`; older versioned snapshots are unchanged.\n\nVerification: **32** runtime regressions on both Linux architectures, **16** all-profile namespace-mode checks, **80** client configurations, four probe regressions, and four inventory regressions. Cold/warm measurements are recorded, not treated as universal benchmarks. See [implementation and verification scope](docs/on-demand-runtime.md). ACP authentication, external client reloads, gateway retirement, vault conflict review, hardware/provider integrations, and deferred security remain separate work.\n\n**Servy rollout verified:** public/local discovery exposes 236 tools without starting any cells; the first safe Nucleus call starts only Nucleus. Deployed doctor, catalog, and revision checks pass. Fresh backups and rollback material are retained, and all 633 existing conflict archive files are unchanged. See the [rollout evidence](docs/on-demand-runtime.md#historical-servy-rollout--runtime-120).\n\n## v1.4.0 — memory safety and runtime verification\n\nThe v1.4.0 source snapshot pins the published cell revisions and runtime 1.1.0. Older versioned snapshots are unchanged. `latest-stable.json` tracks this source snapshot, not a guarantee that every optional integration or GUI session has been exercised.\n\n- Genome: 19 suites / 242 tests with clean lockfile dependencies, including WAL-safe backups, isolated restore, vault conflicts, repeated imports, and exclusive mission claims.\n- Runtime: 20 isolated fault tests covering hung children, SIGTERM/SIGKILL, actual process exit, one restart, profile/cache boundaries, and shutdown cleanup.\n- Nix: real Node 22 package builds on x86_64-linux and native aarch64-linux, using pinned inputs and fixed-output npm dependencies.\n- Dependencies: clean-install Zod 4 build fixes in Genome and Enzyme; Enzyme's 79 tests and Phenotype's 74 tests pass.\n- Ecosystem: ten isolated standalone MCP handshakes and 80 configuration-generation combinations pass. Codex TOML now uses `mcp_servers` and is accepted by the native parser. These are not authenticated GUI tests.\n- Discovery: 290 standalone tools with LLM and model-backed agent features disabled; the same isolated `dev-workspace` runtime exposes 230 tools including `hela.pipe`. Feature-enabled deployments can expose additional Mitosis tools and the optional agent alias; query `tools/list` rather than assuming a static total.\n\nKnown limits: Zed/Codex ACP authenticated-session verification remains pending; duplicate ownership warnings do not establish a connection failure. Existing live vault conflicts remain archived for deliberate review. Android/Blender discovery is not hardware integration. Endpoint authentication and credential rotation remain operator work, not changes included in this release.\n\n**Servy deployment verified (2026-11-06):** the rooted ARM Nix package serves the eight-cell `dev-workspace` profile under systemd. Local/public discovery returns 236 live tools, versus 230 in feature-disabled probes; all three Genome mission tools are present. Deployed doctor and strict version/pin checks pass. Existing conflict archives and unrelated client processes were preserved. See [deployment evidence and remaining client/gateway work](docs/deployment-2026-11-06.md).\n\n**All-profile follow-up (2026-11-06):** all eight profiles pass runtime startup/discovery, exact tool/schema parity, and safe read-only routing in both namespacing modes (**16/16** checks). All ten standalone cells plus runtime pass strict version/pin verification; the 80 client-configuration combinations and four probe regressions pass. Machine selections and release snapshots are unchanged. See [profile verification](docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.md).\n\n## Historical v1.0.0 notes\n\nWe are proud to announce the official **v1.0.0** release of the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** — a modular, immortal cellular AI tooling stack built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), engineered by Azzar.\n\n---\n\n## Architectural Vision & Metaphor\n\nThe **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** is named in respectful recognition of **Henrietta Lacks** (1920–1951) and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells. Using a **cellular biology metaphor**, the ecosystem organizes 10 specialized MCP servers into a harmonious organism:\n\n* **Cellular Specialization**: Distinct organelles (Nucleus, Membrane, Enzyme, Ribosome, Plastid) perform targeted domains.\n* **Deterministic Immortality**: Environments can be snapshot-pinned (`config/snapshots/v1.0.0.json`) and restored cleanly on any machine.\n* **Repository Independence**: Underlying source repositories, technical package names, and Git commit histories remain 100% autonomous.\n\n---\n\n## Key Highlights & Milestones Delivered\n\n### 1. Dual-Backbone Architecture\nEvery profile is grounded by two core backbones:\n* **HeLa Mitosis (`chaining-mcp`)**: Cellular division and orchestration backbone. Bundled Needle 3 agent runtime (plan → execute → observe, offline-first), Needle-backed sequential reasoning (`sequentialthinking`, `agent_run`), task decomposition, and 40 domain prompts. No external sequential-thinking server.\n* **HeLa Genome (`Project-Guardian-mcp-server`)**: Living memory and state backbone. SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), entity-relation tracking, cross-session context restoration, and decision memory.\n\n### 2. 10-MCP Component Taxonomy\n* **HeLa Membrane** (`filesystem-mcp-server`): Sandboxed workspace filesystem operations and search.\n* **HeLa Nucleus** (`terminal-mcp-server`): Isolated command execution with RTK token optimization.\n* **HeLa Ribosome** (`menager-mcp-server`): Interactive PTY session multiplexing and Regex hook engine.\n* **HeLa Enzyme** (`research-assistant-mcp-server`): Unified Google Custom Search + Wikipedia caching.\n* **HeLa Cytosol** (`Browser-Agent`): Playwright browser automation and DOM accessibility perception.\n* **HeLa Phenotype** (`the-designer`): UI/UX design tokens, OKLCH palettes, and Tailwind component synthesis.\n* **HeLa Receptor** (`scrcpy-mcp`): Android mobile automation via ADB bridge.\n* **HeLa Plastid** (`ll3m-agent`): Autonomous Blender procedural 3D modeling and rendering.\n\n### 3. Diagnostic Health Checker (`setup.sh doctor`)\nComprehensive automated diagnostic tool auditing host prerequisites (Node.js >=18, Git, SQLite3), server builds, commit pins, stdio JSON-RPC smoke readiness (\u003c300ms), external runtimes (Playwright, ADB, Blender), and zero-key offline fallback modes.\n\n### 4. 70-Combination Multi-Client Matrix (`npm run test:matrix`)\nFull verification across all 10 client backends (Cursor, Claude, Gemini, Antigravity, OpenCode, Kilo, Zed, Codex TOML, Docker Compose, Skip) and 7 agent profiles with 100% schema accuracy.\n\n### 5. Master Integration Test Suite (`npm test`)\nAutomated stdio JSON-RPC handshake and tools discovery across all 10 servers (**295 active tools discovered and validated in 2.5s**).\n\n### 6. Interactive Documentation Website (`docs/site/`)\nModern, responsive documentation landing site featuring:\n* Interactive live Client & Profile Configurator.\n* Video showcases & embedded YouTube demos.\n* Uploaded Asciinema terminal casts.\n* Searchable 10-MCP Cellular Taxonomy directory.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\n./setup.sh doctor\n./setup.sh\n```\n\n---\n\n## Documentation\n\n* [Beginner's Step-by-Step Guide](docs/beginner-guide.md)\n* [Architecture Guide](docs/architecture.md)\n* [Cross-MCP Workflows A–F](docs/workflows.md)\n* [Security & Reliability Audit](docs/security-audit.md)\n* [Troubleshooting Guide](docs/troubleshooting.md)\n* [Keys & Secrets Matrix](docs/keys-and-secrets.md)\n\n---\n\n## License & Attribution\n\nDistributed under the MIT License. Developed by Azzar.\n\nThe HeLa project respectfully recognizes **Henrietta Lacks** and the enduring scientific legacy of HeLa cells.\n"},{"source":"docs/architecture.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Architecture","summary":"This document provides a comprehensive architectural reference for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem, detailing its cellular design principles, dual-backbone topology, component…","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/architecture.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem Architecture\n\nThis document provides a comprehensive architectural reference for the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**, detailing its cellular design principles, dual-backbone topology, component taxonomies, and cross-MCP communication mechanisms.\n\nCurrent package versions and feature-dependent tool counts are generated in [current versions](current-versions.md). Each of the ten MCP cells has its own version; the ecosystem snapshot and optional runtime have independent versions.\n\n![Blotcat acting as a conductor in front of a modular system of interconnected server blocks](../assets/blotcat-architecture.jpg)\n\n## Organelle video explainers\n\nShort video introductions to all ten organelles are embedded in the [Architecture reference page](site/architecture.html#organelle-videos). The [complete ecosystem explainer](https://youtu.be/1qv5kD3qApQ) covers their roles and collaboration.\n\n* [Mitosis — Orchestration & Routing](https://youtu.be/SV9HRh5iFWY)\n* [Genome — Memory & Context](https://youtu.be/3w39n0Dtuy8)\n* [Membrane — Filesystem Access](https://youtu.be/SIO7em0XLA8)\n* [Nucleus — Command Execution](https://youtu.be/Msy-_oR_y68)\n* [Ribosome — Persistent Workers](https://youtu.be/0pScB7HlMtc)\n* [Enzyme — Research & Retrieval](https://youtu.be/fnIy0RzaevM)\n* [Cytosol — Browser Automation](https://youtu.be/BvW25O6v-8U)\n* [Phenotype — Design Tokens & UI Systems](https://youtu.be/8k6fpEBT3cY)\n* [Receptor — Android Device Automation](https://youtu.be/5cy9ZjyLic8)\n* [Plastid — Procedural 3D Modeling](https://youtu.be/7TMHqKB2DR8)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Philosophy: The Cellular Metaphor\n\nThe **HeLa MCP Ecosystem** is architected around a cellular biology metaphor, respectfully recognizing **Henrietta Lacks** (1920–1951) and the immortal legacy of HeLa cells in biomedical research:\n\n1. **Cellular Specialization**: In biological systems, organelles perform distinct biochemical functions while cooperating as a unified organism. In the HeLa ecosystem, servers act as specialized cellular components (Nucleus, Membrane, Enzyme, Ribosome, Plastid) coordinated by a dual backbone.\n2. **Reproducible source selection**: Commit-pinned snapshots under `config/snapshots/` select known revisions. Platform dependencies and external integrations still require separate validation.\n3. **Repository Autonomy**: Underlying repositories, Git histories, and package dependencies remain completely autonomous and uncoupled.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 4-Tier Naming Architecture\n\nTo balance brand clarity, backwards compatibility, and technical provenance, the ecosystem enforces a 4-tier naming model:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    T1[\"Tier 1: Public Identity\u003cbr/>HeLa Genome\"]\n    T2[\"Tier 2: Machine ID\u003cbr/>hela-genome\"]\n    T3[\"Tier 3: Technical Repo\u003cbr/>project-mcp\"]\n    T4[\"Tier 4: Immutable Commit\u003cbr/>72bca15ebe...\"]\n\n    T1 --> T2 --> T3 --> T4\n```\n\n1. **Tier 1 (Public Identity)**: Clean, uniform human-facing persona name (e.g. *HeLa Mitosis*, *HeLa Genome*, *HeLa Membrane*).\n2. **Tier 2 (Machine Identifier)**: Stable hyphenated identifier (`hela-*`) used in configuration files (`mcpServers`), CLI flags, and profile definitions.\n3. **Tier 3 (Technical Source Repo)**: Independent Git repository name on GitHub maintaining upstream authorship and commit history.\n4. **Tier 4 (Immutable Commit Pin)**: Exact SHA-1 hash recorded in `config/snapshots/` ensuring reproducible builds.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dual-Backbone Coordination Topology\n\nEvery deployed profile in the ecosystem is grounded by a dual backbone:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Client[\"AI Client / Orchestrator\"]\n\n    Mitosis[\"HeLa Mitosis\u003cbr/>(hela-mitosis)\u003cbr/>- Dynamic Routing\u003cbr/>- Step Reasoning\u003cbr/>- Task Decomposition\u003cbr/>- Prompt Catalog\"]\n    Genome[\"HeLa Genome\u003cbr/>(hela-genome)\u003cbr/>- Knowledge Graph\u003cbr/>- Living SQLite DB\u003cbr/>- Entity / Relations\u003cbr/>- Milestones & State\"]\n\n    Membrane[\"HeLa Membrane\u003cbr/>(Files)\"]\n    Nucleus[\"HeLa Nucleus\u003cbr/>(Exec)\"]\n    Ribosome[\"HeLa Ribosome\u003cbr/>(PTY)\"]\n    Enzyme[\"HeLa Enzyme\u003cbr/>(Search)\"]\n    Cytosol[\"HeLa Cytosol\u003cbr/>(Browser)\"]\n    Phenotype[\"HeLa Phenotype\u003cbr/>(Design)\"]\n\n    Client -- \"stdio JSON-RPC\" --> Mitosis & Genome\n    Mitosis \u003c-->|\"Shared Memory\"| Genome\n    Mitosis & Genome --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol & Phenotype\n```\n\n* **HeLa Mitosis (Routing & Planning Backbone)**: Bundled Needle 3 agent runtime (plan → execute → observe with recorded AgentState), suggests multi-step execution paths, Needle-backed sequential analysis, and serves 40 curated domain prompts. Offline-first; OpenRouter is an escalation backend only.\n* **HeLa Genome (State & Memory Backbone)**: Maintains the living SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), restores cross-session context, records task states, and manages project milestones.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Component Taxonomy\n\n### 4.1 Core Backbone Servers\n\n#### 1. HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Orchestration, dynamic routing, and Needle-backed sequential reasoning.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `agent_run`, `sequentialthinking`, `analyze_with_sequential_thinking`, `analyze_tools`, `generate_route_suggestions`, `llm_decompose_task`, `llm_suggest_route`, `workflow_orchestrator`, `workflow_status`, `workflow_cancel`, `list_skills`, `search_skills`, `get_skill`, `suggest_skill_chain`, plus prompt/resource, time, and validation tools.\n* **Zero-Key Behavior**: The bundled Needle 3 agent runs fully offline. Without `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, escalation hands control back to the calling agent (`handoff: true`) with the full state trail — never a fabricated answer. `brainstorming` is the one exception: it needs a generative model and fails honestly without a key.\n\n#### 2. HeLa Genome (`hela-genome` / `project-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Living memory graph, entity-relation-observation state tracking.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `memory_edit` (entity/relation/observation CRUD), `read_graph`, `search_nodes`, `get_session_context`, `sync_central_memory`, `security_scan`, and the three mission tools `create_mission`, `list_missions`, `update_mission`.\n* **Storage**: Embedded SQLite database (`memory.db`) with FTS5 full-text indexing.\n\n---\n\n### 4.2 Core Capability Servers\n\n#### 3. HeLa Membrane (`hela-membrane` / `filesystem-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Sandboxed workspace filesystem operations and search.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `read_file`, `write_file`, `find_files`, `search_in_files`, `copy_file`, `move_file`, `delete_file`, `archive_files`.\n\n#### 4. HeLa Nucleus (`hela-nucleus` / `terminal-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Isolated command execution and RTK token optimization.\n* **Entrypoint**: `build/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `execute_command`, `transfer_file`, `terminal_ls`, `terminal_grep`, `terminal_cat`, `terminal_session_close`, `terminal_session_list`.\n\n#### 5. HeLa Ribosome (`hela-ribosome` / `menager-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Polyglot interactive PTY session multiplexing and Regex hook engine.\n* **Entrypoint**: `build/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `session_spawn`, `session_write`, `session_read`, `session_hook`, `session_close`, `session_list`, `session_prune`.\n\n#### 6. HeLa Enzyme (`hela-enzyme` / `researcher-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Unified research platform: zero-key dual-tier web search + arXiv + Google News RSS + Wikipedia caching.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `google_search`, `wikipedia_search`, `wikipedia_get`, `content_summarizer`, `fact_checker`, `keyword_extraction`.\n\n#### 7. HeLa Cytosol (`hela-cytosol` / `browser-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Playwright-based browser automation with accessibility and DOM perception, screenshot verification, OCR, and dedicated image analysis through the local Mirage VLM. It can analyze a standalone image without launching a browser page.\n* **Entrypoint**: `src/server.js`\n* **Tools**: `browser_navigate`, `browser_screenshot`, `browser_click`, `browser_type`, `browser_get_content`, `browser_visual_analyze`, `browser_image_analyze`, `browser_ocr`, and `browser_extract_schema`.\n\n---\n\n### 4.3 Specialized Capability Servers\n\n#### 8. HeLa Phenotype (`hela-phenotype` / `designer-mcp`)\n* **Role**: UI/UX design tokens, OKLCH palettes, and Tailwind component synthesis.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js`\n* **Tools**: `design_tokens`, `palette`, `design_audit`, `generate_template`, `logo_workflow`.\n\n#### 9. HeLa Receptor (`hela-receptor` / `scrcpy-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Android mobile device control via ADB bridge.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/server.js`\n* **Tools**: `start_session`, `ui_dump`, `ui_find_element`, `tap`, `swipe`, `input_text`, `screenshot`.\n\n#### 10. HeLa Plastid (`hela-plastid` / `ll3m-mcp`)\n* **Role**: Autonomous 3D procedural modeling and rendering in Blender.\n* **Entrypoint**: `dist/index.js` (under `brain/`)\n* **Tools**: `generate_modeling_plan`, `execute_blender_code`, `render_output`, `save_blend`, `get_fast_feedback`.\n\n---\n\n### 4.4 Canonical Tool Boundaries (v1.2.1)\n\nSeveral organelles expose overlapping capabilities. Descriptions and the Mitosis capability router now enforce a single canonical choice per intent, keeping a restricted alternative only for its niche:\n\n| Intent | Canonical | Restricted alternative | Restricted use |\n|--------|-----------|------------------------|----------------|\n| Web search | `hela-enzyme:google_search` | `hela-cytosol:browser_search` | Visual browser workflows only |\n| Content extraction | `hela-enzyme:extract_content` | `hela-cytosol:browser_get_content` | Pages requiring client-side JavaScript |\n| Local file read | `hela-membrane:read_file` | `hela-nucleus:terminal_cat` | Remote SSH targets only (`host` required) |\n| Memory-graph inspection | `hela-genome:execute_sql` / `query_data` | `hela-ribosome:opencode_db` | OpenCode session database only |\n\nTool names are unique ecosystem-wide after the Phase 1 rename, so the router matches boundaries by tool name without needing a server field.\n\n---\n\n### 4.5 HeLa Runtime (optional daemon, `hela-runtime`)\n\nThe optional runtime supervises child MCP processes behind one stdio or Streamable HTTP endpoint. Its generated catalog exposes selected-profile schemas without starting cells. The first invocation starts only its target; concurrent first calls share startup. Unknown tool names do not trigger an all-cell scan.\n\n- **Source:** https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-runtime\n- **Entry:** `hela-runtime/dist/index.js`\n- **Serve mode:** add `--runtime` to any profile, for example `node setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime`. Runtime is not a profile; an omitted profile defaults to `minimal`.\n- **Env:** `HELA_ECOSYSTEM_ROOT`, `HELA_PROFILE`, `HELA_HTTP_PORT`, and `HELA_NAMESPACE_TOOLS` — namespacing is on by default, and `HELA_NAMESPACE_TOOLS=0` restores bare cell-local tool names for clients that pin them.\n- **Features:** true on-demand ignition for every selected cell, fingerprinted schema caches, bounded crash/timeout recovery, namespacing on by default (a bare `read_file` cannot shadow the host's), runtime-internal `hela.pipe`, and HTTP `/healthz` liveness.\n- **Without it:** users keep the individual servers selected by their profile and can use `hela-mitosis:workflow_orchestrator` for cross-cell chaining.\n- **Details:** [on-demand startup, cache invalidation and deployment evidence](on-demand-runtime.md).\n\n#### Response contract: ok, degraded, failed\n\nA tool result has three states, and the state is part of the shape. `hela.pipe` returns `status: \"ok\" | \"degraded\" | \"failed\"`, each step carries its own `status`, and a degraded step contributes a reason to a top-level `degraded` array. `ok` stays true for a degraded result; `isError` is reserved for failures. A cell marks degradation with `degraded`, `partial`, `truncated`, `status: \"degraded\"` or `warnings` on its JSON payload — the object form is preferred because the contract requires a reason. Three rules follow: an assertion needs evidence, a failed payload is never piped onward as input, and degradation must be visible in the shape rather than only in prose. The full contract is in the runtime repository, `docs/response-contract.md`.\n\n## 5. Security & Isolation Model\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Host[\"Host Operating System / Terminal Environment\"]\n    Root[\"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Root\"]\n    Sandbox[\"stdio JSON-RPC Sandbox\"]\n    Membrane2[\"Path Canonicalization\u003cbr/>(HeLa Membrane)\"]\n    Nucleus2[\"Explicit CWD & Timeout Limits\u003cbr/>(HeLa Nucleus)\"]\n    Ribosome2[\"Process Group Teardown\u003cbr/>(HeLa Ribosome)\"]\n    Cytosol2[\"SSRF IP Filtering\u003cbr/>(HeLa Cytosol)\"]\n\n    Host --> Root\n    Root --> Sandbox & Membrane2 & Nucleus2 & Ribosome2 & Cytosol2\n```\n\n1. **Process Boundary**: Each server runs in an isolated Node.js child process communicating exclusively over standard input/output (`stdio`) via JSON-RPC 2.0 messages.\n2. **Memory Safety**: SQLite database operations in `hela-genome` use parameterized queries with WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) mode.\n3. **Credential handling**: Doctor reports presence, not values. Generated client configurations can contain explicitly configured credentials; treat those files and generator output as private. Process separation is not a security sandbox, and remote endpoint authentication/authorization remain explicitly deferred; see [security status](security-audit.md).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Profile System & Lifecycle Management\n\nThe ecosystem configuration generator (`scripts/generate-config.mjs`) reads:\n1. **`config/inventory.json`**: Authoritative component catalog, entrypoints, Git repositories, and alias mappings.\n2. **`config/profiles.json`**: Curated stacks tailored to specific agent personas and hardware targets.\n\nThe installer matches the selected profile against the inventory and chosen snapshot, then renders client syntax: `mcpServers` JSON, OpenCode/Kilo `mcp`, Zed `context_servers`, or Codex `[mcp_servers.*]` TOML. Runtime installation also refreshes selected-profile schema catalogs. All eight profiles are supported; lappy's full ten-cell roster and servy's eight-cell roster are installation choices, not code forks.\n"},{"source":"docs/beginner-guide.md","title":"Beginner's Guide: HeLa MCP Ecosystem","summary":"HeLa connects an AI client to tools for memory, files, commands, research, design, browsers, Android, and Blender. Install only the profile you need. Tools can change files and…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/beginner-guide.html","text":"# Beginner's Guide: HeLa MCP Ecosystem\n\n![Blotcat beginner setup](../assets/blotcat-beginner.jpg)\n\nHeLa connects an AI client to tools for memory, files, commands, research, design, browsers, Android, and Blender. Install only the profile you need. Tools can change files and run commands with the server account's privileges; review your client's permissions before use.\n\nCurrent software versions are listed in [current versions](current-versions.md). Setup time depends on dependencies and downloads; browser and hardware workflows need extra software.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Node.js **22 LTS** is the tested full-stack environment. Enzyme requires at least 20.18.1; Receptor requires 22. Individual packages can declare different standalone engine ranges.\n- Git and a terminal. Use Node directly on Linux/macOS, or `setup.ps1` in Windows PowerShell / `setup.cmd` in Command Prompt. `setup.sh` remains available as a Unix compatibility shim.\n- Optional capabilities: Chromium/Playwright for browser work; the local Mirage model for Cytosol visual analysis, OCR, detection, segmentation, and standalone-image inspection; ADB and a device for Android; and the Blender bridge for 3D.\n\nNeedle and Mirage are profile capabilities rather than separate client deployments. Needle is installed with every canonical profile through Mitosis. Mirage is installed only by profiles that include Cytosol: `dev-workspace`, `research`, `web-devops`, and `all`. Selecting one of those profiles installs both automatically; selecting `minimal`, `headless-server`, `android-testing`, or `3d-modeling` does not install Mirage.\n\n## Install a profile\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem.git\ncd hela-mcp-ecosystem\nnode setup.mjs --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n```\n\nOn Windows PowerShell, run `.\\setup.ps1 --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive`; in Command Prompt, use `setup.cmd` with the same arguments. Change the client to `cursor`, `claude`, `gemini`, `antigravity`, `kilo`, `zed`, or `codex` as appropriate. The installer prints its managed prefix and a private client fragment: review and merge that fragment into your client settings. It does not overwrite existing client files or reuse your working repositories. Run `node setup.mjs` without flags for interactive profile/mode/client selection.\n\nAll eight profiles are supported:\n\n- `minimal`: three research/memory cells.\n- `dev-workspace`: eight development/browser/design cells; select it explicitly when you need the broader workstation stack.\n- `headless-server`: seven cells without browser/device dependencies.\n- `research`: five research/browser/workspace cells.\n- `web-devops`: six frontend/browser/design cells.\n- `android-testing`: five cells including device control.\n- `3d-modeling`: five cells including Blender.\n- `all`: all ten cells.\n\nSee [profiles](profiles.md) for membership. Your selection is independent of other machines: lappy's ten-cell roster and servy's eight-cell roster are examples, not required configurations for everyone.\n\n## One connection or separate servers\n\nThe installer normally configures separate MCP servers. Add `--runtime` to keep the same profile behind one endpoint:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime --non-interactive\n```\n\nRuntime is a serve mode, not a profile named `runtime`. Its catalog advertises schemas without starting cells; a first call starts only its target. [Runtime details](on-demand-runtime.md) explain catalog generation, missing metadata, and recovery.\n\nOther mechanisms are `--mode docker` (separate containers), `--mode docker-runtime` (one runtime container), and `--mode nix` (pinned toolchain and packaged runtime). Runtime modes also support `--transport http`. See [installation modes](installation.md) for exact requirements and commands; Docker needs a working daemon, and HTTP listeners are started explicitly.\n\n## Optional keys\n\nOpenRouter, GitHub, and Google keys are optional for discovery and keyless capabilities. Keyless does **not** mean every operation is offline: web research and external design services still need network access, and generative-model features require their configured provider or local engine. See [keys and services](keys-and-secrets.md).\n\nGenerated configuration can contain credentials you explicitly provide. Keep it private, and do not paste the complete file into a chat or issue.\n\n## Verify the selected installation\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace\nnpm run test:matrix\n```\n\nDoctor checks selected paths, versions, revisions, and actual isolated MCP replies. Runtime checking also verifies catalog parity and zero discovery startups. The configuration matrix checks 80 profile/client combinations; it does not log in to GUI agents or exercise hardware.\n\nFor an explicit prefix, use `node setup.mjs doctor --prefix /path/to/prefix`. For Docker or Nix defaults, select the same `--mode` and profile as installation. `doctor --url http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp` checks a running HTTP endpoint; it does not inspect remote source revisions.\n\nOn a full ten-cell checkout you can also run `npm test` and `npm run test:profiles`. On a partial checkout use `npm run test:profiles -- --profile \u003cid>`. Missing unselected hardware repositories are not a reason to change your machine's profile.\n\nReload the relevant client deliberately after reviewing configuration changes. Some clients, including Zed, can auto-reload servers when settings are saved. Do not change live vault hooks or restart unrelated clients merely to silence a warning. See [integration](integration.md) and [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).\n"},{"source":"docs/code-mode-research.md","title":"Code Mode for HeLa: research and design","summary":"Status: research. Nothing here is implemented.","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/code-mode-research.html","text":"# Code Mode for HeLa: research and design\n\nStatus: research. Nothing here is implemented.\n\nThe question: should the ecosystem expose **one code-execution tool** instead of (or beside)\nhundreds of MCP tool schemas — and what would that cost in security and complexity?\n\nTwo independent labs published the same pattern within six weeks of each other, from very\ndifferent platforms. That coincidence is the strongest signal in this document. The evidence\nis thinner than the blog posts suggest, and the sandbox is the whole problem.\n\n## 1. What Code Mode is\n\nThe model receives one tool whose input is source code. That code calls typed functions\nrepresenting the connected tools, runs in a sandbox, and returns only its final output.\nIntermediate results never enter the model's context.\n\n```\ndirect tool calling                    code mode\n─────────────────                      ─────────\nmodel → tool call → result             model → code ──┐\nmodel → tool call → result                          ├─ sandbox: call, filter, loop\nmodel → tool call → result                          ├─ sandbox: call, join\nmodel → answer                                      └─ return only the answer\n```\n\nThree claims are made, and they fail or succeed independently:\n\n1. **Context economy** — schemas and intermediate results stop competing with the task.\n2. **Composition** — loops, branches, joins and retries move out of the message loop.\n3. **Accuracy** — models write better code than tool-call JSON, because they have seen more\n   of it.\n\nClaim 3 is repeated most and evidenced least.\n\n## 2. Evidence, and what each source actually shows\n\n| Source | Claim | What it demonstrates | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Anthropic, *Code execution with MCP* (Nov 2025) | 150k → 2k tokens, \"98.7%\" | One worked example with a generated tool-file tree; illustrative, not a benchmark. Their platform docs separately claim 35–75% input-token reductions on multi-step workflows — the more defensible number | Direction yes, magnitude no |\n| Cloudflare, *Code Mode* (Sep 2025) | \"Striking\" results; more and more complex tools as TypeScript | Qualitative engineering report; no published eval or failure rates | Medium |\n| CodeAct (ICML 2024) | Code actions beat JSON/text \"up to 20%\" | 17 LLMs, API-Bank plus a new benchmark | High for code-as-action; not MCP-specific |\n\nNone of them quantify: how often generated code is wrong where a tool call would have\nsucceeded, what the sandbox costs to operate, or who reviews code that mutates state.\n\n## 3. What it would cost *here*, measured\n\nMeasured against this installation's live catalog, not a hypothetical:\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Reachable tools in one session | 305 |\n| `tools/list` schema size | **161 KB** |\n| Estimated tokens (bytes ÷ 4) | **≈ 40,000** |\n| Average schema per tool | 528 B |\n| Largest single schema | 4.9 KB (`logo_workflow`) |\n| Declared across all cells | 591 (286 shadowed by bare-name collisions) |\n\n40k tokens is roughly a fifth of a 200k window, paid before the first request. Namespacing\nevery tool would make the number worse, not better — 591 schemas. That is the real\nmotivation: not that code mode is fashionable, but that this catalog is too large to inline.\n\nTwo honest caveats:\n\n- Providers cache tool definitions (Anthropic prompt caching, OpenAI automatic caching), so\n  the *marginal* cost is lower than 40k per turn. The context window is still occupied, and\n  cache reads are cheap but not free.\n- The saving only converts to wall-clock or accuracy if the model actually needed most of\n  those schemas. For a single-tool task, inlining 305 tools versus loading one costs the same\n  either way — and code mode adds a sandbox and a failure mode for nothing.\n\n## 4. Critiques worth taking seriously\n\n- **You still need the types.** Code that calls `membrane.read_file(path, encoding)` must\n  know the signature. Without `describe`, models hallucinate parameters; with it, you have\n  traded a schema block for a discovery round-trip. Neither is free.\n- **Debugging moves to the model.** A failed tool call returns a tool error. Failed code\n  returns a stack trace through a sandbox; the retry burns tokens on the whole program, not\n  one call.\n- **Reviewability drops by default.** Tool calls are individually visible in a transcript;\n  one execution that makes forty calls is not, unless the runtime logs each proxied call.\n  Auditability must be designed in, not inherited.\n- **Prompt injection becomes code execution.** If any tool output can steer the model, the\n  blast radius is now \"whatever the sandbox allows\" instead of \"whatever the next tool call\n  allows\". This is the argument for keeping credentials and network out of the sandbox.\n- **It rewards strong models.** CodeAct shows gains across 17 models, but the spread is wide;\n  a weaker model writing a 40-line program that half-works is worse than three tool calls.\n- **Sandbox dependencies rot.** Native modules (`isolated-vm`) must be rebuilt per Node ABI;\n  WASM runtimes are stable but slow. The cheapest robust option is an OS-level boundary\n  around a plain Node child process.\n\n## 5. Three designs\n\n**A. Execute + discover inside the runtime (recommended).** Two new tools beside the existing\nones: `hela.execute({code})` and a discovery path (`search`, `describe`). Every sandbox call\nproxies back to the runtime, which applies the same roots policy, approvals and audit as a\ndirect call.\n\n![Code mode flow](../assets/diagrams/09-code-mode-flow.svg)\n\n*Source: [09-code-mode-flow.mmd](../assets/diagrams/09-code-mode-flow.mmd).*\n\n**B. Filesystem of tools.** Generate `servers/\u003ccell>/\u003ctool>.ts` plus `index.ts` and let the\nagent read what it needs. Anthropic's shape. Cheapest to bolt on, but it needs a readable\nfilesystem inside the sandbox and costs more tokens per discovery than one `describe` call.\n\n**C. Keep direct calls.** Correct for small catalogs; at 305 tools it is what we do now.\n\nA and B compose: A's `describe` can serve the same generated text B writes to disk.\n\n## 6. Trust boundary\n\n![Code mode boundary](../assets/diagrams/10-code-mode-boundary.svg)\n\n*Source: [10-code-mode-boundary.mmd](../assets/diagrams/10-code-mode-boundary.mmd).*\n\nThe rule that makes the design coherent: **capabilities live in the parent; the sandbox holds\nnone.** No network, no host filesystem beyond a per-execution workspace, no environment, no\ncredentials. Tool calls cross one RPC channel that the parent validates. If the sandbox is\ncompromised, an attacker gets a workspace and a list of tool calls that policy already allows.\n\n## 7. Sandbox options on Linux\n\n| Option | Isolation | Cost | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `node:vm` in-process | **none** — documented as not a security boundary | \u003c1 ms | Only for trusted snippets; never for model output |\n| Node permission model | fs/net/child-process gating | ~25 ms | Useful hardening; still not a security boundary |\n| Bun | none by default | low | Reject |\n| Deno subprocess | capability-based, deny by default | 30–50 ms | Strong ergonomics; ships a second runtime |\n| QuickJS (WASM) | near-perfect (software fault isolation); a guest loop or OOM cannot kill the host | 1–3 ms, slow compute | Excellent fast path for pure data work; async is limited to one concurrent bridge call |\n| `isolated-vm` | V8 isolate, memory-isolated | 2–5 ms; native build per ABI | **Avoid**: maintenance mode, fragile C++, a V8 panic takes the host process down with it |\n| Child process + `bwrap`/seccomp/Landlock | kernel-enforced namespaces, fs and net | 10–25 ms | Strongest for orchestration; Linux-only |\n\nA two-tier split is possible — route pure data work to an in-process QuickJS isolate, and\nonly orchestration to a process sandbox. It buys ~20 ms on the cheap path and doubles the\nexecution surface to maintain. Phase 1 should ship **one** tier; add the fast path only if\nmeasurement shows the process start dominates real tasks.\n\nRecommendation: **child process + `bwrap`/Landlock profile + stdio RPC**, with the\nparent enforcing time, memory, call budget and output size. It is not containment against a\nkernel exploit; it is containment against `rm -rf ~`, `curl | sh`, and runaway loops — which\nis the realistic threat for a local, single-user agent.\n\nThree details that are cheap and easy to forget: mark inherited file descriptors\n`O_CLOEXEC` and `close_range(3, ~0)` before exec (a leaked socket is a channel); run with\n`--unshare-net`, `--cap-drop ALL`, read-only `/usr` and `tmpfs` `/tmp`; and cap tool calls\nper execution (30–50) so an infinite loop cannot hammer an external API even while inside\nthe time limit.\n\nNote what this machine already grants: the nucleus cell runs arbitrary shell commands today.\nCode mode does not add a capability class here; it adds a *narrower, audited* interface and a\nplace to enforce limits that raw shell access does not have. A sandbox weaker than the\nexisting shell would be theatre.\n\n## 8. Progressive discovery\n\nLoading 305 signatures up front recreates the problem. Two tools instead:\n\n- `search(query, {limit})` → ranked `{path, cell, tool, one_line}`, no schemas.\n- `describe(path)` → one TypeScript signature plus its doc comment.\n\nBoth are generated from the same source: a `tools.d.ts` built at boot from each cell's\n`tools/list` schema. The file is the catalog's single representation — `search` indexes it,\n`describe` serves a slice, and the sandbox can be handed the declarations it asked for.\n\nDetail levels (name / signature / full) matter: triage does not need parameter docs.\nNames must be unique for paths to exist, which is the concrete argument for turning\nnamespacing on: 286 declared tools have no addressable identity today because a later cell\nshadows the bare name.\n\n## 9. Execution contract\n\n```\nhela.execute({ code, timeout_ms?, max_calls? })\n  → { status: \"ok\" | \"error\" | \"timeout\" | \"denied\",\n      result, logs,\n      calls: [{ path, ms, ok }],\n      duration_ms }\n```\n\nEnforced by the parent, not the sandbox: wall clock (default 30 s), output (256 KB, then\ntruncate with a marker), call count (default 50), memory (rlimit/cgroup).\n\nFailures are data. Sandbox error, tool error and policy denial all return a structured\n`status` so the model can retry or report, and the host never dies from generated code.\n\n## 10. Security model\n\n| Threat | Mitigation | Residual |\n|---|---|---|\n| Injected code exfiltrates files | no network, workspace-only fs, roots policy per call | data can be written into an allowed root and read later |\n| Destructive calls (`delete_file`, archive extract) | per-tool approval; mutating tools denied by default in code mode | approved code can still be wrong |\n| Resource exhaustion | time, call budget, memory, output caps | legal-but-heavy work still costs CPU |\n| Secrets reachable from code | credentials stay in the parent; bindings, not env | a tool returning secrets leaks them into `logs` |\n| No audit trail | every RPC logged: path, args digest, duration, execution id | logs hold digests, not payloads |\n\nApproval fatigue is the realistic failure: a prompt people click through is not a control.\nRead-only tools should run unprompted; only mutations should stop.\n\n## 11. Implementation plan\n\nPhase 1 — read-only, no approvals:\n1. `code-mode/catalog.mjs`: unique path per tool from live `tools/list`; one-line summary and\n   full signature per tool.\n2. `code-mode/search.mjs`: token-overlap or BM25 over name + description. No embeddings.\n3. `code-mode/executor.mjs`: child process, stdio RPC, `bwrap` profile (`--unshare-net`,\n   `--cap-drop ALL`, `--tmpfs /tmp`, read-only system paths), `close_range` before exec,\n   kill on timeout.\n4. Register `hela.execute` and `hela.describe` behind `HELA_CODE_MODE=1`.\n5. Tests: escape attempts (fs outside workspace, `fetch`, `process`, infinite loop, memory\n   bomb) and golden multi-step tasks.\n\nPhase 2: approval policy for mutating tools; append-only audit log; `--dry-run` returning\nplanned calls without executing them.\n\nPhase 3: snippets — save a successful execution as a skill under\n`~/.local/share/hela/snippets` with a `SKILL.md`, discoverable by `search`. This is the part\nthat compounds: capability grows without schemas growing.\n\nMeasure before/after on three fixed tasks: schema bytes in context, model-mediated calls,\nwall time, wrong-tool rate. If the schema saving does not show up as wall-time or error-rate\nimprovement, stop at phase 1.\n\n## 12. What would make this not worth doing\n\n- If client-side caching keeps schemas out of context, claim 1 mostly evaporates and only\n  composition remains.\n- If HeLa's task mix is mostly single-tool, code mode adds a sandbox for nothing. Measure the\n  mix first.\n- If the sandbox ends up in-process, the honest description is \"no sandbox\", and approvals\n  stop being optional.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Cloudflare, *Code Mode: the better way to use MCP* — https://blog.cloudflare.com/code-mode/\n- Cloudflare, *Code Mode* and *How Code Mode works* — https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/tools/codemode/\n- Anthropic, *Code execution with MCP* — https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/code-execution-with-mcp\n- Anthropic platform docs, *Advanced tool use* (search tools, programmatic tool calling) — https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/tool-use\n- Wang et al., *Executable Code Actions Elicit Better LLM Agents* (CodeAct, ICML 2024) — https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.01030\n"},{"source":"docs/current-versions.md","title":"Current versions and tool surfaces","summary":"Generated from package metadata, inventory, and the schema catalog. Ecosystem 1.11.26; runtime 1.4.5.","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/current-versions.html","text":"# Current versions and tool surfaces\n\nGenerated from package metadata, inventory, and the schema catalog. Ecosystem **1.11.26**; runtime **1.4.5**.\n\n- **HeLa Mitosis** (`hela-mitosis`): server **3.1.3**, GPL-3.0-or-later, 24–29 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp).\n- **HeLa Genome** (`hela-genome`): server **3.3.0**, GPL-3.0-or-later, 31 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp).\n- **HeLa Membrane** (`hela-membrane`): server **3.0.6**, GPL-3.0-or-later, 16 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/filesystem-mcp).\n- **HeLa Nucleus** (`hela-nucleus`): server **3.0.7**, MIT, 7 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/terminal-mcp).\n- **HeLa Ribosome** (`hela-ribosome`): server **3.2.4**, GPL-3.0-or-later, 28 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/menager-mcp).\n- **HeLa Enzyme** (`hela-enzyme`): server **4.3.4**, GPL-3.0-or-later, 24 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/researcher-mcp).\n- **HeLa Cytosol** (`hela-cytosol`): server **3.3.8**, MIT, 76 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/browser-mcp).\n- **HeLa Phenotype** (`hela-phenotype`): server **3.3.0**, MIT, 28 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp).\n- **HeLa Receptor** (`hela-receptor`): server **3.0.6**, MIT, 46 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/scrcpy-mcp).\n- **HeLa Plastid** (`hela-plastid`): server **3.0.5**, GPL-3.0-or-later, 15 canonical tools; [source](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/ll3m-mcp).\n\nCounts vary with declared features. Runtime adds `hela.pipe` and an agent alias when available. Profiles choose eligible cells, not an eager startup count. Historical release notes/snapshots keep their original versions.\n"},{"source":"docs/deployment-2026-11-06.md","title":"Servy deployment verification — 2026-11-06","summary":"This records the earlier runtime 1.1.0 rollout. The subsequent runtime 1.2.0 on-demand deployment supersedes its process/startup state; the evidence below remains historical.","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/docs/deployment-2026-11-06.html","text":"# Servy deployment verification — 2026-11-06\n\nThis records the earlier runtime 1.1.0 rollout. The subsequent [runtime 1.2.0 on-demand deployment](on-demand-runtime.md#historical-servy-rollout--runtime-120) supersedes its process/startup state; the evidence below remains historical.\n\nThe v1.4.0 source release (`c9faa9b`) is deployed to servy's systemd-managed HTTP runtime. This record covers that deployment, not authenticated GUI sessions or live vault reconciliation.\n\n## Published source and builds\n\n- Genome: `16fc4f31735e139d690ae0ace8d4feaf1a603563`.\n- Enzyme: `5399e33c7c8a5b75ad620dc33f66a1cbf49972f7`.\n- Runtime: `797bad451b45d0d67631eeb8cdb1c93d259f614c`, version **1.1.0**.\n- Remaining cell pins are in `config/snapshots/v1.4.0.json`. Historical v1.0.0–v1.3.0 snapshots were not modified.\n\nRuntime recovery passes **20/20** isolated fault tests on x86_64-linux and native aarch64-linux; all 29 fixture PIDs per run were reaped. Coverage includes hung RPCs, SIGTERM/SIGKILL, exit-before-restart, pipe routing, bounded retries, profile boundaries, and shutdown cleanup. No live service cell was faulted during deployment.\n\nFinal-source Nix builds use the committed lockfile, fixed-output npm dependencies, production installation, and Node **22.23.4**:\n\n- x86_64-linux: `/nix/store/gzxl62xr29nh36yb75c242gp4mnsibsh-hela-runtime-1.1.0`.\n- aarch64-linux: `/nix/store/y9hqz9kyx860s5xqx3gk23x7acsghis2-hela-runtime-1.1.0`.\n\nGenome's clean-install regression suite passes **19 suites / 242 tests**. Enzyme's **79 tests** and Phenotype's **74 tests** pass. Ten isolated standalone MCP handshakes, 80 client-configuration combinations, three probe regressions, and three isolated direct/runtime read-only parity calls pass. These checks do not establish authenticated GUI or hardware integration.\n\n## Controlled promotion\n\nAll eight selected cells, runtime, and ecosystem checkouts were clean before fast-forward promotion. Validated staged builds were promoted; Genome, Ribosome, Enzyme, and Phenotype received fresh ARM dependency trees. Old build/dependency directories remain in the rollout's `rollback/` directory. All ten deployed checkouts passed `npm ls --depth=0`; source revisions agreed with lappy at source-release verification.\n\nBefore stopping the service, three online SQLite backups passed integrity checks and the live vault was archived again. Earlier backups were hash-verified. No active database file was replaced or restored.\n\nOnly `hela-runtime.service` was stopped/restarted. Its old ten processes, including a duplicate Genome child, exited. The new service owns one runtime plus exactly eight direct child cells; all use Nix Node 22.23.4. At verification, systemd reported active state, zero automatic restarts, and successful exit status.\n\nThe deployed package is rooted by:\n\n`/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/deployments/todo102-z65w9kzr/runtime`\n\nThe drop-in `/etc/systemd/system/hela-runtime.service.d/10-nix-package.conf` selects that executable and prepends pinned Node to PATH. The original unit remains intact. Existing profile, vault setting, host, port, and endpoint-security configuration were not changed.\n\n## Discovery and ownership\n\n- Default profile: **dev-workspace**, eight cells; no Receptor or Plastid deployment on servy.\n- Local `/healthz`: HTTP 200, runtime **1.1.0**.\n- Local and public MCP initialize/tools discovery: **236 unique tools** at `/mcp`, including `create_mission`, `list_missions`, `update_mission`, and `hela.pipe`; hardware-only tools absent.\n- Public endpoint: `https://hela-runtime.glassgallery.my.id/mcp`.\n- A local HTTP `terminal_session_list` call passed MCP and payload-level error checks.\n- Isolated strict package/initialize/inventory/snapshot/pin checks passed for eight cells and runtime; deployed doctor reported **8/8 READY**.\n\nThe isolated feature-disabled runtime exposes **230 tools**. The live configuration adds `llm_query`, `llm_decompose_task`, `llm_suggest_route`, `llm_summarize`, `agent_run`, and runtime alias `hela.agent.run`. No isolated tools were missing from live discovery. Those additional model-backed tools were discovered, not invoked.\n\nThe 211 unresolved divergent-note conflicts remain archived. All **633** existing servy conflict archive files retained their pre-rollout hashes. No conflict winner, explicit bulk deduplication, or deletion reconciliation was forced.\n\n## Evidence and retained recovery material\n\nServy rollout directory:\n\n`/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/deployments/todo102-z65w9kzr/`\n\nIt contains `manifest.json`, `backup/`, `rollback/`, the runtime GC root, `verify-versions.mjs.log`, `doctor.mjs.log`, `http-verification.json`, and `feature-difference.json`. These are local operational records, not public credential/configuration dumps.\n\nPrevious backups remain at:\n\n- lappy: `/home/azzar/.local/state/hela/backups/pre-10.2-d9nokk5t/`.\n- servy: `/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/backups/pre-10.2-m6j95llu/`.\n\nRollback material is retained, but no live rollback was performed. Any rollback must stop the service, preserve current databases/vaults, inspect compatibility, and select retained code/dependencies deliberately. Do not overwrite active databases or hard-reset repositories.\n\n## Remaining work\n\n- Authenticated Zed/Codex ACP session and GUI warning verification remain pending. Direct MCP and isolated Codex discovery passed; credentials were not copied and the optional ACP duplicate-filter override remains unset.\n- Two older stdio runtimes owned by an active servy OpenCode client were preserved. They require a deliberate client reload; the HTTP service rollout does not update code already loaded in unrelated processes. Local client reload state also remains unverified.\n- PM2 has no active or saved individual MCP cell entries. It still owns the legacy `mcp-gateway` on port **8010**, with no child cells and seven retired backends on **8011–8017** reporting down. Historical entries remain in `dump.pm2.bak`. Review consumers/routes before retiring this gateway; it does not serve the verified runtime endpoint on **8090**.\n- Review archived vault conflicts before reconciliation. Deployment is not conflict resolution.\n- True first-call ignition, full revision/configuration manifest invalidation, optional hardware integrations, and deferred endpoint authentication/credential rotation remain separate work.\n"},{"source":"docs/deployment-2026-11-19.md","title":"Servy runtime update — 2026-11-19","summary":"Records the lappy → servy rollout of ecosystem 1.9.19 and runtime 1.3.3. Evidence for this host only; it does not describe other machines.","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/docs/deployment-2026-11-19.html","text":"# Servy runtime update — 2026-11-19\n\nRecords the lappy → servy rollout of ecosystem **1.9.19** and runtime **1.3.3**. Evidence for this host only; it does not describe other machines.\n\n## Host layout (unchanged by this update)\n\n- Source checkouts: `/home/ubuntu/MCPservers/*` (ecosystem, runtime, eight cells).\n- Service: `hela-runtime.service`, `HELA_PROFILE=dev-workspace`, `HELA_ECOSYSTEM_ROOT=/home/ubuntu/MCPservers/hela-mcp-ecosystem`, HTTP on `0.0.0.0:8090`.\n- Roster: **eight cells** — Mitosis, Genome, Membrane, Nucleus, Ribosome, Enzyme, Phenotype, Cytosol. Receptor and Plastid are deliberately absent: this host has neither `adb` nor `blender`. Lappy runs the ten-cell `all` roster; the difference is an installation choice, not a code fork.\n- ExecStart comes from the `10-nix-package.conf` drop-in and points at a wrapper in a dated deployment directory, which execs a Nix-built runtime with the pinned Node 22.23.4.\n\n## What ran\n\n1. `memory.db`, `hela-runs.db` and the local tool-catalog copy backed up to `/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/backups/pre-1.9.18-20261119T015844Z/`.\n2. All ten checkouts pulled fast-forward to the 1.9.19 revisions (`hela-runtime` 6ca8b61, ecosystem 61f848e, cells at their release commits). `researcher-mcp` was on a detached HEAD and was moved onto `master` first.\n3. Cells rebuilt (`npm ci` + `npm run build`) — eight cells plus runtime, all green.\n4. Runtime packaged with Nix: `hela-runtime-1.3.3` at `/nix/store/pc49i4nc1qs7nfkkk5mz81mnc9jqj508-hela-runtime-1.3.3`, GC-rooted at `~/.local/state/hela/gcroots/runtime-1.3.3`.\n5. Staged wrapper under `~/.local/state/hela/deployments/servy-1.9.18-20261118T190500Z/`, smoke-tested on port 8091 (health reported 1.3.3) before touching the live unit.\n6. Drop-in backed up, ExecStart switched, `daemon-reload`, `systemctl restart`. Old deployment directory and drop-in backup retained for rollback.\n\n## Result\n\n- `systemctl is-active` → active; `/healthz` → `{\"version\":\"1.3.3\"}`.\n- HTTP MCP doctor → READY, **240 tools**.\n- Lazy boot verified: `terminal_session_list` started Nucleus and answered.\n\n## Gotcha worth remembering\n\nAfter the rebuild, the runtime advertised only 209 tools and refused `terminal_session_list` and `keyword_extraction` as unknown. Cause: the runtime trusts a cell manifest only when its build digest matches the catalog, and cells rebuilt on this host hash differently from the catalog produced on lappy — two manifests were rejected and those cells vanished from the surface silently.\n\nFix: regenerate the catalog on the host that runs the cells (`node scripts/generate-tool-catalog.mjs --profile dev-workspace`), then restart. The runtime is on-demand, so nothing else changed. A profile-scoped catalog is a deployment artifact here; the repository copy is untouched.\n\n## Rollback\n\n```sh\nsudo cp /etc/systemd/system/hela-runtime.service.d/10-nix-package.conf.bak-\u003cstamp> \\\n        /etc/systemd/system/hela-runtime.service.d/10-nix-package.conf\nsudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart hela-runtime\n```\n\nThe previous wrapper (`servy-update-20261114T063237Z`) and its Nix runtime are still on disk.\n\n## 1.9.24 rollout (servy, 2026-11-19T21:01Z)\n\nSecond pass on the same day, carrying the RPC and search releases.\n\n1. Checkouts pulled fast-forward: `project-mcp` b720edf (3.0.5), `terminal-mcp` 0088f0b (3.0.6), `researcher-mcp` eb257b8 (4.0.6), `browser-mcp` 8f0b39f (3.2.6), `hela-runtime` c2b02ff (1.3.6), ecosystem d193cc4 (1.9.24). The repository catalog copy was reverted before the pull — on this host it is a deployment artifact, as noted below.\n2. Those four cells rebuilt (`npm run build`).\n3. Catalog regenerated **on servy** with `--profile dev-workspace` before any restart, so the digests match the builds on this host.\n4. Runtime packaged with Nix: `hela-runtime-1.3.6` at `/nix/store/g1npgvsii07mpclxzsxc9lm3gd15n12m-hela-runtime-1.3.6`, GC-rooted by `runtime-nix` inside the new deployment directory.\n5. Staged wrapper at `~/.local/state/hela/deployments/servy-1.9.24-20261118T210125Z/runtime/bin/hela-runtime`, drop-in backed up to `10-nix-package.conf.bak-20261118T210124Z`, ExecStart switched, `daemon-reload`, restart.\n6. Verified over HTTP: `/healthz` → `1.3.6`; `tools/list` → **239 tools** on the 8-cell `dev-workspace` profile (297 unique cell tools at profile all, minus Receptor 46 and Plastid 15, plus the 3 runtime tools); `hela.agent.run` present and the cell-local `agent_run` gone; `execute_command` with `cd /tmp && echo marker-ok` returned `stdout \"marker-ok\\n\"`, exit 0; the scatter query returned zero Wikipedia hits.\n\n239 rather than the earlier 240 because Mitosis now contributes one schema variant (24 tools) instead of the union across its four variants (29). Not a missing cell: Nucleus and Enzyme were both present and callable, which is the failure mode this host hit at 1.9.19.\n\nRollback is unchanged: restore the drop-in backup and restart. The 1.9.21 deployment directory and its Nix runtime stay on disk.\n"},{"source":"docs/documentation-refresh-2026-11-06.md","title":"Documentation refresh — 2026-11-06","summary":"Ecosystem 1.5.1 is a documentation-only source snapshot. The ten MCP cells remain 3.0.0; runtime remains 1.2.0 at its existing implementation revision. Current versions and…","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/docs/documentation-refresh-2026-11-06.html","text":"# Documentation refresh — 2026-11-06\n\nEcosystem **1.5.1** is a documentation-only source snapshot. The ten MCP cells remain **3.0.0**; runtime remains **1.2.0** at its existing implementation revision. [Current versions](current-versions.md) and [profile counts](generated-profile-counts.md) are generated from canonical metadata.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audited all 12 repositories. Updated the ten cells' README version banners and generated canonical tool references; the runtime's existing 1.2.0 reference was already current.\n- Completed all 16 English/Indonesian website pages, including current architecture, profiles, requirements, runtime behavior, diagnostics, privacy, and historical showcase labels.\n- Corrected Codex keys and actual repository paths, including Plastid's `brain` entrypoint. Browser previews intentionally omit private environment values and are not byte-identical installation files.\n- Regenerated seven committed client examples with a sanitized environment. The older seven-cell Compose file is explicitly a deployment scaffold, not full-profile or transport acceptance evidence.\n- Preserved v1.0.0–v1.5.0 snapshots, historical release evidence, and independently versioned Blender/HyperFrames/vendor material.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Documentation generation is idempotent; `docs:check` and all **74** documentation/configuration regressions pass. These checks include local web links/anchors, unique IDs, JSON-LD, current versions, canonical tool lists, script ordering, and release cache-busting.\n- CLI matrix: **80/80** profile/backend cases. Probe and inventory regressions: **4/4** each. Strict package/MCP/inventory/snapshot versions and revision pins: **11/11**.\n- Standalone integration and safe cross-cell checks: **15/15** across ten servers, with no tool-name collisions in the feature-disabled probe environment.\n- All eight profiles: **16/16** namespace-mode checks pass, with exact schemas, zero discovery startups, and safe read-only routing.\n- Fresh native ARM probes validate all eight `dev-workspace` cells against the new documentation revisions. Schema parity retains both verified Nucleus build digests; compiled implementation bytes were reused, not rebuilt as a new software release.\n- Browser checks: **64** visible configurator selections per language; **16** pages at **1200 px** and **390 px** widths without document-level horizontal overflow. Desktop/mobile screenshots were inspected. Canonical search links, successful copy, and denied-clipboard feedback were checked; failed clipboard writes no longer claim success.\n- Current Markdown file-link scan: **104** references checked, no missing targets. This is not an exhaustive external-link availability guarantee.\n- Secret scanning found only the pre-existing probe-test sentinel; no new credentials were added. Hardware, providers, and authenticated ACP/GUI sessions were not exercised by this documentation refresh.\n\n## Publication boundaries\n\nPlastid and Receptor had unrelated remote dependency updates. Documentation-only commits were merged into their default branches without discarding those updates. Snapshot v1.5.1 deliberately pins the documentation-only parents, not unvalidated dependency changes.\n\nThe Pages workflow validates generated documentation and its tests before publishing. Source commit: `7f95832b4f0b9e6ba3057ffbb7df84b62e2bcd91`. Published Pages commit: `3077d3f8d02436637b4322407c58621069dfe922`.\n\n- [Ecosystem CI](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/actions/runs/42233639731): successful.\n- [Validated site build/sync](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/actions/runs/42233639734): successful.\n- [GitHub Pages deployment](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/actions/runs/42233666134): successful.\n- Public checks at [hela.my.id](https://hela.my.id/) and its Indonesian pages: all **16** pages return HTTP 200 with v1.5.1 and versioned scripts; all **26** discovered internal resources return success. Generated data, app, stylesheet, counts, and the hero image load successfully.\n- Public browser configurators pass **64 selections per language**, with exact profile membership, current metadata, working canonical search links, and no desktop/mobile document overflow.\n\nNo live runtime was restarted or reconfigured. Servy's service remained active at PID `715090`, with zero automatic restarts during native ARM catalog verification. Active databases, existing vault conflicts, security settings, credentials, and unrelated client configurations were not changed. Deferred endpoint security, authenticated ACP acceptance, deliberate external-client reloads, legacy gateway retirement, and conflict reconciliation remain separate work.\n"},{"source":"docs/generated-profile-counts.md","title":"Generated profile counts","summary":"Regenerate with npm run catalog:generate; includes hela.pipe and the agent alias where enabled.","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/generated-profile-counts.html","text":"# Generated profile counts\n\nRegenerate with `npm run catalog:generate`; includes `hela.pipe` and the agent alias where enabled.\n\n- `minimal`: 3 cells, 80–86 runtime tools.\n- `dev-workspace`: 8 cells, 235–241 runtime tools.\n- `headless-server`: 7 cells, 159–165 runtime tools.\n- `research`: 5 cells, 172–178 runtime tools.\n- `web-devops`: 6 cells, 183–189 runtime tools.\n- `android-testing`: 5 cells, 133–139 runtime tools.\n- `3d-modeling`: 5 cells, 94–100 runtime tools.\n- `all`: 10 cells, 296–302 runtime tools.\n"},{"source":"docs/installation-acceptance.md","title":"Installer acceptance — 2026-11-07 (historical record)","summary":"This document records the 2026-11-07 Linux acceptance run. The current repository also provides Node-first, PowerShell, and CMD installer entrypoints; native Windows…","category":"operations","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/installation-acceptance.html","text":"# Installer acceptance — 2026-11-07 (historical record)\n\nThis document records the 2026-11-07 Linux acceptance run. The current repository\nalso provides Node-first, PowerShell, and CMD installer entrypoints; native Windows\ncold-install acceptance remains a separate verification task and is not implied by\nthe historical results below.\n\nLocal acceptance of the managed installer on Linux x86_64 with Node 22. This records working-tree changes, not a published release or a live-service rollout. Ecosystem release metadata remains 1.5.1; cells remain 3.0.0 and runtime 1.2.0.\n\n## Real execution\n\n- All eight profiles passed all five modes: `individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, and `nix` — 40 mode/profile checks.\n- Doctor verified 269 cell/runtime endpoints across those checks, including 24 private runtime HTTP probes.\n- All 320 profile/mode/client-format configurations passed structural checks. The generated Cursor-format commands were also executed: 162 actual client connections, including reconnects for persistence.\n- All 40 cases created an isolated Genome entity, restarted the client connection, and read the entity back. Runtime cases started no cells during discovery and only Genome on the first memory call.\n- The matrix reused freshly built isolated full-source artifacts, a Nix runtime package, and a full Docker image. These are **not 40 independent cold builds**. Native fixture repositories are linked into temporary prefixes; production installation still refuses symlinked source repositories.\n\nSeparate real installations passed fresh doctor checks: native individual/minimal, native runtime/all, Nix/minimal with and without the model, Docker-runtime/all with the model, and Docker/minimal without it. The model-free Nix and Docker installations were checked to contain no Needle model. The Docker image default profile was checked as well.\n\nExplicitly started native, Nix, and Docker Compose HTTP listeners passed `doctor --url`, then were stopped. Compose attached stdio also passed a real Genome mutation/reconnect/read cycle using named volumes, with no published stdio ports. The isolated Compose projects and their test volumes were removed. No doctor containers remained after acceptance.\n\nNix runtime output links were verified as GC roots. The runtime derivation can be reused from the local Nix store; cell dependencies are still installed from lockfiles and built in their managed source directories.\n\nThe pinned Needle engine/model completed an actual offline tool-selection smoke test on both the host and the full Docker image, returning a successful `read_graph` call. This supplements engine execution, asset checksums, and license verification; it is not a planner-quality benchmark.\n\n## Regression gates\n\n- 52 installer tests: profile/mode plans, isolated fixture installs and reinstalls, malformed options, snapshot/checkout failures and retry, repository origin/dirty/symlink refusal, output preservation, missing interpreters, changed builds, model checksum failures, HTTP protocol errors, and Docker cleanup on failure.\n- 74 documentation/configurator tests.\n- 80 standalone profile/client configuration cases.\n- Four MCP isolation/failure probes.\n- Documentation metadata consistency, shell syntax, and Git whitespace checks.\n\nThe secret scan completed; its sole finding was the existing synthetic credential sentinel in the isolation test, not a credential. This is not a container CVE audit.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nBuild isolated fixtures first using the [installation guide](installation.md). The source fixture must include all ten cells plus runtime; the Docker fixture must contain the full image. Then run:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test:install\nnpm run test:docs\nnpm run test:matrix\nnpm run test:probe\nnpm run docs:check\nnpm run test:install:modes -- \\\n  --source-prefix /path/to/fresh-runtime-all \\\n  --nix-prefix /path/to/fresh-nix-install \\\n  --docker-prefix /path/to/fresh-docker-all \\\n  --out /path/to/modes.json\n```\n\nThe matrix uses temporary private workspaces/data directories and deletes only its own temporary fixtures. It does not reuse production databases, vaults, or provider credentials. Generated client defaults may advertise more optional tools than doctor's deliberately provider-disabled baseline.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nReal installation acceptance in this historical record is Linux x86_64 only. The added CI jobs cover clean Docker/Nix installs and fixture regressions, but remote CI did not run for those working-tree changes. macOS, ARM64, WSL, and native Windows cold installs were not repeated in this acceptance pass.\n\nBrowser executables, Android/Blender bridges, external providers, authenticated GUI/ACP clients, host device/SSH access from containers, and security hardening remain separate checks. `READY` certifies the stated MCP/install checks, not every tool's external integration. Existing services, databases, vault conflicts, and historical release snapshots were not migrated or replaced.\n"},{"source":"docs/installation.md","title":"Installation modes and doctor","summary":"Blotcat assembling a selected profile into a managed runtime","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/installation.html","text":"# Installation modes and doctor\n\n![Blotcat assembling a selected profile into a managed runtime](../assets/blotcat-installation.png)\n\nThe installer supports eight profiles through five modes: `individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, and `nix`. Runtime-based modes support stdio and Streamable HTTP. A mode selects the execution mechanism; a profile selects eligible cells.\n\nOn a clean selected workspace, installation creates a profile-aware HeLa persona under `agent_persona/` and links `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `GEMINI.md` to its session entrypoint. Frea and Mema are Azzar's personal AI assistant personas: Frea contributes autonomous technical execution and adaptive judgment, while Mema contributes security, credential boundaries, reproducibility, and continuity. HeLa is a distributable synthesis of those operating principles; it does not copy their private persona files, memories, biography, credentials, paths, or identifiers. Existing `agent_persona/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `GEMINI.md` files are preserved and never overwritten automatically.\n\nCapability setup follows the selected profile. Every canonical profile includes Mitosis, so Needle 3 is provisioned unless `--without-model` is explicitly passed. Profiles containing Cytosol (`dev-workspace`, `research`, `web-devops`, and `all`) also provision the managed Mirage Python bridge and its `cactus-compute[vision]` dependency. Profiles containing Nucleus or Ribosome check for tmux; when missing, the installer asks before installing it through the platform package manager. `HELA_SYSTEM_DEPS_INSTALL=1` opts into that install without a prompt, including with `--non-interactive`, after the sudo availability check. CI always skips system dependency installation, and `--without-system-deps` overrides the opt-in. Existing tmux versions are never upgraded; versions older than 1.8 are reported as incompatible. Docker images and the Nix devShell provide tmux themselves.\n\nSee [local acceptance evidence](installation-acceptance.md) for the tested mechanisms, real client connections, persistence checks, and platform/integration boundaries.\n\n## Requirements and boundaries\n\n- Use Node 22+ and Git. Native modules need Python, make, a C/C++ compiler, and platform headers. `--mode nix` supplies a pinned development toolchain and packages the runtime with Nix.\n- Docker modes require a working Docker daemon and Compose v2. Their images build the selected cells from pinned source; they do not require host npm dependencies or per-cell Dockerfiles.\n- The installer and maintenance commands are Node-first and run on Linux, macOS, and native Windows. Use `node setup.mjs` on Unix-like systems, `.\\setup.ps1` in PowerShell, or `setup.cmd` in Command Prompt. The Bash files remain compatibility shims; Nix mode still requires a Unix-compatible Nix environment such as WSL.\n- The repository build, test, clean, and update runners use Node scripts, including the Windows `npm.cmd` executable where required. Native Windows cold-install acceptance is still a separate verification boundary; optional browser, Android, Blender, provider, and client integrations remain platform-specific.\n- The pinned local Needle engine/model is installed by default on Linux x86_64/ARM64, macOS ARM64, and Windows x64/ARM64. `--without-model` explicitly selects core MCP without that integration; it remains available for platforms without a pinned engine. Assets are fetched from an immutable upstream revision, checksum-verified, and accompanied by their license.\n- Mirage profiles require Python 3.9+ and network access during installation so the installer can create `browser-mcp/user_data/mirage/.venv` and install `cactus-compute[vision]`. The generated launcher passes the managed Mirage Python path to Cytosol and to the runtime when Cytosol is part of the selected profile. Mirage weights are downloaded and cached by Cactus on first image analysis.\n- Chromium/browser services, Android devices/ADB, Blender/its bridge, provider credentials, and GUI client authentication remain separate integration requirements. `READY` does not certify those operations or provide an OS sandbox.\n\nInstallation does not start a persistent service, replace existing repositories, or rewrite live client settings. For profiles containing Nucleus or Ribosome, tmux installation requires interactive consent or the explicit environment opt-in above. Endpoint security and credential rotation are not changed by this installer.\n\n\u003c!-- hela:installer-flags:start -->\n\u003c!-- Generated by scripts/generate-docs.mjs from scripts/installation.mjs. -->\n| Flag | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--profile VALUE` | Any of the eight canonical profiles (default minimal) |\n| `--mode VALUE` | Select the installation mode. |\n| `--client VALUE` | Generate a client fragment, never overwrite live client settings (default skip) |\n| `--prefix VALUE` | Managed installation directory; existing unmanaged directories are refused |\n| `--root VALUE` | Metadata source checkout (default this checkout) |\n| `--snapshot VALUE` | Exact snapshot (default latest-stable) |\n| `--out VALUE` | Explicit generated fragment destination; refuses existing files |\n| `--workspace VALUE` | Workspace exposed to installed tools (default PREFIX/workspace) |\n| `--image VALUE` | Docker image tag override |\n| `--system VALUE` | Target system hint: `any`, `gui`, or `headless`. |\n| `--transport VALUE` | Runtime transport: stdio or HTTP. |\n| `--port VALUE` | HTTP listener port (default 8080) |\n| `--url VALUE` | Doctor URL for a running HTTP endpoint. |\n| `--non-interactive` | No prompts; missing/invalid arguments fail immediately |\n| `--runtime` | Alias for runtime, or docker-runtime with --mode docker |\n| `--with-runtime` | Also build runtime with an individual installation |\n| `--reconfigure` | Generate a new fragment from a verified existing installation |\n| `--dev` | Explicitly follow each snapshot's branch instead of its pin |\n| `--json` | Emit doctor output as JSON. |\n| `--help` | Show help |\n| `-h` | Alias for `--help`. |\n| `--without-model` | Explicitly omit the local Needle engine/model (core MCP only) |\n| `--without-system-deps` | Skip tmux probing and installation. |\n| `--nix` | Alias for `--mode nix`. |\n\u003c!-- hela:installer-flags:end -->\n\n## Measure persona and discovery payload size\n\nRun `node scripts/measure-overhead.mjs --profile \u003cid>` to print the generated base persona size and a profile's schema discovery projection in UTF-8 bytes. The projection uses each selected cell's first (default feature) catalog variant, namespace-qualified tool names, and compact JSON; it excludes JSON-RPC framing and runtime-only aliases. The rough token estimate uses `ceil(UTF-8 bytes / 4)`, not a model tokenizer, so treat the byte counts as reproducible and the token counts as an explicit approximation.\n\n## Managed layout\n\nDefault prefix: `${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/hela/installations/\u003cprofile>-\u003cmode>`. Use `--prefix /absolute/path` to choose another directory.\n\nEach prefix contains its own ecosystem metadata, sibling source repositories, verified installation manifest, workspace, private data directories, and generated client fragments. Plastid is cloned at the repository root and built from `ll3m-mcp/brain`.\n\nThe installer refuses unmanaged nonempty prefixes, dirty repositories, symlinked repositories, origin/pin/version mismatches, failed installs/builds, and existing explicit output files. A failed install retains a pending marker; doctor and launchers will not treat it as ready. Re-running the same installation performs locked dependency installation, rebuilds, and verifies it again. Existing data is not restored, reset, or deleted.\n\n## Individual servers\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode individual --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode individual --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nMerge the generated private fragment into the client file after review. Native client entries start an installed launcher, which selects the correct executable, workspace, and persistent data directory. Unrelated client/provider settings are preserved because the installer never overwrites them.\n\n## Single stdio runtime\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --client codex --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\n`--runtime` is an alias for `--mode runtime`. `--with-runtime` additionally builds the daemon for an individual installation without switching its client fragment to one connection.\n\nThe selected-profile catalog is generated from fresh probes. Runtime verification checks exact cold catalog parity and zero cell startup, including its HTTP transport. The first actual cell call starts only its target.\n\n## Docker: separate cells or runtime\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode docker --profile dev-workspace --client cursor --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode docker --profile dev-workspace\n\nnode setup.mjs --mode docker-runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode docker-runtime --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\n`--client docker` remains a shortcut for a Docker installation with Compose output. Use `--runtime` with it for the aggregated daemon. Both Docker modes build one profile image; client entries call Docker directly with the immutable image ID, launching separate cell containers or one runtime container. Those stdio clients do not need a host Node runtime after installation. `compose.json` provides the equivalent Compose services.\n\nFilesystem, terminal, and process tools operate inside the container and explicitly mounted workspace. Host SSH keys, device access, external browser engines, and Blender bridges are not silently copied or granted; configure those integrations deliberately.\n\nStdio needs attached stdin, not published ports. Compose services have `stdin_open: true`, no TTY, and persistent named volumes. Use `docker compose -f /path/to/compose.json run --rm --no-deps -T \u003cservice>` for an attached stdio connection; `up -d` alone does not connect an MCP client to stdio.\n\nDoctor starts uniquely named temporary containers with no host data/vault mounts or inherited provider keys, verifies actual MCP replies and image source provenance, and removes those containers. It does not stop existing containers or clean global images/volumes.\n\n## Nix runtime\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode nix --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode nix --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nNix supplies the pinned Node/native-build toolchain for cell builds and builds the runtime package. The installation's `nix-runtime` output link is retained as a GC root. This is a packaged runtime plus locked cell builds, not a claim that all ten cells are independent Nix derivations. Keep private files untracked; Git-backed flake references exclude ignored credentials and data from Nix source copies.\n\n## HTTP runtime\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --transport http --port 8080 --client codex --non-interactive\n# Start the installed launcher explicitly; its path is printed by setup:\nnode /path/to/prefix/hela-mcp-ecosystem/scripts/launch.mjs hela-runtime\nnode setup.mjs doctor --url http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp --json\n```\n\nThe same transport flags work with `--mode nix` and `--mode docker-runtime`. HTTP listeners bind to loopback on the host. Docker publishes only `127.0.0.1:\u003cport>`; `compose.json` includes the HTTP command and port mapping. Explicitly start it with Compose `up -d` if desired.\n\nHTTP fragments are supported for OpenCode, Kilo, Codex, and Cursor. Other client backends fail clearly rather than emit unverified HTTP syntax; use `--client skip` and configure their MCP URL manually. No listener is auto-started during installation. Doctor's private HTTP probe does not substitute for checking the running URL.\n\n## Updates and automatic checks\n\nManaged installations use the immutable `latest-stable` snapshot as their update contract. The controller compares exact cell revisions and declared package versions before changing anything; it does not follow moving cell branches and it never edits live client configuration.\n\nCheck an installation without changing it:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs update check --prefix /path/to/prefix\n```\n\nApply a reviewed update explicitly:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs update check --prefix /path/to/prefix\nnode setup.mjs update apply --prefix /path/to/prefix --confirm\nnode setup.mjs doctor --prefix /path/to/prefix --json\n```\n\nAn apply stages the release through the same pinned installer used for a clean install, records the previous manifest and release in `\u003cprefix>/.hela-update/rollback-\u003ctimestamp>/`, and restores the previous source revisions if verification fails. A successful result includes `restartRequired: true`; restart the runtime and MCP client before using the new build. `--confirm` is mandatory for both the CLI and the runtime agent's `hela.update.apply` tool.\n\nAutomatic checks are opt-in and check-only. They do not apply releases, restart services, or alter client files:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs update auto-check enable --prefix /path/to/prefix --interval-hours 24\nnode setup.mjs update auto-check status --prefix /path/to/prefix\nnode setup.mjs update auto-check disable --prefix /path/to/prefix\n```\n\nThe controller installs a systemd user timer on Linux, a launchd agent on macOS, or a Windows Task Scheduler job on native Windows. The scheduled command only writes the private `.hela-update-check.json` result cache. Network failures are reported as `unknown` and never trigger a mutation. Use `--json` for an agent or monitoring integration; remote metadata must use HTTPS and repository pins remain commit-validated.\n\n## Snapshots, reconfiguration, and failures\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode runtime --profile minimal --snapshot v1.5.1 --client skip --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs --reconfigure --prefix /path/to/existing/prefix --client zed --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --prefix /path/to/existing/prefix --json\n```\n\nSnapshots must declare exact commits and server versions. Incomplete historical metadata is rejected, not silently ignored. `--dev` follows recorded branches for host modes and records the resolved revisions/versions; Docker requires pinned snapshots. Optional provider keys are read from environment, not command-line secret flags. Generated fragments can contain those supplied credentials and are written with private permissions.\n\nReconfiguration infers the existing mode/profile/transport from an explicit prefix and only generates another fragment. To change transport or port, rerun installation with the new settings; a stdio fragment must not point at an HTTP-only launcher.\n\n## Switch modes in place\n\n`node setup.mjs mode` converts an existing managed installation to another execution mechanism without creating a second prefix or discarding data:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs mode --prefix /path/to/existing/prefix --mode runtime --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --prefix /path/to/existing/prefix --json\nnode setup.mjs mode --prefix /path/to/existing/prefix --mode docker-runtime --client opencode --non-interactive\n```\n\nEvery mode is a valid target: `individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, and `nix`. The profile, snapshot, workspace, and `data/` directories are preserved; only the artifacts the target mode needs are built. Host-mode switches reuse cells that already sit at the pinned revision with a clean worktree, so repeated conversions take seconds rather than rebuilding every package. Moving into a Docker mode builds the profile image; moving out deletes `compose.json`. Transport and port carry over unless `--transport` or `--port` is passed, and HTTP targets require a runtime mode. Switching to `--mode nix` must run inside Nix, exactly like a fresh Nix install.\n\nThe command verifies the converted installation with the same doctor checks used after installation and generates a new client fragment when `--client` is supplied; it never edits live client settings. If verification fails, the previous manifest is restored, a pending marker is written, and the command exits nonzero so doctor reports the degraded state. A pending or unmanaged prefix is refused, and re-selecting the current mode is an error rather than a silent no-op.\n\nDoctor exits nonzero for missing or incomplete installations, modified builds, wrong revisions/versions, missing images, failed MCP replies, and runtime catalog/HTTP failures. A remote `--url` check verifies MCP initialize/discovery and the runtime version, not remote filesystem pins or hardware. Existing standalone sibling/nested checkouts remain diagnosable when no managed installation is selected.\n\nNever overwrite live SQLite files or force vault conflicts to resolve installation warnings. Use validated backups and a deliberate migration if you intend to move existing memory into a new managed prefix.\n"},{"source":"docs/integration.md","title":"MCP client integration","summary":"The canonical inventory and selected profile drive configuration. Do not copy old repository paths or hand-maintain a second list of servers. Current versions and counts are in…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/integration.html","text":"# MCP client integration\n\n![Blotcat connecting desktop clients](../assets/blotcat-integration.jpg)\n\nThe canonical inventory and selected profile drive configuration. Do not copy old repository paths or hand-maintain a second list of servers. Current versions and counts are in [current versions](current-versions.md).\n\nThe installer now creates a managed prefix and private client fragment instead of replacing live client files. Use `--mode individual`, `runtime`, `docker`, `docker-runtime`, or `nix`; see [installation modes](installation.md). Review and merge the generated fragment. Its launcher supplies the installed executable, workspace, data location, and selected profile.\n\n## Generate configuration\n\nUse Node 22 LTS and build the selected cells first:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode runtime --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --non-interactive\n```\n\nTo inspect a generated file without installing it into a client:\n\n```bash\nnode scripts/generate-config.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client opencode \\\n  --root /absolute/path/to/MCPservers/hela-mcp-ecosystem --stdout\n```\n\nFor manually maintained repositories, `--root` points to the ecosystem checkout and cell directories normally sit beside it. For a managed installation, add `--prefix /path/to/prefix --mode \u003cmode>` to generate a fragment using its verified launchers and metadata. Plastid's package is `ll3m-mcp/brain`, not its Git repository root.\n\nOutput may contain credentials supplied through environment variables or options. Do not publish generated private configuration. The committed examples use placeholders and no real keys.\n\n## Client formats and locations\n\n- **Cursor:** `mcpServers` JSON in `~/.cursor/mcp.json`.\n- **Claude:** `mcpServers` JSON. Claude Desktop commonly uses `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` on macOS, `%APPDATA%/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` on Windows, and `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` on Linux; CLI integrations can use a different location. Confirm the file used by your installed client.\n- **Gemini / Antigravity:** `mcpServers` JSON; the installer supports the configured Gemini/Antigravity path. Confirm your client version's actual file before merging.\n- **OpenCode / Kilo:** `mcp` JSON. Local entries have `type: \"local\"` and a command array.\n- **Zed:** `context_servers` JSON in `~/.config/zed/settings.json`. Preserve unrelated settings and shared server definitions.\n- **Codex:** TOML under **`[mcp_servers.\u003cname>]`**, not `[mcpServers.\u003cname>]`, in `~/.codex/config.toml`.\n\nGenerate a private output file with `--out \u003cpath>` and review/merge it instead of replacing an existing client configuration blindly. Eight profiles and ten generator backends, including Docker and skip, are checked by the CLI matrix. Browser previews use placeholder paths; the CLI is the installation/configuration source.\n\n## Single-endpoint runtime\n\nKeep the selected profile and add the serve-mode flag:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime --non-interactive\n```\n\nThere is no `runtime` profile. `HELA_PROFILE` selects eligible cells and defaults to `minimal` when unset. Runtime startup and discovery spawn no cells; a call starts only its target. Standalone server definitions remain supported for installations that do not use the runtime.\n\nThe installer generates the selected-profile schema catalog. After rebuilding a cell:\n\n```bash\nnpm run catalog:generate -- --profile dev-workspace\nnode setup.mjs doctor --profile dev-workspace --runtime\n```\n\nSee [on-demand runtime](on-demand-runtime.md) for stale-cache refusal and explicit `hela.pipe` bootstrap. A healthy HTTP liveness endpoint alone is not full MCP or external-integration acceptance evidence.\n\n## Zed and Codex ACP ownership\n\nCodex-native MCP entries and Zed-forwarded entries can have the same name. A duplicate warning can refer to the forwarded duplicate rather than failure of the native entry. `codex mcp list` inspects configuration; it does not execute servers or prove connectivity.\n\nDo not remove shared top-level Zed definitions as a workaround: native Zed and other agents may need them. Native agent-profile permissions are not a per-ACP forwarding switch. Authenticated ACP sessions are verified without the GUI by `npm run test:acp` (`scripts/verify-acp.mjs`), which drives the same adapter Zed does; the adapter must be new enough for the account's model list — the registry build `codex-acp` 0.11.1 fails every model with `model_not_found`, while 2.7.0 works (see [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md#codex-acp-every-model-fails-with-model_not_found)). See the adapter-version-specific guidance in [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md#zed-forwards-a-server-that-codex-already-defines); the optional merge override is not enabled by this guide.\n\n## Keys and optional services\n\nGitHub tokens are optional for live remote prompt/catalog operations. Enzyme can use keyless network fallbacks when Google CSE credentials are absent; configured CSE uses `GOOGLE_API_KEY` and **`GOOGLE_CSE_ID`**. Model-backed tools require their configured engine/provider. SQLite remains Genome's base engine; Obsidian and Redis are optional. Read [keys and services](keys-and-secrets.md) and the selected server's generated canonical tool reference.\n\n## Docker installations\n\nUse the installer to build and verify a selected-profile image before generating/using its client fragment:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --mode docker --profile headless-server --client docker --non-interactive\nnode setup.mjs doctor --mode docker --profile headless-server\n```\n\n`compose.json` uses the built image ID, actual entrypoints, attached stdio without a TTY, and persistent volumes. `--mode docker-runtime` selects one runtime container. Add `--transport http` only for runtime-based modes; the installer generates loopback-only HTTP settings and leaves listener startup explicit. The older seven-cell `config/docker-compose.yml` is historical scaffolding, not the supported installer path. Hardware and provider integrations are still separate.\n\n## Acceptance and reloads\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs doctor --profile dev-workspace --runtime\nnpm run test:matrix\nnpm run docs:check\n```\n\nUse `--runtime` only when that runtime is installed. Run full-stack integration/profile tests on a machine with the required cells, or select the installed subset. Reload only the relevant clients after review; some clients auto-reload immediately on settings changes. Back up live databases/vaults before changing synchronization hooks. Do not restore over active SQLite files, force a vault conflict winner, or kill unrelated processes during verification.\n\nPowerful filesystem, command, browser, and agent tools run with their server account's privileges. Current verification is not a security sandbox or authenticated-public-endpoint guarantee; see [security status](security-audit.md).\n"},{"source":"docs/keys-and-secrets.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Keys & Secrets Guide","summary":"This document explains prerequisites and optional provider credentials. Keyless operation is not the same as offline operation or a guarantee of free/unlimited external…","category":"gettingStarted","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/keys-and-secrets.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Keys & Secrets Guide\n\n![Blotcat Secrets & Credentials Vault](../assets/blotcat-secrets.jpg)\n\nThis document explains prerequisites and optional provider credentials. Keyless operation is not the same as offline operation or a guarantee of free/unlimited external services. See [current versions](current-versions.md) for software metadata and [security status](security-audit.md) for unresolved access-control work.\n\n---\n\n## 1. System Prerequisites\n\nBefore running setup, ensure your system meets the baseline requirements:\n\n- **Node.js:** 22 LTS is the tested full-stack environment. Enzyme requires at least 20.18.1 and Receptor requires 22; other packages declare their own standalone ranges.\n- **npm:** use the version bundled with the selected Node runtime and install locked dependencies with `npm ci`; no global npm upgrade is required by this guide.\n- **Git:** use a current version (release snapshots specify at least 2.30).\n- **SQLite:** Genome uses Node SQLite bindings; the SQLite CLI is optional for inspection. Do not replace active database files during upgrades.\n- **Docker/Compose:** optional, and generated Compose files are scaffolds requiring transport/device/persistence review.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Keys & Secrets Catalog\n\nProvider credentials are optional for MCP discovery and keyless capabilities. A selected external operation can still require credentials, network access, an installed local engine, or a live external service.\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        HELA MCP KEY MATRIX                             │\n├───────────────────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────────┬───────────┤\n│ Secret Name           │ Target Component     │ Necessity   │ Fallback  │\n├───────────────────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────────┼───────────┤\n│ OPENROUTER_API_KEY    │ HeLa Mitosis         │ Optional    │ Local*    │\n│ GITHUB_TOKEN          │ HeLa Mitosis         │ Optional    │ Bundled   │\n│ GOOGLE_API_KEY        │ HeLa Enzyme          │ Optional    │ Wikipedia │\n│ GOOGLE_CSE_ID         │ HeLa Enzyme          │ Optional    │ Wikipedia │\n└───────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────────┴───────────┘\n```\n\n`Local*` depends on the bundled/configured Needle engine and model. The matrix is not a guarantee that every tool can complete without its required provider or hardware.\n\n---\n\n### 1. `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`\n\n* **Target Component**: **HeLa Mitosis** (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)\n* **Purpose**: Powers dynamic LLM intelligence for task decomposition (`llm_decompose_task`), AI route ranking (`llm_suggest_route`), and query routing (`llm_query`).\n* **Where to get it**:\n  1. Visit [OpenRouter API Keys](https://openrouter.ai/keys).\n  2. Create a free account.\n  3. Generate an API key (`sk-or-v1-...`).\n  4. Provider model availability, quotas, and pricing can change; check the provider before relying on a free route.\n* **If you DO NOT have it**:\n   * **Consequence**: Provider-backed calls need some configured provider credentials; another configured provider can still be used when supported. Local planning can work without keys when its engine/model are available.\n   * **Fallback Behavior**: Needle planning/execution and budgeted escalation are separate. Unavailable escalation can hand control back to the caller; `brainstorming` needs a generative model and fails when none is configured. This is not a universal latency, crash-freedom, or offline guarantee.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `GITHUB_TOKEN`\n\n* **Target Component**: **HeLa Mitosis** (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)\n* **Purpose**: Synchronizes the latest community prompt collections, guidelines, and chatmodes from GitHub repositories at runtime.\n* **Where to get it**:\n  1. Visit [GitHub Personal Access Tokens](https://github.com/settings/tokens).\n  2. Generate a token with public read-only access.\n* **If you DO NOT have it**:\n  * **Consequence**: Live GitHub syncing is disabled.\n   * **Fallback Behavior**: Bundled prompt/instruction catalogs remain available. This does not make unrelated research, browser, provider, or remote-runtime operations offline.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `GOOGLE_API_KEY` & `GOOGLE_CSE_ID`\n\n* **Target Component**: **HeLa Enzyme** (`hela-enzyme` / `researcher-mcp`)\n* **Purpose**: Optional Tier 1 web search queries via the Google Custom Search JSON API.\n* **Where to get them**:\n   1. **API Key**: Use [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials) and check current Custom Search API availability and quotas for your account.\n  2. **CSE ID**: Create a Search Engine ID at [Programmable Search Engine](https://programmablesearchengine.google.com/controlpanel/all) (set Search the entire web = ON).\n* **If you DO NOT have them**:\n   * **Consequence**: Configured Google CSE is unavailable; supported keyless network fallbacks may still work. Network failures, rate limits, and provider availability remain possible.\n  * **Fallback Behavior**: Live web search cascades to keyless parallel search (DuckDuckGo, Bing, etc.) and Wikipedia fallback. `academic_search` queries arXiv's public OpenSearch API, `news_monitor` queries Google News RSS feeds, `search_trends` queries Hacker News Algolia, and content extraction falls back to Jina Reader (`r.jina.ai`).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Configuration & Diagnostics\n\n### Initial Setup\nRun the setup script:\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs\n```\nThe installer reads optional provider keys from environment and never prompts for or accepts secret values as CLI flags. Keyless defaults can still use the network. Generated private client fragments can contain supplied credentials; review them before sharing, and merge them into client settings deliberately.\n\n### Reconfiguring Keys Anytime\nTo update keys or change clients on an installed ecosystem:\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --reconfigure\n```\n\n### Safe Diagnostic Health Check\nTo verify your environment and check which fallbacks are active without exposing keys in logs:\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs doctor\n```\n"},{"source":"docs/on-demand-runtime.md","title":"On-demand runtime and canonical discovery","summary":"Blotcat waking one selected cell while the other runtime cells sleep","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/on-demand-runtime.html","text":"# On-demand runtime and canonical discovery\n\n![Blotcat waking one selected cell while the other runtime cells sleep](../assets/blotcat-on-demand-runtime.png)\n\nThe runtime implements §10.3: no cell starts during runtime startup or `tools/list`. The first call starts only its target; concurrent calls share that startup. Cells already started retain the existing crash/timeout supervisor. Standalone MCP servers are unchanged, all eight profiles remain supported, and the runtime/installer default is `minimal` when no profile is selected.\n\nThe same endpoint owns profile-aware agent instructions. `prompts/list` exposes `hela-profile-bootstrap`, `prompts/get` renders it from the selected profile, and `resources/read` serves `hela://profile/context`. On a clean workspace, installation also creates a private profile-aware HeLa persona under `agent_persona/` and links the harness entrypoints to it. HeLa is the distributable synthesis of Azzar's private Frea and Mema personal AI assistant personas: Frea contributes autonomous technical execution, while Mema contributes security and continuity discipline. Their private files, memories, credentials, and identifiers are not distributed. Existing `agent_persona/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `GEMINI.md` files are preserved; the runtime context remains authoritative for active organelles. No individual cell deployment is required.\n\n## One inventory, generated schemas and counts\n\n`config/inventory.json` remains the source of cell paths, revisions, versions, environment declarations, and conditional tool features. `config/profiles.json` selects membership. Config generation, doctor, version verification, integration, and profile tests use the same inventory loader/path resolution.\n\nThe generated `config/tool-catalog.json` contains actual standalone MCP schemas, revision/version metadata, verified build digests, and feature variants. It is a derived artifact, not another server registry. Mitosis currently has four LLM/agent feature combinations; other cells have one schema variant. The generator enables registration flags only for discovery and never invokes model-backed tools.\n\nBuild outputs can differ between machines even at one source revision. Multiple digests are retained together only if fresh probes return identical schemas for every declared variant. New or changed conditional tool registration must declare its switches under the cell's `toolFeatures`; other relevant settings belong in its `env` list. Configuration changes require a new runtime instance.\n\nRun from the ecosystem checkout after building the selected cells and runtime:\n\n```bash\nnpm run catalog:generate -- --profile dev-workspace\nnpm run catalog:check -- --profile dev-workspace\nnode setup.mjs doctor --profile dev-workspace --runtime\nnpm run test:profiles -- --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nThe installer refreshes selected-profile catalog entries when installing the runtime. Partial installations do not need unrelated hardware cells. An installation with all cells can omit the profile argument to generate/check all entries.\n\nThe same generator writes [profile-count documentation](generated-profile-counts.md) and `docs/site/data/tool-counts.json`. Counts include `hela.pipe` and the optional agent alias; they are feature-dependent ranges, not fixed totals copied into each client configuration.\n\n## Cache isolation and stale metadata\n\nThe format-2 private cache separates ecosystem roots, profiles, and namespace modes. Per-cell fingerprints cover declared and actual revisions, build contents, effective feature flags, and declared environment settings. Cache files contain fingerprints and schemas only, not environment values. Atomic publication prevents partial reads.\n\nLegacy, corrupt, and mismatched cache entries are ignored. A valid generated catalog supplies cold schemas without child processes. Missing/stale catalog entries are not advertised. Unknown calls do not scan other cells. Explicit `hela.pipe` routing can start an uncatalogued named cell and learn its schemas; regenerating the catalog restores full cold discovery.\n\nDoctor validates selected paths/builds, real isolated MCP handshakes, package/inventory/snapshot versions and revisions, and—when `--runtime` is requested—exact catalog/tool agreement with zero discovery spawns. Hardware checks are limited to the selected profile and remain separate from MCP readiness. API-key checks report presence, never values or assumed provider readiness.\n\n## Verification scope\n\n- **32/32** runtime regressions pass on x86_64 Linux and native ARM, covering timeout recovery, lazy startup, 24 concurrent first calls, cache invalidation, profile/root/namespace switching, legacy/corrupt caches, explicit bootstrap, and shutdown. **41 fixture PIDs** per run were verified reaped.\n- All eight profiles pass **16/16** namespace-off/on discovery/schema/routing checks. Discovery starts zero cells; only the safe Genome/Nucleus probes start those cells. Cold discovery, cold first-call, and warm-call durations are recorded in JSON output without asserting machine-independent latency limits.\n- The **80** client-configuration combinations, four probe regressions, and four canonical inventory regressions pass. Default standalone integration now excludes the multiplexing runtime from collision comparisons.\n- Eight additional runtime discovery checks cover all four Mitosis feature combinations in both namespace modes, without invoking model tools or starting cells. Doctor reports READY with cold catalog parity for all eight profiles.\n- Reproducible Nix packages use Node 22.23.4 and pinned/fixed-output inputs on both Linux architectures. Runtime versions derive from package metadata.\n\nThese checks do not authenticate GUI ACP sessions, reconcile live vault conflicts, exercise Android/Blender hardware, invoke model providers, or complete deferred endpoint security. Lappy retains its ten-server roster; servy retains its eight-cell `dev-workspace` selection.\n\n## Updating from the endpoint\n\nThe runtime exposes the update controller directly, so an agent can maintain the cells it uses without shelling out: `hela.update.check` is read-only and reports the installed snapshot against the latest one, and `hela.update.apply` is confirmation-gated, stages the release through the pinned installer, verifies it with doctor, and keeps a rollback directory. Both return machine-readable JSON; `apply` reports `restartRequired: true` when the new build is on disk.\n\n## Historical Servy rollout — runtime 1.2.0\n\nThis section records the deployed Servy HTTP service, which remains on runtime `1.2.0`. The current source checkout and direct stdio runtime are `1.2.1`; this evidence is retained because the systemd deployment has not been promoted to that source version. Runtime source `9c92dbf5612db43fe80f1680ba4af97c4b5ea9b0` and ecosystem source release `1c47ca1c202c6a2acea79d7c6fdac5a19ef3c0bf` are deployed. Final-source Nix outputs are:\n\n- x86_64-linux: `/nix/store/r15rmywrswf01nj2xrvx753p1fh4f6rh-hela-runtime-1.2.0`.\n- aarch64-linux: `/nix/store/bhm781bld50yi9brmy2mn74p1ybzdzcy-hela-runtime-1.2.0`.\n\nThe ARM package is rooted under `/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/deployments/todo103-psnzx8x4/runtime`. Promotion fast-forwarded clean source checkouts, retained the old build/drop-in, and took fresh integrity-checked online backups of three databases plus a vault archive. Existing profile, environment, host, port, and security settings were preserved; only the selected runtime executable changed.\n\nLocal and public HTTP initialize/discovery report runtime **1.2.0**, **236 unique tools**, all three Genome mission tools, and no hardware-only tools. Both discovery checks left the service with **zero child cells**. A safe `terminal_session_list` call started only Nucleus; Genome did not start. Observed Nucleus calls were approximately **471 ms cold** and **13 ms warm** in this one run, including HTTP/MCP overhead—not a portable benchmark.\n\nDeployed doctor reports READY, catalog parity is exact, revision checks pass, and the GC root is verified. All previous HTTP-service PIDs exited. All **633** pre-rollout conflict archive files retained their hashes. No database file was restored/replaced, and unrelated client-owned runtimes and the legacy PM2 gateway were not stopped.\n\nThe rollout directory contains `manifest.json`, `http-verification.json`, `doctor.log`, `versions.log`, `catalog.log`, `backup/`, `rollback-dist/`, and the package GC root. Native committed-source staging/test evidence remains under `/home/ubuntu/.local/state/hela/staging/todo103-release-ah25pauz/`; local test/measurement logs are under `/var/tmp/scratch/opencode/todo103/`. Authenticated ACP, deliberate external-client reloads, gateway consumer review, vault reconciliation, and deferred security remain separate.\n"},{"source":"docs/prebuilt-skills.md","title":"Prebuilt agent skills","summary":"HeLa MCP servers can ship companion agent skills alongside their MCP tools. Skills provide task workflows and reference material to compatible agent harnesses; they do not add…","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/prebuilt-skills.html","text":"# Prebuilt agent skills\n\nHeLa MCP servers can ship companion agent skills alongside their MCP tools. Skills provide task workflows and reference material to compatible agent harnesses; they do not add MCP tools or change server authorization. The skill list belongs to each server's `skills` array in [`config/inventory.json`](../config/inventory.json), and selected profiles determine which servers' skills are eligible.\n\n## Server catalog\n\n| Server | Bundled skills | Source |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mitosis** (`hela-mitosis`) | [`skill-finder`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp/tree/master/skills/skill-finder), [`human-writing`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp/tree/master/skills/human-writing), [`caveman`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp/tree/master/skills/caveman), [`mema-vault`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp/tree/master/skills/mema-vault), [`blotcat-illustrations`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp/tree/master/skills/blotcat-illustrations), [`pcb`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp/tree/master/skills/pcb) | [`chaining-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/chaining-mcp) |\n| **Genome** (`hela-genome`) | [`code-reviewer`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp/tree/master/skills/code-reviewer), [`guardian-cache`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp/tree/master/skills/guardian-cache), [`guardian-memory`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp/tree/master/skills/guardian-memory), [`guardian-security`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp/tree/master/skills/guardian-security), [`guardian-session`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp/tree/master/skills/guardian-session), [`guardian-tracker`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp/tree/master/skills/guardian-tracker), [`guardian-wall`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp/tree/master/skills/guardian-wall) | [`project-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/project-mcp) |\n| **Cytosol** (`hela-cytosol`) | [`browser-agent`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/browser-mcp/tree/master/skill/browser-agent) | [`browser-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/browser-mcp) |\n| **Phenotype** (`hela-phenotype`) | [`logo-design`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/tree/master/skills/logo-design), [`motion-designer`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/tree/master/skills/motion-designer), [`brand-architecture`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/tree/master/skills/brand-architecture), [`modern-web-guidance`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/tree/master/skills/modern-web-guidance), [`video`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/tree/master/skills/video) | [`designer-mcp`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp) |\n| **Membrane, Nucleus, Ribosome, Enzyme, Receptor, Plastid, Runtime** | No bundled agent skills declared in the current inventory. | See their repositories in [`config/inventory.json`](../config/inventory.json). |\n\n## Profile availability\n\nEvery profile includes Mitosis and Genome, so their 13 skills are available in every profile. The specialist skills below are available only when the profile includes their server:\n\n| Profile | Additional skill server(s) | Additional skills |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `minimal` | — | — |\n| `dev-workspace` | Cytosol, Phenotype | `browser-agent`, `logo-design`, `motion-designer`, `brand-architecture`, `modern-web-guidance`, `video` |\n| `headless-server` | Phenotype | `logo-design`, `motion-designer`, `brand-architecture`, `modern-web-guidance`, `video` |\n| `research` | Cytosol | `browser-agent` |\n| `web-devops` | Cytosol, Phenotype | `browser-agent`, `logo-design`, `motion-designer`, `brand-architecture`, `modern-web-guidance`, `video` |\n| `android-testing` | — | — |\n| `3d-modeling` | — | — |\n| `all` | Cytosol, Phenotype | `browser-agent`, `logo-design`, `motion-designer`, `brand-architecture`, `modern-web-guidance`, `video` |\n\nThe inventory and profile definitions are the source of truth. Regenerate or review this mapping after changing either `skills[]` in `config/inventory.json` or membership in `config/profiles.json`.\n\n## How skills are installed and refreshed\n\n- A skill is declared on its owning server with a repository-relative `path`. Its source directory must contain `SKILL.md`.\n- `npm run skills:sync -- --profile \u003cid>` mirrors skills from servers in that profile to the configured harness skills directory. Use `--all` for the complete catalog. The sync reads the installed source checkouts, so a profile without Phenotype does not receive Phenotype skills.\n- `npm run skills:check -- --profile \u003cid>` reports missing, changed, or duplicate skill copies without writing. Run the corresponding `skills:sync` command after updating server checkouts.\n- Managed installations pin each server to a commit in their selected snapshot. The tracked `config/inventory.json` pins the source snapshot; release snapshots preserve their original commits and are not rewritten to absorb later skill changes.\n- Skills are an agent-harness companion layer. A server may have tools without a skill, and an installed skill does not imply that its MCP server is active in the selected profile.\n\n## Modern Web Guidance pin and requirements\n\nPhenotype **3.0.3**, pinned in `config/inventory.json` to commit [`d9bf96d460f8de3adc5d5627efd76f8f23d8ee2a`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/tree/d9bf96d460f8de3adc5d5627efd76f8f23d8ee2a), bundles the Apache-2.0 skill from [GoogleChrome/modern-web-guidance](https://github.com/GoogleChrome/modern-web-guidance) at release `v1.0.0`, commit [`4dd968349e54ca45a05dd5c818fb035c9054b022`](https://github.com/GoogleChrome/modern-web-guidance/tree/4dd968349e54ca45a05dd5c818fb035c9054b022). The bundled skill pins the CLI to `modern-web-guidance@1.0.0` and the guidance corpus to `2026_11_09-5bcc021b`. The skill and license are included in the Phenotype checkout and synced with its other skills; querying guidance uses the CLI and therefore needs Node.js 22+ and outbound network access. See the skill's [`UPSTREAM.md`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/blob/d9bf96d460f8de3adc5d5627efd76f8f23d8ee2a/skills/modern-web-guidance/UPSTREAM.md) and [`SKILL.md`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/designer-mcp/blob/d9bf96d460f8de3adc5d5627efd76f8f23d8ee2a/skills/modern-web-guidance/SKILL.md).\n\nUpdating this skill requires refreshing the upstream skill files and license, verifying the CLI and corpus pins, updating Phenotype's installed-skills catalog, then recording the new Phenotype commit in the ecosystem inventory. Keep immutable historical snapshots unchanged.\n"},{"source":"docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.md","title":"All-profile verification — historical record (2026-11-06)","summary":"This historical verification predates the current minimal default. At the time of this run, the runtime default was dev-workspace; current installations default to minimal…","category":"archive","archived":true,"link":"library/docs/profile-verification-2026-11-06.html","text":"# All-profile verification — historical record (2026-11-06)\n\nThis historical verification predates the current `minimal` default. At the time of this run, the runtime default was `dev-workspace`; current installations default to `minimal` unless a profile is selected. HeLa changes apply to the shared servers/runtime, not a hardcoded lappy or servy configuration. Each installation keeps its selected profile.\n\nMachine configurations were preserved:\n\n- Lappy has all ten standalone HeLa servers, equivalent to the `all` roster. Ten native Codex definitions were confirmed without printing environment values or credentials. This does not mean every client uses a single `HELA_PROFILE=all` runtime.\n- Servy's systemd HTTP runtime selects `dev-workspace`, eight cells. The profile is not named `dev-workflow`. Receptor and Plastid remain intentionally absent there.\n- Other installations can select any of the eight profiles in `config/profiles.json`.\n\n## Acceptance checks\n\nThe new `npm run test:profiles` command passed on lappy for all eight profiles: `minimal`, `dev-workspace`, `headless-server`, `research`, `web-devops`, `android-testing`, `3d-modeling`, and `all`.\n\nEach profile runs twice, with namespacing off and on: **16/16 runtime checks**. The verifier obtains actual tool schemas from the ten standalone cells, then checks that each runtime:\n\n- Advertises exactly the selected cells. Runtime 1.2.0 starts none during discovery and only starts the safely invoked cells during routing checks; see the [on-demand follow-up](on-demand-runtime.md).\n- Exposes exactly their tools plus `hela.pipe`, without collisions or out-of-profile tools.\n- Preserves each tool's input schema, including namespaced tool names.\n- Includes all three Genome mission tools.\n- Routes a safe Genome read-only call, plus a Nucleus session-list call where selected.\n\nWith model-backed features disabled, the `all` runtime exposes **291 tools**: 290 standalone tools plus `hela.pipe`. `dev-workspace` exposes **230** under the same conditions. Servy's feature-enabled live runtime exposes **236**; those feature-dependent differences are not profile failures.\n\nStrict package/initialize/inventory/snapshot/revision verification also passes for all ten cells and runtime. The client-generation matrix passes **80/80** profile/client combinations. Probe regressions pass **4/4**, including explicit profile/namespacing isolation from the host environment.\n\nAll probes use disposable HOME/project/central-memory locations, excluding inherited credentials and live vault hooks. Model, Android, and Blender operations were not invoked. These checks establish MCP startup/discovery/schema/routing support, not hardware integration or authenticated GUI sessions. Published release snapshots were not rewritten to backdate this additional evidence.\n\n## Repeating the checks\n\nFrom the ecosystem checkout, using Node 22:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test:profiles\nnpm run test:profiles -- --json\nnode scripts/verify-versions.mjs --profile all\nnpm run test:matrix\nnpm run test:probe\n```\n\nThe full matrix requires all ten cells to be installed and built. A machine with only one selected profile can test that profile without installing unrelated hardware cells:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test:profiles -- --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nMissing required builds fail explicitly rather than silently skipping a profile. This command starts isolated test processes; it does not rewrite client configuration, change the live profile, or restart a service.\n"},{"source":"docs/profiles.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem — Profile & Agent Persona Catalog","summary":"Blotcat Profile Selection & Specialization","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/profiles.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem — Profile & Agent Persona Catalog\n\n![Blotcat Profile Selection & Specialization](../assets/blotcat-profiles.jpg)\n\nThe HeLa MCP Ecosystem organizes capabilities into **agent-oriented profiles**. Each profile pairs the two foundational backbone servers (**HeLa Mitosis** for orchestration and **HeLa Genome** for persistent state) with a tailored set of specialized capability servers.\n\n## HeLa Persona\n\nWhen installation targets a clean workspace, the installer creates a private, profile-aware HeLa persona under `agent_persona/` and links the harness entrypoints to it. The scaffold contains `IDENTITY.md`, `SOUL.md`, `TOOLS.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and `USER.md`.\n\nFrea and Mema are Azzar's personal AI assistant personas. They are private persona definitions, not HeLa organelles, hosted services, model providers, or installation dependencies:\n\n* **Frea** is Azzar's autonomous technical problem-solving persona, centered on initiative, deep context, adaptive communication, and protective honesty.\n* **Mema** is Azzar's security and continuity-focused persona, centered on vault-first credential handling, reproducible operations, resilient state, and disciplined memory.\n\nHeLa is the distributable ecosystem persona that synthesizes their documented operating principles for the selected profile. It does not copy Frea's or Mema's private files, memories, biography, credentials, paths, or identifiers. As a harness-level guide, it is not a replacement for the runtime: it reads the selected `HELA_PROFILE`, uses the runtime's `hela://profile/context` resource or `hela-profile-bootstrap` prompt as the authority for active organelles, and keeps routing within that profile's capability boundary. If no profile is selected, the installer and runtime use `minimal`.\n\nExisting `agent_persona/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `GEMINI.md` files are preserved. HeLa does not overwrite an existing persona, copy private biography or credentials into a new workspace, or distribute machine-specific paths and identifiers.\n\n---\n\n## The Backbone Foundation\n\nEvery profile in the ecosystem includes the core dual backbone:\n\n* **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis` / `chaining-mcp`)**: Intelligent tool discovery, Needle-planned routing, Needle-backed sequential thinking, task decomposition, skills catalog, and workflow orchestration.\n* **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome` / `project-mcp`)**: Living SQLite knowledge graph (`memory.db`), task tracking, architectural decisions, and cross-session context restoration.\n\n![Blotcat carrying runtime memory between context nodes](../assets/blotcat-illustrations/03-project-guardian-memory.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## Profile Catalog\n\nThere are eight profiles. The canonical membership is `config/profiles.json`; current versions and generated tool counts are in [current versions](current-versions.md) and [profile counts](generated-profile-counts.md).\n\nAgent skills bundled by MCP server are selected with the same profile. See the [prebuilt skills catalog](prebuilt-skills.md) for the complete server-to-skill mapping, profile availability, and sync behavior.\n\n![Blotcat sorting profile choices between GUI and headless work](../assets/blotcat-illustrations/01-profile-selection.jpg)\n\n### Profile video explainers\n\nThese short walkthroughs introduce each profile's server roster, typical workflow, host requirements, and trade-offs:\n\n* [`minimal` — Research & Memory](https://youtu.be/Ruq-Gm0hmcg)\n* [`dev-workspace` — Full Desktop Engineering](https://youtu.be/sdRqv7JhPjM)\n* [`headless-server` — Operations Without a Desktop](https://youtu.be/wHRFYXymeeE)\n* [`research` — From Sources to Synthesis](https://youtu.be/5Lis8z24tks)\n* [`web-devops` — Frontend & Browser QA](https://youtu.be/StGeqfP7Cyg)\n* [`android-testing` — Real Device QA](https://youtu.be/nPCrcBSEUeA)\n* [`3d-modeling` — Blender Asset Creation](https://youtu.be/f92PId5t5ek)\n* [`all` — Every Capability in One Profile](https://youtu.be/oqIucJ8OYCI)\n\n### `minimal` — HeLa Minimal\n\nThree servers: `hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`, and `hela-enzyme`. This is the smallest research/memory stack; it does not require browser, Android, or Blender dependencies. Keyless research still uses the network.\n\n### 1. `dev-workspace` — HeLa Dev Workspace\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Desktop Workstation\n* **Agent Persona:** Full-Stack Software Engineer & Desktop Workstation Agent\n* **Purpose:** Complete autonomous software development, testing, UI design, and browser verification.\n* **Included Servers (8 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`), `HeLa Ribosome` (`hela-ribosome`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n  * Design & Web: `HeLa Phenotype` (`hela-phenotype`), `HeLa Cytosol` (`hela-cytosol`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores project history and open tasks.\n  2. Mitosis decomposes feature requirements into steps.\n  3. Membrane and Nucleus inspect code, run builds, and execute tests.\n  4. Phenotype generates design tokens and Tailwind components.\n  5. Cytosol validates web UI through Playwright, accessibility trees, screenshots, and local Mirage visual analysis.\n  6. Genome records decisions, test results, and observations.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `headless-server` — HeLa Headless Server (Core 7)\n\n* **Target System:** Headless Linux Server / CI/CD Runner / Remote VM\n* **Agent Persona:** Cloud Infrastructure, Headless Server & CI/CD Automation Agent\n* **Purpose:** Autonomous server management, CI/CD pipeline execution, background harness orchestration, and repository maintenance without requiring display servers.\n* **Included Servers (7 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`), `HeLa Ribosome` (`hela-ribosome`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n  * Design: `HeLa Phenotype` (`hela-phenotype`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores environment state.\n  2. Mitosis plans deployment or maintenance sequence.\n  3. Ribosome spawns and monitors long-running background PTY tasks.\n  4. Nucleus executes terminal commands and RTK-optimized queries.\n  5. Enzyme researches technical documentation or package dependencies.\n  6. Genome tracks task completion.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `research` — HeLa Research Terminal\n\n* **Target System:** Headless / Desktop\n* **Agent Persona:** Deep Research Investigator & Technical Knowledge Synthesizer\n* **Purpose:** Multi-source research, web scraping, literature analysis, fact-checking, and structured documentation generation.\n* **Included Servers (5 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`)\n  * Interaction: `HeLa Cytosol` (`hela-cytosol`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores research topic and prior facts.\n  2. Mitosis constructs research query plan.\n  3. Enzyme queries Google Search and Wikipedia.\n  4. Cytosol extracts full article content from web pages and can inspect image content when needed.\n  5. Membrane writes research synthesis to markdown files.\n  6. Genome stores discovered facts into the knowledge graph.\n\n---\n\n### 4. `web-devops` — HeLa Web Dev + Browser Automation\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Desktop\n* **Agent Persona:** Frontend Engineer & Web Verification Specialist\n* **Purpose:** Frontend component creation, design token synchronization, automated browser testing, and visual regression checking.\n* **Included Servers (6 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`)\n  * Design: `HeLa Phenotype` (`hela-phenotype`)\n  * Interaction: `HeLa Cytosol` (`hela-cytosol`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores frontend specifications.\n  2. Phenotype generates OKLCH tokens and Tailwind configuration.\n  3. Membrane creates component files.\n  4. Nucleus starts local development server.\n  5. Cytosol navigates, interacts, captures screenshots, and performs visual or standalone-image verification.\n  6. Genome records UI validation results.\n\n---\n\n### 5. `android-testing` — HeLa Android Testing Rig\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Mobile Automation Host (requires `adb` and USB/Wi-Fi connected device)\n* **Agent Persona:** Mobile QA Tester & Android Device Automation Engineer\n* **Purpose:** Mobile application testing, UI interaction, ADB automation, and mobile debugging.\n* **Included Servers (5 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`)\n  * Mobile: `HeLa Receptor` (`hela-receptor`)\n  * Knowledge: `HeLa Enzyme` (`hela-enzyme`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores issue ticket.\n  2. Nucleus inspects build outputs and logcat streams.\n  3. Receptor connects to device, inspects UI XML tree, and executes tap/swipe gestures.\n  4. Enzyme investigates error codes or crash logs.\n  5. Genome records reproduction steps and bug resolution.\n\n---\n\n### 6. `3d-modeling` — HeLa 3D / Blender Station\n\n* **Target System:** GUI / Workstation (requires local Blender 3.6+ installation)\n* **Agent Persona:** Generative 3D Artist & Blender Scene Architect\n* **Purpose:** Autonomous 3D modeling, procedural mesh generation, material assignment, scene composition, and rendering in Blender.\n* **Included Servers (5 Servers):**\n  * Backbone: `HeLa Mitosis` (`hela-mitosis`), `HeLa Genome` (`hela-genome`)\n  * 3D: `HeLa Plastid` (`hela-plastid`)\n  * Workspace: `HeLa Membrane` (`hela-membrane`), `HeLa Nucleus` (`hela-nucleus`)\n* **Standard Workflow:**\n  1. Genome restores 3D project requirements.\n  2. Mitosis breaks down 3D asset generation stages.\n  3. Plastid generates and executes Python scripts inside Blender.\n  4. Membrane saves generated `.blend` scene and rendered `.png` images.\n  5. Genome records asset metadata and scene relations.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `all` — HeLa All (Full 10-MCP Stack)\n\n* **Target System:** Any / Fully-Equipped Workstation\n* **Agent Persona:** Complete HeLa Autonomous Engineering Multi-Agent Stack\n* **Purpose:** Unrestricted access to all 10 servers in the ecosystem.\n* **Included Servers (10 Servers):**\n  * `hela-mitosis`, `hela-genome`, `hela-membrane`, `hela-nucleus`, `hela-ribosome`, `hela-enzyme`, `hela-cytosol`, `hela-phenotype`, `hela-receptor`, `hela-plastid`\n\n### Runtime serve mode (orthogonal to profiles)\n\nThe HeLa Runtime is **not a profile** — it is a serve method. Any profile can be served through the runtime:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --profile dev-workspace --client opencode --runtime   # builds dev-workspace cells + daemon\nHELA_PROFILE=minimal node hela-runtime/dist/index.js             # run with minimal profile\n```\n\nThe runtime advertises only the selected cells' schemas and starts each cell only when invoked. Discovery starts none. Use `--runtime` with any profile; `HELA_PROFILE` selects eligible cells and defaults to `minimal` when unset. Standalone servers remain supported.\n\n---\n\n## Profile Compatibility Matrix:\n\n| Profile ID | Target System | Total Servers | Backbone Servers | Headless Compatible | Requires External Runtime / Device |\n|---|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|---|\n| `minimal` | Any / Headless | 3 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes | Network for online research |\n| `dev-workspace` | GUI / Desktop | 8 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes (Playwright) | Playwright / Chromium |\n| `headless-server` | Headless | 7 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes | None |\n| `research` | Headless / GUI | 5 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes | None |\n| `web-devops` | GUI / Desktop | 6 | Mitosis + Genome | Yes (Playwright) | Playwright / Chromium |\n| `android-testing` | Device Host | 5 | Mitosis + Genome | No | `adb` + Android Device |\n| `3d-modeling` | Workstation | 5 | Mitosis + Genome | No | Blender CLI |\n| `all` | Full Host | 10 | Mitosis + Genome | Partial | Playwright, `adb`, Blender |\n"},{"source":"docs/reliability.md","title":"HeLa MCP Reliability Contract","summary":"How the MCP stack is wired, what breaks it, and how to repair it. Written after the 2026-11-20 outage where apt-get autoremove purged nodejs and every MCP host failed with…","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/reliability.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Reliability Contract\n\nHow the MCP stack is wired, what breaks it, and how to repair it. Written after the\n2026-11-20 outage where `apt-get autoremove` purged `nodejs` and every MCP host\nfailed with `NotFound: ChildProcess.spawn (/usr/bin/node …)`.\n\nAudited 2026-11-21 by a second agent against the live system; every number here was\nre-measured or dropped. Where a figure is approximate, it says so.\n\n## How the diagrams work\n\nEach diagram exists twice, on purpose:\n\n- **Rendered SVG** in `assets/diagrams/*.svg`, embedded below. This is what `markm`\n  displays — its renderer is `markdown-it` + `highlight.js` + `katex` with **no Mermaid\n  plugin**, so a raw Mermaid block would appear as code. It also runs with `html: false`\n  and escapes raw HTML, which is why this document contains no `\u003cdetails>` or inline\n  `\u003cimg>` tags: they would print as literal text.\n- **Mermaid source** in `assets/diagrams/*.mmd`, linked under each diagram. Edit there and\n  re-render; never hand-edit an SVG.\n\nRelative image paths are safe: markm rewrites a relative image reference into\n`data-local-src`, resolves it against the open document's directory (normalising `..`),\nthen loads it through Neutralino's `filesystem.readBinaryFile`.\n\nGitHub, Obsidian and VS Code render the embedded SVG natively.\n\nRegenerate after editing any `.mmd`:\n\n```bash\ncd assets/diagrams\nfor f in *.mmd; do\n  PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=/usr/bin/google-chrome \\\n    npx -y @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli@11 -i \"$f\" -o \"${f%.mmd}.svg\" -b white -t neutral\ndone\n# mermaid-cli writes no trailing newline; the pre-commit end-of-file hook rejects that.\nfor f in *.svg; do [ -n \"$(tail -c1 \"$f\")\" ] && printf '\\n' >> \"$f\"; done\n```\n\nWhite background with the `neutral` theme on purpose: the SVGs stay readable in light\nand dark viewers alike.\n\n| Diagram | Question it answers |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Layer map | What talks to what, and who owns which failure? |\n| 2. Boot timeline | What happens between \"host starts\" and \"305 tools\"? |\n| 3. Triage tree | Host says *failed* — where do I look first? |\n| 4. Cell supervision | Why did one tool stop working mid-session? |\n| 5. Trust boundaries | Who can write credentials, and what can the sandbox touch? |\n| 6. Incident anatomy | How did the 11-20 outage actually unfold? |\n| 7. Check loop | What runs hourly, and what may it change on its own? |\n| 8. Before/after | Why is it one launcher instead of six inline configs? |\n\n**Naming:** there are **5 client applications** and **6 config files** — Gemini keeps two\n(`~/.gemini/settings.json` and `~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json`). Diagrams that say\n\"configs\" mean six; diagrams that name hosts show five.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Layer map\n\n![Layer map](../assets/diagrams/01-layer-map.svg)\n\n*Source: [01-layer-map.mmd](../assets/diagrams/01-layer-map.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\n- The **host** only knows one command. If it reports failure, the problem is at or below\n  the launcher — never \"that one client's settings\" unless `hela-doctor` says `hosts` is\n  drifting.\n- The **cells** are children of the runtime. If the runtime boots and lists tools, the\n  host is healthy even if one cell later dies (diagram 4).\n- The launcher's own overhead is ~6 ms. A bare `node` start is ~25 ms, so the launcher is\n  not a meaningful cost — it is one shell process plus four tests the runtime would\n  otherwise fail on later with a worse message.\n\n### Why the shape changed\n\n![Before and after](../assets/diagrams/08-before-after.svg)\n\n*Source: [08-before-after.mmd](../assets/diagrams/08-before-after.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\nThe left side is what the machine looked like before this work: five clients each holding\n27–31 env vars inline, two of them already disagreeing on `NEEDLE_TOOL_INDEX_PATH` and\n`HELA_ALLOWED_ROOTS`, and every one of them pinning `/usr/bin/node`. The right side is\ntoday: identical command everywhere, one file to rotate, one place to repair.\n\n## 2. Boot timeline\n\n![Boot timeline](../assets/diagrams/02-boot-timeline.svg)\n\n*Source: [02-boot-timeline.mmd](../assets/diagrams/02-boot-timeline.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\nMeasured three times against the launcher: `initialize` is answered at ~257–269 ms\n(mean ~262 ms), and `tools/list` adds ~3 ms. So **tool registration is the cheapest step**,\nnot the expensive one — nearly all of the time is Node starting and the runtime reading\nits cell catalog. Budget ~300 ms total.\n\n| Exit | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `78` | unreadable secrets, missing entry, or node below the required floor |\n| `127` | no node on PATH, **or** `node -v` unparseable (the message names the binary and the raw output) |\n| silent | runtime booted but exposed 0 tools → `HELA_PROFILE` or manifest |\n\nHost-side startup timeouts are separate: Codex uses `startup_timeout_sec = 60`, and the\ndoctor's own probe allows 90 s by default (120 s in the timer). Nothing in the chain\nassumes 60 s.\n\nWhy the launcher logs to a file as well as stderr: hosts swallow child stderr. After a\nfailure the UI keeps only a one-line status; the log keeps the reason. The timestamp is\nbest-effort — if `date` is unavailable the line still records the reason, with\n`unknown-time` in place of the clock.\n\n## 3. Triage tree\n\n![Triage tree](../assets/diagrams/03-triage.svg)\n\n*Source: [03-triage.mmd](../assets/diagrams/03-triage.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\nStart at the symptom the host actually showed. Note the asymmetry: interpreter and wiring\nfailures are fatal for every host, cell deaths are contained to one tool family, and\nsandbox denials are normal behavior that merely reports badly.\n\n## 4. Cell supervision\n\n![Cell supervision](../assets/diagrams/04-supervision.svg)\n\n*Source: [04-supervision.mmd](../assets/diagrams/04-supervision.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\nRestart delays are **1 s, 2 s, 4 s**. The code caps the delay at 8 s, but with the default\nthree attempts that cap is unreachable — the third failure marks the cell dead before any\ndelay is computed.\n\nMore important, and easy to misread: **the attempt counter is never reset**. A cell that\ncrashes, recovers, crashes, recovers, then crashes again is dead on that third crash, even\nacross hours. It is three strikes per runtime lifetime, not per outage. Only a host\nrestart re-arms.\n\n## 5. Trust boundaries\n\n![Trust boundaries](../assets/diagrams/05-trust-boundaries.svg)\n\n*Source: [05-trust-boundaries.mmd](../assets/diagrams/05-trust-boundaries.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\nTwo separate boundaries, often confused: *who may write credentials* and *what the sandbox\nmay touch*.\n\n`mcp.env` is **sourced as shell**, not parsed. `set -a; . \"$SECRETS\"` means any command\nsubstitution or function definition in that file executes as you at every MCP session\nstart, across all six configs. The boundary that matters is therefore **write** access to\n`~/.config/hela/` — 0700 dir, 0600 file. The launcher warns if the file is group/world\nreadable; `hela-doctor` fails it and `--repair` fixes it.\n\nThe `~/gdrive` exclusion is not incidental — that tree contains a Drive shortcut cycle\n(a shortcut pointing back into its own folder), which makes any local traversal descend\nuntil `PATH_MAX`. Keeping it outside the roots means the filesystem cell cannot be walked\ninto that trap.\n\n## 6. Incident anatomy\n\n![Incident timeline](../assets/diagrams/06-incident.svg)\n\n*Source: [06-incident.mmd](../assets/diagrams/06-incident.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\nThe 50-minute gap between cause and symptom is the reason the launcher now logs and the\ntimer checks hourly. The other lesson: **restarting the app alone does not clear this** —\nthe host caches the failed connect, so the binary has to exist first.\n\n## 7. Check loop\n\n![Doctor loop](../assets/diagrams/07-doctor-loop.svg)\n\n*Source: [07-doctor-loop.mmd](../assets/diagrams/07-doctor-loop.mmd) — edit, then re-render (see top).*\n\nThe doctor has ~22 distinct check names; which ones emit depends on state. A healthy run\ntoday prints 17 lines (15 ok, 2 warn) in ~0.4 s including the live handshake. The two\nsteady-state warnings are housekeeping: credential-bearing backups, and shadowed tools.\n\n| Layer | Check line | Detects | `--repair` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Interpreter | `node`, `node-mark`, `node-floor`, `node-path`, `node-shadow`, `npm` | missing/old node, **auto-marked nodejs**, stale launcher gate, PATH shadowing | node only |\n| Runtime | `manifest`, `runtime-entry`, `runtime-deps` | missing/unreadable manifest, missing `dist/index.js`, missing MCP SDK | no |\n| Cells | `cells` | unbuilt entry, absent `node_modules` | no |\n| Assets | `needle-engine`, `needle-model`, `needle-profile`, `scrcpy-server` | truncated model, non-executable engine, missing adb payload | no |\n| Secrets | `env-file`, `env-dir`, `env-keys`, `env-credentials` | perms, missing keys, **blank/truncated credentials** | perms |\n| Wiring | `hosts`, `host-mode:\u003cname>`, `host-missing`, `host-binaries` | drift off the launcher, disabled client, dead third-party commands | no |\n| Exposure | `host-secrets`, `backups`, `backups-large` | plaintext keys in configs, credential backups, unscannable large backups | chmod 600 |\n| End-to-end | `live` | handshake, tool count, shadowed tools | no |\n\n`--repair` performs four mutations, all idempotent: `apt-get install nodejs` +\n`apt-mark manual`, `chmod 600` on `mcp.env`, `chmod 700` on `~/.config/hela`, and\n`chmod 600` on credential-bearing `*.bak*` (including large ones it cannot scan). It never\nedits a host config.\n\n## The three failure classes seen so far\n\n| Class | Symptom | Detection | Repair |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Interpreter loss/downgrade | `ChildProcess.spawn (/usr/bin/node)` NotFound; or cells failing on `engines.node` | `node`, `node-mark` | `--repair` reinstalls the pinned package and re-marks it manual |\n| Config drift | one client works, another doesn't; keys missing or stale | `hosts`, `env-keys` | point `command` at the launcher; restore keys from `*.bak-secrets-centralized` |\n| Credential sprawl | plaintext keys in host configs or backups | `host-secrets`, `backups` | strip env blocks; chmod 600 or delete backups |\n\n## Commands\n\nThe host layer is implemented once, in `scripts/host-layer.mjs`, reached through\n`doctor.mjs --hosts`. `~/.local/bin/hela-doctor` is a thin wrapper that resolves the\ncheckout, so there is no second implementation to drift.\n\n```bash\nnode scripts/doctor.mjs --hosts          # host layer, full check + live handshake\nnode scripts/doctor.mjs --hosts --json   # machine-readable\nnode scripts/doctor.mjs --hosts --repair # node, credential modes, backup exposure\nhela-doctor --no-live                    # same, via the installed wrapper\n```\n\nThe client entry point is `scripts/host-entry.sh`, installed as\n`~/.local/bin/hela-runtime-mcp`. It resolves the interpreter, gates the version,\nsources the credentials file and logs failures — that is the one command every host\ncalls.\n\nExit codes: `0` healthy (warnings allowed), `1` failures, `2` doctor itself broke\n(`--notify` fires on 2 as well). Logs: `journalctl --user -u hela-doctor.service`;\nlauncher failures append to `~/.local/state/hela/logs/mcp-launcher.log` (rotated at\n512 KB).\n\nNote that a plain run is not read-only: it writes `~/.local/state/hela/node-min-major`\n(the launcher's version gate) and refreshes `node-pin` (the version `--repair` restores).\n\n## Automation\n\n`hela-doctor.service` runs\n`%h/.local/bin/hela-doctor --repair --notify --timeout 120` 2 minutes after login and\nhourly thereafter. It exits 1 on genuine failures; `SuccessExitStatus=0 1` keeps that an\nalert rather than a failed unit.\n\nTwo facts worth knowing:\n\n- `Persistent=true` in the timer is inert — it only applies to `OnCalendar=` timers.\n- The steady state is `0 warn` now that the credential-bearing backups are gone. The\n  remaining warnings, when they appear, are housekeeping (world-readable backups,\n  shadowed tools) rather than failures.\n\n## Operating rules\n\n1. **Never pin an absolute interpreter in host configs.** Use the launcher; it resolves\n   `node` at spawn time and fails with an actionable message. Note the exception: the\n   installer manifest `.hela-install.json` still carries `\"node\": \"/usr/bin/node\"`. No\n   runtime code reads it — only the ecosystem's install/launch scripts and this doctor —\n   so it is a latent trap, not a live one. Retire it when convenient.\n2. **One credentials file, and treat it as code.** `~/.config/hela/mcp.env`, 0600, dir\n   0700. It is sourced, so anyone who can write it can run code as you at every session\n   start. Never paste keys into client configs; the durable copy lives in mema-vault.\n3. **Keep nodejs marked manual.** `hela-doctor` now fails with `node-mark` if the mark\n   drifts back to `auto` — that mark is what let `autoremove` delete the runtime the first\n   time — and `--repair` re-asserts it.\n4. **`HELA_ALLOWED_ROOTS` is the sandbox boundary.** Currently project, Documents,\n   Downloads, persona, skills, memory, HeLa state/install, `/tmp`, `/var/tmp`. Add a root\n   when a legitimate tool is denied; never restore `/`.\n5. **After changing `mcp.env` or the launcher, restart the host.** A running MCP server\n   keeps the env it booted with, and hosts cache a failed connect until restart.\n6. **Do not pass `HELA_SECRETS_FILE` or `HELA_RUNTIME_ENTRY` through a host.** The\n   launcher ignores them by design; if you find a config setting one, that is drift.\n\n## Known limits\n\n- **The live probe proves less than it looks.** It spawns no cell and calls no tool:\n  `tools/list` is answered from the manifest cache because cells are created on first\n  tool call. `[OK] live 305 tools` means node runs, the launcher gates pass, the manifest\n  parses and the SDK answers. No cell is known to be executable.\n- **305 of 591 declared tools are reachable.** With `HELA_NAMESPACE_TOOLS=0`, bare names\n  collide across cells and the runtime keeps whichever cell registered last; the agent\n  catalog resolves the same collisions first-cell-first. The doctor reports the 286\n  shadowed names as a warning. Turning namespacing on exposes all of them but renames\n  every tool, so it is a deliberate choice, not a flip.\n- Policy denials from `filesystem-mcp` surface as `Invalid tools/call result:\n  invalid_union` instead of `path escapes configured workspace roots` — the cell returns\n  a malformed error shape. Access is still denied; only the message is wrong.\n- `node-shadow` and `npm` warnings are informational; `--repair` cannot fix a PATH that\n  resolves a different node, only the installed package.\n- Diagram SVGs carry mermaid's `\u003cforeignObject>` labels, which browsers render but\n  non-browser rasterizers (librsvg, ImageMagick) drop — boxes appear empty. Use Chrome\n  headless to flatten to PNG.\n- Audit tooling caveat: `rtk`-wrapped `cat`/`sed`/`wc` has served a stale revision of this\n  file (410 lines) while `/usr/bin/*` showed the current one. When verifying a document,\n  read it with absolute binaries.\n\n## Rollback and credential recovery\n\nThe credential-bearing backups were removed after every secret they held was copied into\n**mema-vault** (Fernet, master key required):\n\n| Vault entry | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `hela-openrouter-api-key` | current provider key used by the runtime |\n| `hela-github-token` | current `GITHUB_TOKEN` |\n| `hela-google-api-key` | current enzyme search key |\n| `hela-google-cse-id` | current search engine identifier |\n| `hela-*-old-20261118` (×4) | rotated values found in the deleted backups |\n| `chatgpt-codex-resume-token` | conversation-resume JWT from a Codex state backup |\n\nTo rebuild `mcp.env` after a loss:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.config/opencode/skills/mema-vault\npython3 scripts/vault.py env hela-openrouter-api-key --env OPENROUTER_API_KEY --   python3 - \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n# write the four values into ~/.config/hela/mcp.env, then chmod 600\nEOF\n```\n\nThen `chmod 600 ~/.config/hela/mcp.env` and run the host doctor. Non-secret settings\n(`HELA_PROFILE`, paths, cache sizes) are documented above and in the launcher itself.\n\nConfig-shaped rollbacks from before the launcher migration no longer exist on disk; the\nshape they encoded is the one this document describes.\n"},{"source":"docs/security-audit.md","title":"Security status and verification scope","summary":"Blotcat placing an API key into a local vault while untrusted inputs stay outside","category":"operations","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/security-audit.html","text":"# Security status and verification scope\n\n![Blotcat placing an API key into a local vault while untrusted inputs stay outside](../assets/blotcat-security-v2.png)\n\nThis is an operational status document, **not a completed security certification**. Earlier architecture descriptions overstated guarantees about sandboxing, credential isolation, offline operation, and test coverage. A process boundary, a profile, or a passing health endpoint does not establish those guarantees.\n\n## Explicitly deferred work\n\nEndpoint authentication, per-client authorization, exposed-credential rotation, public-exposure review, and resource/session limits remain deferred by the operator's development decision. Deferred does not mean implemented or safe for unrestricted public use. The documentation refresh does not change access controls or rotate credentials.\n\nFilesystem, shell, PTY, browser, model-agent, and device operations can act with the server account's privileges. Scope and deploy them accordingly. Runtime profiles select eligible cells; they are not a substitute for an authorization policy or an OS sandbox.\n\n## What the current checks establish\n\n- Source snapshots pin revisions. Runtime schema catalogs additionally validate versions/build digests and declared feature variants; private caches separate profiles, roots, namespace modes, revisions, builds, and declared configuration.\n- Isolated tests cover runtime timeouts, process exit before restart, bounded retries, first-call startup, concurrent calls, stale-cache refusal, and shutdown. This is lifecycle/reliability evidence, not complete adversarial testing.\n- Doctor checks real isolated MCP initialize/discovery, selected paths/versions/revisions, and optional runtime catalog parity. Hardware, provider, and authenticated GUI checks remain separate.\n- Genome's online backups include committed WAL data and validate integrity; restore refuses existing destinations and sidecars. This does not authorize overwriting an active database.\n- Vault synchronization preserves divergent notes in conflict archives. Archived conflicts still need deliberate review; transport success does not prove reconciliation.\n\nSee [runtime verification](on-demand-runtime.md), [profile verification](profile-verification-2026-11-06.md), and each server's current tool reference for exact scope. Do not promote historical benchmark/test totals into current security claims.\n\n## Credentials and generated output\n\nDoctor and schema-generation probes do not display credential values; isolated probes exclude inherited keys and live vault hooks. Generated client configuration, however, can contain credentials explicitly supplied by its user. Treat configuration files, generator stdout, backups, and issue attachments as private. Never paste a raw client configuration or process environment into a public issue.\n\nPresence of an API key does not prove provider readiness. Keyless research can still make network requests. Needle's local execution depends on its engine/model, and some generative operations need a configured model provider. Optional remote runtime/vault deployment also changes where data is processed and stored.\n\n## Carry-forward validation\n\nPTY orphan handling, SSH failures/transfers, browser network boundaries, archive/symlink policies, Android hardware, Blender execution, live search fallbacks, and external design services still need their own targeted checks. Tests for some of these exist, but full coverage is not implied by ecosystem configuration or discovery checks.\n\nCurrent package versions and generated counts are in [current versions](current-versions.md). Historical release notes and immutable snapshots retain their original versions and evidence; they are not current acceptance guarantees.\n"},{"source":"docs/troubleshooting.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem Troubleshooting & Diagnostic Guide","summary":"This guide provides systematic diagnostic procedures, error code explanations, and recovery workflows for the HeLa MCP Ecosystem.","category":"operations","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/troubleshooting.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem Troubleshooting & Diagnostic Guide\n\nThis guide provides systematic diagnostic procedures, error code explanations, and recovery workflows for the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**.\n\n![Blotcat wearing a hard hat, looking at a smoking server block while consulting a thick manual](../assets/blotcat-troubleshooting.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Fast Diagnostic Workflow: `node setup.mjs doctor`\n\nBefore manual debugging, always run the automated diagnostic health check:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs doctor\n# or\nnpm run doctor\n```\n\nTo run diagnostics against a specific profile:\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs doctor --profile dev-workspace\n```\n\nTo output machine-readable JSON:\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs doctor --json\n```\n\nFor managed installations, select the installed `--mode` and profile, or use an explicit prefix:\n\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs doctor --prefix /path/to/prefix --json\nnode setup.mjs doctor --url http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp --json\n```\n\nThe URL form checks an already-running HTTP endpoint, not its remote filesystem. See [installation modes](installation.md) for Docker, Nix, listener startup, and private client fragments.\n\n### Understanding Diagnostic Output\n\n* **`READY`**: Managed source/build/image identity and isolated MCP initialize/discovery checks passed. Runtime modes also check cold catalog parity and private HTTP discovery.\n* **`DEGRADED` / server `ERROR`**: A required check failed; inspect the JSON report for its server/error. Pending installations, changed builds, missing interpreters/images, and bad MCP replies fail readiness and return nonzero.\n* **Integration scope**: Discovery does not verify Playwright executables, Android/Blender bridges, provider credentials, GUI authentication, or arbitrary workspace access. Legacy unmanaged checkout diagnostics have a different report layout.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Common Issues & Recovery Procedures\n\n### 2.1 Diagnostic Failure: Missing Entrypoint or Build Artifacts\n\n**Symptom**: `[ERROR] Entrypoint file missing` or `dist/index.js not found`.\n\n**Root Cause**: The TypeScript source code has not been compiled into JavaScript build artifacts.\n\n**Fix**:\nFor a managed prefix, rerun the original setup command with the same mode/profile/prefix. Locked dependencies and selected builds are recreated and probed. Preserve dirty working-tree changes rather than resetting them. A pending marker is cleared only after successful verification.\n\nFor manually maintained sibling checkouts:\n```bash\n# Rebuild all 10 servers automatically\nnpm run build:all\n\n# Or build a single server manually\ncd \u003cserver-directory>\nnpm run build\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Server Stdio JSON-RPC Hang or Crash\n\n**Symptom**: `[ERROR] Exited early with code 1` or tool timeouts during execution.\n\n**Root Cause**: Node module dependencies are missing, or environment variables are malformed.\n\n**Fix**:\n```bash\n# Reinstall dependencies for the failing server\ncd \u003cserver-directory>\nnpm ci\nnpm run build\n```\n\nVerify stdio responsiveness manually:\n```bash\nnode \u003centrypoint-file>\n# The process should listen on stdin without throwing an uncaught exception. Press Ctrl+C to exit.\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Client Does Not Recognize Configured MCP Servers\n\n**Symptom**: Cursor, Claude, Gemini, or Zed reports that tools are not available.\n\n**Root Cause**: Configuration file was written to the wrong path, contains relative paths, or the client was not restarted.\n\n**Fix**:\n1. **Regenerate configuration with absolute paths**:\n   ```bash\n   node setup.mjs --reconfigure --prefix /path/to/prefix --client cursor --non-interactive\n   ```\n2. **Review and merge the printed private fragment into the correct client file**. Setup does not write these live files:\n   * **Cursor IDE**: `cat ~/.cursor/mcp.json`\n   * **Claude Desktop**: `cat ~/.claude.json`\n   * **Gemini / Antigravity**: `cat ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/mcp_config.json`\n   * **OpenCode**: `cat ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`\n   * **Kilo CLI**: `cat ~/.config/kilo/config.json`\n   * **Zed Editor**: `cat ~/.config/zed/settings.json`\n   * **Codex / ChatGPT**: `cat ~/.codex/config.toml`\n3. **Check reload behavior**: some clients require a restart; Zed can restart context servers immediately when settings are saved. Back up active memory/vault state before changing sync hooks. Never paste raw client configuration containing credentials into an issue or chat.\n\n### Zed forwards a server that Codex already defines\n\nThe message `MCP server hela-… was not started, because the Codex config already defines an MCP server with this name` refers to the forwarded duplicate. It does not prove that Codex failed to start its native entry. `codex mcp list` inspects configuration, not connectivity; check MCP startup/discovery or an actual safe tool call separately.\n\nZed 1.27.0 forwards project context servers to external agents; native `agent.profiles.*.context_servers` controls are not a per-ACP forwarding switch. Do not remove shared top-level `context_servers` just to silence Codex warnings: native Zed and other agents may need them.\n\nThe installed codex-acp 2.5.0 supports an optional per-agent environment override:\n\n```json\n\"env\": { \"DISABLE_MCP_CONFIG_FILTERING\": \"true\" }\n```\n\nPlace it under `agent_servers.codex-acp` only if deliberately choosing merged forwarded/native ownership. This disables adapter duplicate filtering, **not forwarding**; it can fail when future definitions disagree. Identical definitions passed isolated merge/discovery checks, but authenticated Zed ACP sessions still require verification. Keeping Codex-owned entries and leaving this toggle unset preserves native ownership, with the duplicate warning still possible.\n\nSource references: [Zed forwarding](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/c84af5b99cbc366d675064e52b23664a015b6e35/crates/agent_servers/src/acp.rs#L44-L65), [agent settings schema](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/c84af5b99cbc366d675064e52b23664a015b6e35/crates/settings_content/src/agent.rs#L842-L904), and [adapter toggle](https://github.com/agentclientprotocol/codex-acp/blob/v2.5.0/src/CodexAcpClient.ts#L1620-L1623).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Pinned Snapshot Recovery & Rollback\n\n**Symptom**: Upstream repository changes broke a server build or altered expected tool schemas.\n\n**Root Cause**: Moving `main`/`master` branch contains unreleased or breaking changes.\n\n**Fix**:\nRestore known-good, audited release snapshot:\n```bash\nnode setup.mjs --snapshot v1.5.1 --profile dev-workspace --non-interactive\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2.5 SQLite Database Lock or Corruption (`HeLa Genome`)\n\n**Symptom**: `database is locked` or `SQLite error` in `project-mcp`.\n\n**Possible causes**: Competing writers, a long transaction, permissions, filesystem failures, or a damaged database. A lock error alone does not prove corruption or missing WAL mode.\n\n**Fix**:\n1. Identify the actual database path and owning processes. Inspect integrity read-only:\n   ```bash\n   sqlite3 -readonly /absolute/path/to/memory.db \"PRAGMA integrity_check;\"\n   ```\n2. Take an online, validated backup that includes committed WAL data. Do not copy only the main file while SQLite is active; no daily-backup schedule is implied by the package.\n3. Restore only to a new, absent destination with no SQLite sidecars, verify integrity, and review a controlled switchover. Never overwrite an active database. Preserve divergent Obsidian notes and existing conflict archives instead of choosing a winner automatically.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6 External Hardware Driver Issues\n\n#### HeLa Receptor (Android / ADB)\n* **Verify ADB availability**: `adb version`\n* **Verify device connectivity**: `adb devices` (ensure device is authorized with USB debugging enabled).\n\n#### HeLa Cytosol (Browser Automation)\n* **Install Playwright browsers**:\n  ```bash\n  npx playwright install chromium\n  ```\n\n#### HeLa Plastid (Blender 3D)\n* **Verify Blender CLI in PATH**:\n  ```bash\n  blender --version\n  ```\n* If installed outside standard PATH, create a symlink:\n  ```bash\n  sudo ln -s /path/to/blender /usr/local/bin/blender\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Getting Further Support\n\n* **Run Integration Tests**: `npm test` discovers the installed cells; use `-- --profile \u003cid>` for a selected subset. Tool counts depend on enabled features.\n* **Run Matrix Tests**: `npm run test:matrix` verifies 80 profile/client configuration combinations, not GUI authentication or hardware operations.\n* **File an Issue**: Open an issue at [`Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/issues`](https://github.com/Hela-Ecosystem/hela-mcp-ecosystem/issues) with reviewed/redacted output from `node setup.mjs doctor --json` and the failed check. Do not attach private configs or environments.\n\n---\n\n## Codex ACP: every model fails with `model_not_found`\n\n**Symptom.** A Codex thread in Zed refuses to answer and the ACP log (`dev: open acp logs`) shows:\n\n```\nUnhandled error during turn: {\"type\":\"error\",\"status\":400,\n \"error\":{\"type\":\"invalid_request_error\",\n \"message\":\"The 'gpt-6-luna' model is not supported when using Codex with a ChatGPT account.\",\n \"code\":\"model_not_found\"}}\n```\n\nThe same account works in `codex exec`, which is the clue: the account is fine, the adapter is stale. `codex-acp` bundles its own Codex core, and the build shipped by the Zed registry here (`0.11.1`) carries a model list older than the account, so every model it offers — including the configured one — is rejected. Nothing is wrong with the ChatGPT login, and no credentials need to be copied anywhere.\n\n**Fix.** Replace the registry adapter with a current one and point Zed at it as a custom agent:\n\n```sh\nmkdir -p ~/.local/share/zed/agent_servers/codex-acp-2.7.0\ncd ~/.local/share/zed/agent_servers/codex-acp-2.7.0\nnpm i @agentclientprotocol/codex-acp@2.7.0\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agent_servers\": {\n    \"codex-acp\": {\n      \"type\": \"custom\",\n      \"command\": \"/home/USER/.local/share/zed/agent_servers/codex-acp-2.7.0/node_modules/@agentclientprotocol/codex-acp/dist/index.js\",\n      \"args\": [],\n      \"env\": {},\n      \"default_config_options\": { \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\", \"mode\": \"agent-full-access\" }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nZed applies the settings change without a restart. Keep the model in `default_config_options` aligned with `~/.codex/config.toml`; a model that the account rejects fails the turn the same way regardless of adapter version.\n\n**Verify without the GUI.** From an ecosystem checkout:\n\n```sh\nnode scripts/verify-acp.mjs          # or: npm run test:acp\n```\n\nIt reads the custom entry from `~/.config/zed/settings.json`, drives the same adapter Zed does, and passes only when the agent returns text for a real prompt. Override the binary with `HELA_ACP_AGENT_BINARY` when testing a candidate build.\n"},{"source":"docs/workflows.md","title":"HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Workflows & Integration Guide","summary":"This document defines the standard operational workflows and cross-MCP coordination patterns enabled by the HeLa MCP Ecosystem.","category":"reference","archived":false,"link":"library/docs/workflows.html","text":"# HeLa MCP Ecosystem: Workflows & Integration Guide\n\n![Blotcat Workflows Orchestration](../assets/blotcat-workflows.jpg)\n\nThis document defines the standard operational workflows and cross-MCP coordination patterns enabled by the **HeLa MCP Ecosystem**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Architectural Foundation: Dual-Backbone Coordination\n\nEvery profile in the HeLa MCP Ecosystem is anchored by two coordinating backbone servers:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    Agent[\"Agent / LLM Orchestrator\"]\n\n    Mitosis[\"HeLa Mitosis\u003cbr/>(Orchestrator)\u003cbr/>- Dynamic Routing\u003cbr/>- Step Reasoning\u003cbr/>- Tool Planning\"]\n    Genome[\"HeLa Genome\u003cbr/>(State & Memory)\u003cbr/>- Knowledge Graph\u003cbr/>- Entities / Relations\u003cbr/>- Observations\"]\n\n    Membrane[\"HeLa Membrane\u003cbr/>Workspace FS\"]\n    Nucleus[\"HeLa Nucleus\u003cbr/>Command Exec\"]\n    Ribosome[\"HeLa Ribosome\u003cbr/>PTY Harness\"]\n    Enzyme[\"HeLa Enzyme\u003cbr/>Research / Wiki\"]\n    Cytosol[\"HeLa Cytosol\u003cbr/>Browser + Vision\"]\n\n    Agent --> Mitosis & Genome\n    Mitosis \u003c-->|\"Shared Context\"| Genome\n    Mitosis & Genome --> Membrane & Nucleus & Ribosome & Enzyme & Cytosol\n```\n\n- **HeLa Mitosis (`hela-mitosis`)**: Acts as the cognitive orchestrator. It decomposes complex prompts, analyzes tool performance, and runs Needle-backed `sequentialthinking` step-by-step logic (each thought is an observe → decide → execute agent step recorded in AgentState).\n- **HeLa Genome (`hela-genome`)**: Acts as the immutable project memory. It stores entities, relations, observations, and session milestones in `memory.db`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Canonical Workflows Catalog\n\n### Workflow A: Autonomous Feature Engineering\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `dev-workspace`, `headless-server`\n**Components**: `hela-genome` → `hela-mitosis` → `hela-membrane` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Mitosis as HeLa Mitosis\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n\n    Agent->>Genome: get_session_context / search_nodes(task)\n    Genome-->>Agent: Previous architecture & constraints\n    Agent->>Mitosis: analyze_with_sequential_thinking(feature_req)\n    Mitosis-->>Agent: Step-by-step implementation plan\n    Agent->>Membrane: read_file / write_file(source_code)\n    Membrane-->>Agent: Code written to workspace\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"npm test\" / \"pytest\")\n    Nucleus-->>Agent: Test run output\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[…])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Context Restoration**: Call `get_session_context` on `hela-genome` to retrieve active architectural decisions and dependencies.\n2. **Decomposition**: Call `llm_decompose_task` or `sequentialthinking` on `hela-mitosis` to generate an execution plan.\n3. **Workspace Modification**: Call `read_file` and `write_file` on `hela-membrane` to apply minimal, production-ready code changes.\n4. **Validation**: Call `execute_command` on `hela-nucleus` (or `rtk` wrapper) to run automated unit tests.\n5. **State Recording**: Call `memory_edit` on `hela-genome` to commit progress to the knowledge graph.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow B: Deep Research & Architecture Synthesis\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `research`, `dev-workspace`\n**Components**: `hela-enzyme` → `hela-cytosol` → `hela-mitosis` → `hela-genome` → `hela-membrane`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Enzyme as HeLa Enzyme\n    participant Cytosol as HeLa Cytosol\n    participant Mitosis as HeLa Mitosis\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n\n    Agent->>Enzyme: wikipedia_search / google_search(query)\n    Enzyme-->>Agent: Structured technical summaries & citations\n    Agent->>Cytosol: browser_navigate(spec_url) -> browser_get_content(format=markdown)\n    Cytosol-->>Agent: Live technical documentation content\n    Agent->>Mitosis: llm_summarize(extracted_data)\n    Mitosis-->>Agent: Synthesized architectural comparison\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"create_entities\") + memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\")\n    Agent->>Membrane: write_file(\"docs/architecture/spec-x.md\")\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Knowledge Retrieval**: Query `wikipedia_search` or `google_search` via `hela-enzyme` for background literature and reference APIs.\n2. **Live Web Extraction**: Use `browser_navigate` and `browser_get_content(format=markdown)` via `hela-cytosol` to extract documentation from official docs sites.\n3. **Synthesis**: Run `llm_summarize` and `analyze_with_sequential_thinking` on `hela-mitosis` to synthesize key architectural trade-offs.\n4. **Knowledge Ingestion**: Call `memory_edit` on `hela-genome` linking findings to existing system components.\n5. **Artifact Generation**: Save the final report using `write_file` on `hela-membrane`.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow C: End-to-End Web Feature with Design & Browser Verification\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `web-devops`, `dev-workspace`\n**Components**: `hela-phenotype` → `hela-membrane` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-cytosol` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Phenotype as HeLa Phenotype\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n    participant Cytosol as HeLa Cytosol\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n\n    Agent->>Phenotype: design_tokens(action=\"theme\") / design_tokens(action=\"tailwind\")\n    Phenotype-->>Agent: Design tokens & OKLCH color palette\n    Agent->>Membrane: write_file(\"tailwind.config.js\" & component files)\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"npm run dev\")\n    Nucleus-->>Agent: Local dev server active on port 3000\n    Agent->>Cytosol: browser_navigate(\"http://localhost:3000\") -> browser_screenshot\n    Cytosol-->>Agent: Rendered screenshot, accessibility tree, and Mirage visual analysis\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Design verified on DOM & visual snapshot\"])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Design System Token Generation**: Call `design_tokens`, `palette`, or `logo_workflow` on `hela-phenotype`.\n2. **Component Implementation**: Write JSX/Tailwind files using `write_file` on `hela-membrane`.\n3. **Dev Server Launch**: Launch background local web server using `execute_command` on `hela-nucleus`.\n4. **Visual & Accessibility Verification**: Navigate to `http://localhost:3000` via `browser_navigate` on `hela-cytosol`, verify DOM via `browser_get_content(format=axtree)`, take a visual snapshot with `browser_screenshot`, and use `browser_visual_analyze` when the rendered image needs scene, text, object, or layout interpretation. Use `browser_image_analyze` for a standalone image that does not come from an active browser page.\n5. **Milestone Recording**: Save verification screenshot path and milestone to `hela-genome`.\n\n> **Live Production References Generated by HeLa Phenotype (`the-designer`):**\n> * [Ellis UI Archive / The Collection](https://demo-designer.glassgallery.my.id/) — 24 bespoke anti-slop design systems (*Game Boy 8-bit, Top Secret Dossier, Nutrition Facts, Library Card, Gothic Tome, Swiss Archival*).\n\n---\n\n### Workflow D: Mobile App Test Automation & Verification\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `android-testing`, `all`\n**Components**: `hela-genome` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-receptor` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n    participant Receptor as HeLa Receptor\n\n    Agent->>Genome: get_session_context (device test suite)\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"adb devices\")\n    Nucleus-->>Agent: Connected device ID detected\n    Agent->>Receptor: start_session(deviceId) -> ui_dump\n    Receptor-->>Agent: XML/axtree hierarchy of active mobile screen\n    Agent->>Receptor: tap(element_id) / input_text(\"user_input\")\n    Receptor-->>Agent: UI transition complete\n    Agent->>Receptor: screenshot(\"artifacts/test-pass.png\")\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Mobile flow X verified on physical Android\"])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Device Discovery**: Call `device_list` on `hela-receptor` or `execute_command(\"adb devices\")` on `hela-nucleus`.\n2. **Session Initialization**: Call `start_session` on `hela-receptor`.\n3. **UI Hierarchy Inspection**: Call `ui_dump` or `ui_find_element` to inspect Android view hierarchy token-efficiently via XML rather than screenshots.\n4. **Interaction**: Execute `tap`, `input_text`, `scroll`, or `key_event` to execute test scenario.\n5. **Visual Proof & Persistence**: Call `screenshot` and record test result observation in `hela-genome`.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow E: Autonomous 3D Asset Creation & Render Pipeline\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `3d-modeling`, `all`\n**Components**: `hela-genome` → `hela-mitosis` → `hela-plastid` → `hela-membrane` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n    participant Mitosis as HeLa Mitosis\n    participant Plastid as HeLa Plastid\n    participant Membrane as HeLa Membrane\n\n    Agent->>Genome: search_nodes(\"3D Assets\")\n    Agent->>Mitosis: generate_route_suggestions(\"Procedural chair generation\")\n    Agent->>Plastid: generate_modeling_plan(\"Modern lounge chair\")\n    Plastid-->>Agent: Phased Python Blender script\n    Agent->>Plastid: execute_blender_code(script) -> render_output\n    Plastid-->>Agent: High-resolution render output PNG\n    Agent->>Plastid: save_blend(\"assets/chair.blend\")\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Generated 3D chair asset and render\"])\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Asset Requirement Analysis**: Retrieve scene requirements from `hela-genome`.\n2. **Plan Synthesis**: Call `generate_modeling_plan` on `hela-plastid`.\n3. **Procedural Execution**: Run `execute_blender_code` to generate geometry, apply materials, and setup lights.\n4. **Render Output**: Trigger `render_output` and `get_fast_feedback` for visual QA.\n5. **Persistence**: Save `.blend` file via `save_blend` and register the asset entity in `hela-genome`.\n\n---\n\n### Workflow F: Long-Horizon Complex Refactor with PTY Harness\n\n**Primary Profiles**: `dev-workspace`, `headless-server`\n**Components**: `hela-ribosome` → `hela-nucleus` → `hela-genome`\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Agent\n    participant Ribosome as HeLa Ribosome\n    participant Nucleus as HeLa Nucleus\n    participant Genome as HeLa Genome\n\n    Agent->>Ribosome: session_spawn(\"long-build\", \"mvn clean install -T 4\")\n    Ribosome-->>Agent: Session spawned (ID: sess-101)\n    Agent->>Ribosome: session_read(\"sess-101\") (bounded polling)\n    Ribosome-->>Agent: Streamed stdout/stderr chunk\n    Agent->>Nucleus: execute_command(\"git status\")\n    Agent->>Genome: memory_edit(action=\"add_observations\", observations=[\"Long refactor build verified via PTY session\"])\n    Agent->>Ribosome: session_close(\"sess-101\")\n```\n\n#### Step-by-Step Tool Sequence:\n1. **Interactive Session Creation**: Call `session_spawn` on `hela-ribosome` for interactive, long-running processes (compilers, debuggers, REPLs).\n2. **Session Monitoring**: Read process output with `session_read` or send input with `session_write`.\n3. **Session Termination**: Clean up process with `session_close`.\n4. **Memory Recording**: Log completed refactor state to `hela-genome`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Best Practices & Token Economy\n\n1. **Structured Memory Over Raw History**: Always record key findings and milestones to `hela-genome` so subsequent agent turns do not need to re-read thousands of lines of raw logs.\n2. **Hierarchy Inspection Over Full Screenshots**: For web (`hela-cytosol`) and mobile (`hela-receptor`), prefer accessibility trees and XML dumps for element location, reserving bitmap screenshots for final visual verification.\n3. **Fail-Fast Pre-Validation**: Always verify prerequisite binary availability via `node setup.mjs doctor` before launching complex cross-MCP pipelines.\n"}];
